      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>cDOITGALL'S PATBNT SELF-RAISING</head>
        <p>cDOITGALL'S PATBNT SELF-RAISING</p>
        <p>'mends to accommodate you at the variout places you visit, and especially if you are travelling alone or with other women for companions, always be careful to stay at the best hotels. It would be a good plan to JOIN THE CYCLISTS' TOURING CLUB and thus save yourself any trouble in this matter. This club makes special arrangements with every botel in nearly every part of the country, and you will know by joining this body, for which there is but a nominal fee, where you will break your trip each day, and what you will have to pay. But there is another most important point in tour- ing which must not be forgotten. This is your choice of companions. Select friends who are not entirely governed by self, and who are willing to sacrifice their own wishes 'a bit for the sake of the party. "The more the merrier" is an old saying, but, however,:one which does not hold good if one's companions are selfish and quarrelsome. Remember, too, that you also must give way to the wishes of others, and that you must not expect what 7U do not give. Perhaps worse than selfish and exacting friends are those whose manners are not any too refined. Unfortunately, one is often judged by one's companions. This may be somewhat unjust, but it is true enough to make us a great deal more careful whom we associate with, and in whose company we arrange to spend our long-longed-for holiday. It is advisable for those who anticipate a pleasant tour to be very careful of their diet. pione knows better than the tourist what:a ravenous appetite the exercise encourages, and the inclination to satisfy it sometimes is greater than discretion should allow. Never, under any circumstances, should a heavy meal be taken! immediately before or after a long ride. Indeed, even after a light meal a full half- hour should elapse before the machine is again taken into use. Indigestion is the great bugbear of the cyclist, and no tour, however wonderfully planned, will be enjoyable to you with its unsolicited com- pany. If you feel the g,nawings of hunger at any time when no dinner is at hand, avail yourseliof a few bars of chocolate, which will chase away the uncomfortable feelings, at least for a time. A last bit of advice,which,perhaps, you will not be above taking, is to always clean your bicycle up over- night in preparation for the following day. The task may be an unwelcome one to you, especially if you are tired and longing for a good rest, but it is one which repays you afterwards. Travel stain&amp; are far easier to dispose of when fresh,for one thing, and for another, it puts one in a good humour to get up on a fine morning to find a spotless steed all ready and waiting for an early spin.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>(Copyright.)</head>
        <p>(Copyright.)</p>
        <p>CYCLING AND CYCLISTS.</p>
        <p>No. IV.—CYCLING FOR LADIES</p>
        <p>BY MISS AMY JACOBS • (LADY MARJORIE OF "THE CYCLE").</p>
        <p>k cycling tour is the great end in view of all who take to the wheel. It is the greatest ambition of the beginner, and the thought of it invariably spurs her on to overcome all the small difficulties which spring in her path during the throes of learn- ing. However, the eagerness with which the first tour is looked forward to frequently overcomes the discretion which the novice should display in many matters, concerning her own fitness for the under- taking, and the condition of her machine. We can</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>Lu, :GS SUMMER TREATMENT Now is the time during the warm months of Summer to fight against Consumption and build up the health and strength by taking</head>
        <p>Lu, :GS SUMMER TREATMENT Now is the time during the warm months of Summer to fight against Consumption and build up the health and strength by taking</p>
        <p>ANGIER'S Petpoieurn FAULSION</p>
        <p>It is pleasant to take even in the hottest weather, and not only promotes appetite, aids digestion, a.nd increases weight and strength, but it also acts through the blood directly upon the lungs, and exerts a wonderfully soothing, healing and strengtn- ening influence. Of chemists, es. od. and 4s. tid. Be careful to ask for Angier's Emulsion and accept no substitute. It is made with our specially purified oil, which no other chemist can obtain. Imitations made NCrith ordinary petroleum do not have the same effect, and might even prove harmful. A sample sent free on receipt of 3d. for postage. The Angi‘.r Chemical Co., Lta., 32 Snow Hill. London, 8.C..</p>
        <p>People who boy new music and like it good, are apt to find the luxury an expensive one. For, speaking generally, good nausio is dear. There are exceptions, however. When the big advertiser comes into the field, he has a way of upsetting a good many old notions. Here, for example,is Mr. Thomas Holloway, of pills and ointment fame,publishing new songs by prominent com- posers. We have just received from Mr. Holloway two really flue songs by Lindsay Lennox ; one of these, "Love's Message," is a charming song, while the other, " The Beautiful Prayer," is a truly beautiful work, full of melody. There are some songs which seem to haunt the mind of anyone who has once heard them, and '• The Beautiful Prayer " is one of these, and is by ethe same author as " Love's Golden Dream." The published price of these two new songs—which are full music size—is 2s. each. But this is where the big advertiser comes in. If you write at once to Mr. Thomaa Fioll3way, 78, New Oxford-street, London, and inclose 21d. in stanins, a copy ot either song will be sent to you free. The offer of course is a special one, and in applying for the song you must mention that you saw the announcement in this piper.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>MINERS' PERMANENT RELIEF SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>MINERS' PERMANENT RELIEF SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>THE WORKMEN'S COMPENSATION ACT.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon, a meeting of the Lancashire Rod Cheshire Miners' Permanent Relief Society took place in the Mining School, Wigan, Mr. Alfred Hewlett, peed— dent of the society, in the chair. The Chairman, in opening the proceedings, stated that they were assembled in compliance with an undertaking , given at the annual general meeting, when it was promised that the Beard would carefully consider the position in which the society would be placed by the operation of the Workmen's Compensation Act. The Secretary (Mr. G. L. Campbell, J.P.) then pro- ceeded to read the report, in which the Board recom- mended that the employers be requested to assist the scciety by continuing to collect the contributions as at present, and by aiding in the verification of accidents, employers so assistieg to be honorary members ; that, as far as practicable, the widows and children and hurt men now in receipt of payments be made a first charge upon the accumulated benefit fund ; that the rule whereby the ordinary membc rs were called upon for in- creased subscriptions at collieries where no pro- prietors' percentages were paid be rescinded ; that the contributions of the ordinary members should remain as at present ; and that from and after the let July tke benefits be as follows (a) Surgeons to be provided. (b) Disablement allow- ances : Ten shillings per week for the first fortnight and 7s. per week as long as the member is unable to work I from the effect of the accident, with proportionate al- lowances for half-members. (c) Fatal accidents : Funeral allowances of £5, with additional grant to unmarried members of £lO, to half members £2 10s. widows per week 45., children per week 24. The Chairman moved that the report of the Board be received and placed upon the minutes.—TLus was reconded by Mr. Lunford, of [Dudley. Mr. Hewlett raid the recommendation of the report went to tte length of saying that as far as practicable the accumulated moneys should be devoted to the widows, children and disabled. in order to secure this they would have to ask the chief registrar of friendly societies to make an inquiry into the position and condi- tion of the society, and to formulate a scheme to allo- cate to those people concernel the moneys which had ' been accumulated. The chief registrar • might deal with the question better by making a con. tinuing scheme or declaring that the society should be , dissolved. The employers felt that the responsibilitiesl which would be placed upon them at the earl of th month would be heavy enongh, and they could not and did not intend after that date to pay the 35, 15, or 10 per cent, to the society, as the case might be. The em- ployers were quite agreed to allow the money to be col- lected as it was at present until such time as the tegis- tear should :flake his inquiry. After the registrar had made his report, it would not be for the employers to flat, what course should be pursued in thefuture as they would not be eeintributing to the funds. In order to put the society in a right position it was necessary to make certain alterations in the rules, and these were submitted by the Board of Management in their report. The proposals erred on the Liberal side. and he would nct quarrel with them. A resolution was then submitted to the meeting authorising the board of management to obtain an in- quiry into the position of the society by the chief trar. '•</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks was passed to the chairman. and the proceedings terminated.</p>
        <p>• EMALE AILMENTS</p>
