      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>ALL UNDERSTAND THE LONGING</head>
        <p>ALL UNDERSTAND THE LONGING</p>
        <p>which comes with the first few days of spring sun- shine to leave the hurry and bustle of the busy city, and to revel in the quiet joys of the country- side. The summer flies by all too quickly for the cyclist, who grudges every available day spent away from the now uuiversally beloved pastime. Still, under some conditions, a cycling tour is apt to dispel all the glamour woven round it by the inex- perienced. Successful touring is most certainly an art; it is not everyone who understands it, and there are few indeed who can honestly say they can enjoy it to the fullest extent. And now, shall we give away the secret of successful touring? In the first place, the rider's own condition must be taken into account. If you have, for weeks past, only just pottered round the parks and squares near your home, to start on a tour—if only a short one— would be madness. You want to train yourself up for</p>
        <p>to begin with a short journey- at first and gradually increase the distance, until by the end of a few weeks you are quite assured of your own powers of endurance, and can, therefore, start your new undertaking with at least one good point in your favour. Next look to your machine. Is it sound</p>
        <p>and in good condition enough to carry you over your holiday without any breakdowns or mishaps?</p>
        <p>- _ . If it is not, have it seen to at once. As you are a</p>
        <p>beginner, you will not undetstand the points of your machine yourself, and you had better put it into the hands of an experienced man, or your indepen- dence will do</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>FAR MORE HARM THAN GOOD.</head>
        <p>FAR MORE HARM THAN GOOD.</p>
        <p>H your machine is sound you can see to its adjust- ments yourself. Do not allow any of your friends to see to this for you. No one knows better than yourself what suits you personally, and you may have to suffer serious inconveniences on the road through delay in being obliged to readjust your machine for the sake of your own comfort. See, then, that everything is in order before you start, screw every nut safely home, adjust your saddle to your liking, and well pump up your tyres. Con- vince yourself, too, that your brake is in good working order, and that your lamp is in perfect condition for lighting. And now look to the con- tents of your wallet. Accidents are always liable to occur. See that you are prepared for them by carrying with you a complete set of tools necessary for executing minor repairs. Should you not under- stand the art of puncture mending, don't experi- ment at the risk of spoiling your holiday. However, you will find many friends of the roadside, no doubt, who do, and your repair outfit may be found useful at any time. But here a little precaution may be necessary. Do not implicitly put your faith in trangers ; there are many ill-conditioned men goin I, about in the garb of gentlemen, and unless you are quite sure that honest and kindly meant assistance is proffered you, do not hesitate about refusing it. Prefer rather to walk your machine to the • nearest village, and so waste part of your valuable time, than to risk placing yourself IN ANY AWKWARD PREDICAMENT.</p>
        <p>Even if your knight of the roadway is genuine, see that he understands something about the repairs be offers to make, or you may be left in a worse state than ever. Man's natural chivalry where -women are concerned often prompts him to go to the rescue when his proffered aid is simply of no avail at all, and it will often be found so in the case of cycling. Upon the question of dress there are many im- portant matters to be considered. For a costume choose a strong wearing material, which will not shew the dust, and have it made sensibly and plainly. On the other hand, don't go to extremes and make yourself look a fright—there is no occasion for this, although many folks seem to think so. You can have it made becomingly even if it is plain, and your hat can be as natty as you please, provided it is not too large and is not trimmed with any de- structible flowers and feathers. The difficulty with cycling hats, however small, is the tendency to blow off orto perch inartistically upon one side. The use of hat-pins is not advisable; they are dangerous; and the resistance they offer against the wind stops the natural flow of blood to the roots of the hair, thereby reducing the nutriment they should receive. Elastic is too unbecoming to be thought of, but an excellent plan is to stitch two small combs into the crown of the hat, so that when the latter is out on the teeth can be pusfied into the hair and thus keep the hat secure. In the matter of under- garments, leave everything that is linen at home and WEAE ALL TWAT IS WOOLLEN.</p>
        <p>Besides being safer, healthier, and more comfort- able, you will find the latter keep much cleaner and fresher than the former. Wear suspenders in •preference to garters, and never commit the folly of wearing even one innocent petticoat; knickers 'beneath a skirt are the only suitable garments for cycling. Very few women care for the ordinary ,cycling shoe, and very few; sad to relate, wear them. They are supposed to be ugly, clumsy, and . awkward, but those who have once found the comfort of the correct cycling shoe soon alter their opinions on this seemingly small matter. In any case, shoes worn for cycling should possess sensible •toes, if not exactly square, at least having plenty of room for the foot without any cramping. Dress- 'holders are very generally worn by touring wheel- •women, since their use prevents the skirt from , ticking up over the knees in the uncomf oda ble way that every woman knows. There are any amount Of patent dress-holders to be obtained, ,but these sort of things can be easily made. Purchase a piece of garter elastic, and at one end stitch a loop to be slipped over the foot, at the other end fasten a small safety pin, which can be attached to the skirt at any distance from the hem the wearer chooses. The articles of the toilet you will want to take with 'you depend somewhat on the length of the tour. As .a role, little else besides a few handkerchiefs, a toothbrush, comb and brush, and a robe de suit, would be required for a short journey. If a longer one, other necessities can be sent on by rail, so that arrangements can be made for a change of dress at several stopping places along the route. And now we will leave the all-important question of dress and divert to other matters equally ilia- portant.</p>
        <p>11IE SUCCESS OF A. Toms depends a great deal upon the route selected. Hilly ,country may be .very picturesque, but one forgets the beauties of a scene when wearied out by exces- ftive hill-climbing. For the first tour, .at least, a liat colliery should be chosen. if ter mapping out your tour, arrange the distances for each day, and the various places you will put up s.t for the night. arranged tour is suPpased to lose half many cyclists, who are of opinion A previously- its fascination by that uncertainty adds to tue pleasure, but you only want one roue experience to convince you to the ' "coats ary. practically' a novice at As to distance: if you are touring, forty miles would ,he a fair daily jaunt to be kept up on consecutive days. This may seem very slow work, but for perfect enjoyment, to enable the rider to take in all the beauties of the scene around, and to obtain the true benefit of QS exercise, there is nothing like a moderate pace. Don't overdo it at first; your freshness may deceive you, and when once clone up half the pleasure seems to have suddenly g( ne• It is far wiser to allot a certain distance for each day and to stand by it. Remember also that it is a foolish plan to economise in the matter of choosing your destina- tion at the finish of each day's ride. If you have UO</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>i'LOU It PREVENTS INDIGESTION. .</head>
        <p>i'LOU It PREVENTS INDIGESTION. .</p>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER Mr DiArt Nistram, Plain straw sailor bats never look so well as when trimmed simply with the masculine band and bow, but some of the " modified " sailors—so- called—that are of more fanciful straws, and have brims slightly turned up at the edge, may be decorated rather more extensively. A pretty way of trimming such a hat is with a rather narrow band of velvet round the base of the crown and a twist of checked glace silk above, the latter being formed into a big, full bow at the side with or without the addition of a couple of wings. Whether the edge of the brim is bound or not depends on the kind of straw.</p>
        <p>Ruches, on hats especially of tulle or chiffon, re- quire some dexterity of manipulation; still after a little practice their manufacture become easy enough. A satisfactory way of trimming a hat of, say, pale grey satin straw, is with a ruche of silver spotted net, very full and wide at the back, so that it quite conceals the crown where the brim is turned up against it, but narrowing down almost to nothing in front, where a pair of outspread white (or black) wings should be placed. A wreath of pink or deep red flowers under the brim at the back would put the finishing touch to a really •pretty little hat. Duck and drill, two washing fabrics, formerly patronised solely by the sterner sex, have of late years been popular for ladies' and children's cos- tumes, and have almost completely ousted piqué, which required more care in the laundry. Unlined blouses of drill, in white, ivory. and the pale Yellows, pinks, and blues so lately introduced, are admirable for boating and tennis, especially if accompanied by skirts of serge or alpaca Where the complete costume is not affected.</p>
        <p>• The serge blouse is always useful, and can be made as smart as desired. Trimmings of braid in bayadere fashion, are easily arranged, as the bands are sewn on after the blouse is joined, and it is quite easy to keep the circular line. The shaped basque is put on separately, and the blouse can be fastened down the centre. If made without lining, the white habit front should be made separately, and fastened at the back, the front left longer than the opening, and pinned down over the corset. Blouses suitable either for ladies or children can be made in linen or drill. They should be made entirely without lining, and the centre pleat put on separately. The sleeve are almost straight, and pleated into narrow wristbands, and the blouse is also pleated into a two-inch band at the waist, over which a belt can be worn. The collar is edged with a pleated frill, and outlined with braid, and to avoid a too low neck, this collar is mounted on a narrow band, which keeps it in position. For small girls who wear this blouse with a serge skirt, flat buttons should be sewn on the band of the skirt, and buttonholes placed in the band of the blouse.</p>
        <p>Now that the empire frocks are relegated solely to the very tiny children, the fashions for elder girls follow the more simple styles of the adult, leavirg, however, those designs where elaborate trimmings and tucks and gaugings innumerable de- mand the hand of the artist in gowns. Yoke and pianafore styles still reign supreme, and there are few prettier mothods of introducing relief colour or material in a legitimate fashion. The pianofore bodice can also be finished in the same adaptable style leaving the top lightly tacked, BO that frills or yoke can easily be replaced, and these arrange- ments keep the frock smart and fresh to the last. Bright, showy colours are a mistake for young girls, and soon become shabby, while good black or blue serge retains its freshness, and can be re- novated or varied as desired. The open-fronted coat worn with fronts or blouses is admirably suited to the tall girl of four- teen to sixteen, but the sleeves must be small and close, the bascue tightly fitted, and the fronts kept straight and firm with tailor's canvas. A plain coat, without collars or revers, is most useful where variation is desired, especially it a bias band of silk, with one loose edge, is laid round the neck inside the coat, so that the edge of collar or revers may simply be laid inside, and the silk laid over it —the work really of a few moments, and yet leas. jag a finished effect. Your loving Jrame.'</p>
