      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JUNE 17, 1898 (UoPYright.) CHILDREN OF CUBA: A-TALE OF LOVE AND WAR</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JUNE 17, 1898 (UoPYright.) CHILDREN OF CUBA: A-TALE OF LOVE AND WAR</p>
        <p>" WOMEN'S CHAT." Among the many accomplishments of the Queen, one of no small importance, yet one of the least known, is, or rather was, that of mountaineering. In her younger days the Sovereign was an enthusiastic mountaineer, and during the annual visits of the Coutt to Balmoral, Her Majesty delighted in ascending the highest and most noted mountains of Scotland. —Mount Keen—the most easterly mountain in the United Kingdom and over 3,000 feet in height, has been crossed three times by the Queen, the third occasion being in September, 1867. This was the last of Her Majesty's mountain excursions. During the sojourn of the Court at Balmoral, nothing could have been quieter than the daily routine there ; but at Windsor several festivities are to take place, among them a performance, in the Waterloo Chamber, of the opera "Romeo et Juliette," the principal members of the cast being Madame Emma Eames,M. Edouard and Jean de Reezke, and M. Plancon. Little Prince Edward of York seemed to thoroughly enjoy the inspection of the Crescent, and has it appears, already stated his determination to become a sailor, like his father. His small Royal Highness is now quite a finished little gentleman, and takes off his hat when occasion demands, in the prettiest way possible. It is true his efforts to appear sedate and dignified are not always crowned with Racemes, but he does his best, and is a tremendous favonrite wherever be goes. In September next, Mr. Sims Reeves will at, an the age of seventy-eight. That this should be so seems al- most an impossibility, for it is only a few months back that he was singing with artistic expression and perfect taste,though undoubtedly failing voice, at one of the St. James's Hall ballad concerts. Still the fact remains,and as he is now in delicate health, it is proposed to do for him what was done for Signor Mario, arrange a benefit concert, with liberty to the subscribers te pay for their tickets as much as they please beyond the purceaae price. Mr. Sims Reeves was far in the twenties before his voice developed his power, and, it seems, did not al- ways get along smoothly with his teacher, an able, but somewhat brusque-mannered individual. Cnce when he was going through an exercise, his teacher, it is said. abruptly interrupted : " Stop,you are flat, very flat." And you sir, are sharp, very sharp," retorted the— afterwards—great tenor,as he picked up his music, and dashed from the room. Good music has been quite a feature of the present London season. Hostesses have introduced it upon all possible occasiors,and frequently concerts have preceded it,or taken the place of,small dances. One of the most successful concerts,so far,was that given by the Duchess of Wellington at Apslee liouse,which ie,in itself, an in- teresting sight, with all its fine statuary, and priceless old pictures. Apsley House is occasionally thrown open on Sundays to a limited number of visitors, who are ad• mitted by ticket,tut the Duke demands that each guest shall wear slippers. Forty pairs are kept for this specia purpose. Speaking of the Duke of Wellirgton rem;nds me of his ancestor, the second Duke, who was r oted for his wit. When the Wellington Statue was taken down from its position opposite Apsley House, with a view to re- casting it, the Duke received a letter from an old friend who wrote in a querilous manner concerning the ills of life that gather with advancing years. The Duke, re- plied, condemning his correspondent's melancholy. " Look at me," he wrote, " take a treason from me." " never complain, and yet lam old," lam in Willie health, I have no son I " have but one eye—and now they are going to melt down my father." Black roses are an accomplished fact, M. Feticoff, an amateur grower, of Voronezh, in Russia, having auc ceecled in producing such blooms. That they are not slickaerlcytyo a facebecom e very plentiful at present though is el for decorative purposes, pouf rwp ohsaets . possibleitduseffic u a black l a to rose could imagine. Like the black tulip, it is of courses curiosity, but when we have said this, we have surely said all that is in favour of the latest " triemph " in horticultural sr'. Methods by which colouring is brought about indi- rectlyehrotigh artificial means, have long been known to growers of flowers, but the processes by which it is possible to deprive blooms of perchance a bad odour,and impart to them a pleasant one, are of more recent dis- covery. In the case of the African marieold, the seeds were first allowed to soak for two days in rosewater,con- taining a little musk. They were I hen nartially dried and eown. The flowers that grew in time were not en- tirely deprived of their bad odour,but.mingled with the original smell,the agreeable odours of tbe rose and mush could be detected. The seeds of these plants were twain sown, after similar treatment, and it was reused that</p>
        <p>there was a still further improvement. Ribbon manufacturers must surely be Masi ing huge fortunes, for the yards of rill bon tnat have been pressed into service this summer, mutt already have rdre into many millions- Ribbon embriddery still remains the trimming of the moment, and appears in various guises upon all characteristic novelties. I have just sown for instat ce,a very pretty cravat of rose pink glace silk,the full bows of safeh were each trimmed with three rows of narrow mink ad ribbon, in a deep shade of violet, pet some twn inches apart from each other. The ends, which were fairly long and wide, were most beautifully embroidered with sprays of dark violets and green foli- age, carried out entirely in ribbon embroidery, both flowers and leaves consisting of very narrow ribbons, cleverly interlaced and looking wonderfully pretty in the rose-pink silk ground. So far, there has been no perceptible diminution this season in the gleaming and glittering embroideries with which we have been wont to decorate our gowns, mantles, and headgear for so many months past. Floral designs worked in diamonds, em, raids, amethysts, or pearls, and dPt round with foie traceries of silver,appear as bofore on skirts, bodices, and cloaks, while lace of the rarest description, both ancient and modern, is mingled judiciously with the jewellod embroideries, and in many cases, also sewn with tiny jewels. To imitate that style of dress, patronised by the " mere man" would seem to have been the one aim and object in life, of a considerable number of our sex for some years past. The one fashion though we should have done so well to copy, we have let alone, viz : that of innumerable pockets. We have always been poor pocketlees creatures, but at the present time we are worse off than ever. Up to this summer we were allowed some sort of apology for the pocket, buried somewhere I in the region of the plat:pet hole,where it was torture to endeavour to extract or replace,a handkerchief or purse, but with the new skirts which fit so closely round the hips, and possrss the merest scrap of fulness at the back, pockets went out and now no really good dressmaker dreams of allowing us one. Parisians are carrying little embroidered satin bags in which they put their hand- kerchief, purse, or anything else they fancy, but no bag be it ever so fascinating, can make up for the loss of a pocket, however meagre and inconvenient. The difficulty experienced by housekeePera in dealing with fi enlaces, bas been considerable this year. To do away with fires altogether has been an inspossibilitVi and how to make a grate look pretty, and at the same time be In a position to get a fire just by applying the necessary match, is a problem many have found diffi- culty in solving. An excellent idea is to pile dried fir- cones on the coals. The effect is pretty, and they can either be saved from the burning, or allowed to scent the room with the delicious fragrance they give out in com- bustion, If, however, the grate is ugly beyond resit:imps tints little hand-painted screens, mounted in bamboo, which can be had for something under three shillings, are perhaps the best. They can be removed in a second, should a fire be desired, and yet stay to ornament some corner of the room. rfere is the recipe for a delicious sweet :----9oak a tea cupful of tapioca in cold water for two hours, then pour over it a pint of boiling water, and cook twenty minutes. Pare and cook two pounce of cooking apples, cover the bottom ot a pie dish with them, sprinkle over two table- Spoonfuls of sugar, and flavor with the juice of one lemon Pour the tapioca over the apples, and bake in a slow oven one hour.</p>
