        <p>BY GRAHAM REDPATH Author of "The Baffled Press Gang,* "When Things Went Wry," Ste.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER IX. A SILTER</p>
        <p>- - Vice and virtue struggle for victory in every city, and they did so in Havana. Here, as elsewhere, the struggling poor lived in the decayed quarters, away in a waste square, down by the wharves. The houses were rickety and old, apparently dirty and damp. In an out-of-the-way corner of this depressing quarter stood a house built of stone, ,gravel and cement combined, of rude and curious shape, which sufficed to redeem it from obscurity. The stone pillars were slightly askew, the top-stone aslant, the door, low, but broad, the front windows clean and tidy, the yellow walla greenish-grey in places. The outlook was so depressing that none but victims of adversity would dream of living in it, That was the position of the doctor, and circum- stances made this his home. Here his two daughters worked bravely at embroidery, crocheting and sewing. A neighbourly shopkeeper assisted in the sale of the work, and her advice helped •the Cardonnas to economise, though it was always difficult to make ends meet. It pleased or displeased the doctor, according to his mood, to see his daughters work so hard, for to him they lost little of their loveliness in adversity. As time wore on, the efficiency and economy with which his household was managed brought sweeter and softer thoughts to him, and yet, at times, the subdued spirit of his tender-hearted family, instead of being a means of quiet strength, lacerated his feelings and made him think savagely of the man who, in the guise of friendship, had stolen into and despoiled his household of its former comfort and happiness. Proud and persevering as the doctor was, he could not subdue hysterical outbursts of passion whenever he thought of Vargas, who lived in the largest mansion in the Calle de O'Reily, the aristocratic quarter of the city from whence each Sunday he drove 'to the Cathedral in an elegant equipage. Sometimes Vargas feared lest the eyes of the honest doctor were watol4ng hip' as he knelt in prayer. But conscience' whispered: "You are Honest' Vargas ; the surgeon is only a crank.',' And yet at times Vargas felt in the secrecy of his own soul that he had allowed himself to be swayed by a motive as paltry as the sum he bad stolen. His remorse, however, came too late; he would at times willingly have undone the great mischief he had caused, for his wealth was ever accuriiii- lating—and to what purpose? He had no relations whom he cared a straw about, and no one whom he wished to benefit; but he dared not humble himself in the eyes of his former or present friends. He feared what the world would say. . Yet in, his more meditative moods he thought he might make some reparation through Castilia—if he only had the courage to do the dark thing he had dreamt he had done—and leave his property to her. Frequently he battled with the idea, but he could not banish it.</p>
        <p>--"`Iie;;;,- dear, there's a letter for you," said Castilla one day to her father. "From whom, I wonder," he said carelessly, as he glanced at the address and tore open the envelope. It was very like Leon's handwriting, thought Castilla, as she began to dust the room, to provide an excuse for remaining. But Leon would not write to her father; why should he? "Read this," said the doctor, handing over the letter to his daughter:</p>
        <p>"Toledo, Spain;</p>
        <p>"My Dear Doctor, —I am deeply grieved io know your misfortune, and the more so when I reflect that it is caused through the confidence and trust you placed in a man professing to be your dearest friend. Feeling sure that you will wish to prosecute your rightful claim, I send an enclosure to assist you, and trust you will succeed in the effort. Of course, you will use this in whichever way you deem fit. lam pained beyond measure to think of the circumstances which have made it possible for me to be of service to you. I hope you and your family, however, will soon find the sun of prosperity once more shining upon you. , "Your affectionate friend, ."Lisox ALcANTARA." With a little cry Castilia fell; she had fainted. When she looked up, she saw her father counting the notes which Leon hadenclosed. "They are from him; he is generous; but," she added in still softer accents, "surely he dared not have sent them if he had been as false as they say."</p>
        <p>. She sank down again, quite overcome. The doctor was confused. Between his daughter's sudden prostration and Leon's goodness he was puzzled. There was something behind it all, and some explanation would be forthcoming, he felt certain. "If he had been as false as they say." The more he pondered upon it, the more the phrase bewildered him. He knew Castilia had received a letter from Leon subsequent to that from Castello, and iri reply to her own. Up to now he had pre- sumed that Leon's explanation had been unsatis- factory. But he could scarce conceive how this could be after the doubt expressed by Castilla. _ _ _ . . .</p>
        <p>- "Be candid, and confidezin me," said the doctor tenderly to his daughter when she revived. "I feel sure you fret about him, and I'm confident there's some misunderstanding at the bottom of it all." As plainly as she could, after • a little awkward hesitation, she told the story, between many a sob. "Castello has some hidden motive in all this, said Dr. Cardonna.</p>
        <p>"Castello scarcely could!" "Castello may be a Vargas of different degree. We must prevent another sharing ".a fate like ours." "But he had no motive."</p>
        <p>"So you think; that's what .1 thought of Vargas. But had I known he had asked you to marry him, I should have been more cautious." _ _</p>
        <p>"hen are 3r,;;u. going to accept Leon's help'!" asked Castilia. _ _</p>
        <p>"I think there can be no harm in that."' "I don't know, but in our present position,:see- ing that he and I are not--' 'There, now, before you finish what you were 'going to say," interrupted the doctor, 'suppose you read his letter again." Castilla blushed deeply, and tears filled her eyes. "What do you say now ? " "I think you are right, father." "That's splendid! Both of you are better than you think, and, bless you, I believe you'll understand yourselves again better than ever very soon."</p>
        <p>"But how, father ? " anxiously asked Castilia. "How can that be ? " _</p>
        <p>"Just wait, dear, and you'll see. I don't prophesy often, but this time I don't mind; I think I know."</p>
        <p>The doctor, in accepting the money as a loan, till he could repay it, asked Leon frankly if there was a word of truth in Castello's allegations. Leon replied kindly and briefly. He enclosed a letter in Castello's handwriting, acknowledging the untruthfulness of the slander and how it had come about: that he himself had got into the scrape for which Leon had been blamed, and that he had given Leon's name instead of his own. This—this apology—was how the cowardly and malicious Castello had avoided being compelled to go out and meet sword to sword the man he had maligned This good news overjoyed Castilia and her parents. Happiness was still her portion, and it had come through a trouble which might not prove such a great misfortune after all. There were the opportunities here of a reunion of hearts, and the most was made of them, without much loss of time or much ado.</p>
        <p>Poor Coria would scarcely have been a woman had she not felt this second blow keenly. She tried to crush out all memory of the dreams of her early womanhood. To some extent she felt that her happiness had been a sacrifice to propitiate the general well-being of her family. Certainly brighter days dawned for the Car- donnas; the head of the household was in practice for himself ; they were in a home more worthy of their past renown; and the date of Castilia's wedding was fixed. And Coria, too, was being wooed by another, the handsome fellow-passenger on the San Justo. Between the narrator of thrilling stories of piratical adventure and the Huesca Ferrol now before her, Coria observed considerable difference. Those hazel eyes, half-hidden by long eyelashes, shone and sparkled as she looked into them. It WWI not the first time she had admired them. Huesca had grown slightly taller and broader. His face was smooth and round— a picture of good health and good humour. There was none of that brooding, !half-melancholy look left, and he was nee</p>
