        <p>unwelcome. , unnii dfi</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>, . guest at tne cardonnasy, who were . ....indful of his kindness on the high seas Huesca was now owner of two small schooners, , illng ea master in one ,of them; and his convet- Aim. was full of incident and anecdote of the f Atli Seas, and thq4rrible tragedies—half-love, ' if-bate—in the -lives of the settlers and ha - ---.Ei. He was cheerful, chatty and thoughtf , bringing a bright ray of sunshine into a life f I gloom; and the friendship ripened till it beca e very sincere and cordial. Then he boldly askd Cone an i for her hand and heart, and she aecepted h 1 4s. i All were happy save the,doetor.:: He,was troubl d and anxious, for he could not help musing. lA. 'stfong feeling of hatred towards the destroyer of his happiness, the motive of whose revenge he knew, possessed him. ,At times he, strove against the gradually-increasing influence, lest it should trick him into othe deed that,should disgrace 31is family when their fortunes and happiness were in a measure restored and secured, but the cankd at his heart grew till he began to tremble at its power. Castilia was looking forward to Leon's arrival and their coming marriage. The time was feet approaching. Coria was busy preparing, and took a deep interest in the innumerable things there were.to do. . The night preceding Leon's arrival Coria had a frightful, haunting dream. It was so vivid and .realistic that it ref used. to be driven away with the reassurance daylight usually brings. , Vargas, the rich old banker, with grey loch ... bland smile, was proceeding along the cathed!.• aisle with' leisurely tread and pompous, ..bearing. Behind came the doctor with bloodshot- ey'es' and scowling brow, as she saw him on the day of his return to the hotel, pale, haggard and weary. She rose from her seat with a great effort, to intercept him, but swooned instead. On recoveringf her *vision she saw both men on the chancel floor, quite dead, and the people fleeing from the place in fear. The priest, pointing downward, said bitterly : "Let them lie and rot here! I forbid them Christ,ian burial! They have defiled this sacred place; it is God's House ,no longer." It was frilly a terrible dream. Leon' e arrival that day was opportune. It helped to drive the frenzied thoughts of an over-excited brain away, but Coria 'felt uneasy amidst all the happiness the day had brought. The was a hollow mockery, gaiety to ier l In despair she told her dream to Leon, who good-naturedly told her not to trouble her silly little head with the matter ; it was a common experience for people to dream mad things like that, but they did not trouble about them ; the less they did, so, the lesi Chance was .there that they would come true. , But Coria went away and wept. The ghastly dream seemed to her the shadow of impending sorrow. , ,</p>
        <p>I not</p>
        <p>416</p>
        <p>CHAPTER X. SUNLIGHT AND SHADOW.</p>
        <p>People partially wrecked will, in ,mad revenge, complete their ruin. Whom they cannot forgive and cannot forget they must strike down, even if the blow is fatal to themselves. All along Dr. Cardonna had nursed his wrath Bide by side with his grief, and now his craving for revenge was terrible in its strength. •Deter- ruination to avenge his great wrongs became urxre- lenting fierce and fixed. •</p>
        <p>So -far had his Mind been mastered that he found it impossible to eradicate the idea. At times his passions seemed to overpower him, for the effort to conquer the. temptation to break the Sixth Com- mandment utterly prostrated him. He fought hard to recover his former Control, but his will was permanently weakened. One Sunday morning, about three weeks before the date fixed for the marriage of Castilia to Leon, he went to church alone, and his family congratu- lated themselves on finding how really rational and happy he looked. himself "Father is nearly again," said Cori.; to her mother. The latter only shook her head, and said: "I trust it is so, dear, for his as well as your sake ; but I fear not." - - .</p>
        <p>His appearauce in church created some little sur- prise, for the doctor had attended. very little in&amp;ee since his reverse. He walked composedly ti.;</p>
        <p>front seat in the centre aisle.</p>
        <p>A carriage drove up a little later, and Vargas entered, lie was ushered to his accustomed place. When communicants arose to proceed to the altar rails_, he was amongst the first to rise. The doctor went too; and it so happened that Vargas and his victim—the swindler and the swindled--knelt down 'side bY side. The priest, as he approached with the wafers, saw, but did not repel the suppliant. Perhaps the pious man did not—in his anxiety to discharge his high and holy duty in 'a proper. spirit—observe the stern and resolute look in the doctor's eyes and face.</p>
        <p>With bowed head and bent back; the doctor seemed a model of meekness and humility; but,as the priest descended from the altar to the suppliants he instantly rose to his full height, and imperiously waving the priest aside, cried : "Not you, but I, shill administer to him the Sacrament—the last he will ever need!" breaking as he did so a phial on the head of Vargas. Turning towards the congregation, many of whom were standing and trembling, he drew a revolver and blew out his brains. He 'staggered and fell on the tesselated pavement quite dead, beside the writhing form of the banker. There was a stampede amongst the horror-stricken congregation, and news of the dreadful event was soon known. everywhere. Leon, disbelieving it, rushed into the cathedral before even medical assistance had arrived.</p>
        <p>The scene was terrible. He quickly recognised the doctor—the dead father of his intended bride—and beside him lay the unconscious banker, the so-called "Honest" Vargas, with the face, which had once so fascinated him, twisted with anguish. On forehead and scalp the skin turned quite black; the liquid ate its way deep into the purple- looking flesh, and soon the front of the skull was bare to the bone. . .</p>
        <p>The neckerchief was removed by the doctors, disclosing on the neck an ugly scar exactly similar in shape to that which Leon observed on the Spaniard in the English Lake district. "Great heavens!" exclaimed Leon, "there can be no mistake ; Vigo Lleren and Vargas are the same!" In these startling and tragic circumstances the discovery appalled Leon, ,making him feel sick at heart. But it was the key to a mystery. The unconscious and contorted form was removed at once. The subtle preparation of acids had almost done its foul and deadly work, and the face of the victim was already a frightful and hideous object to behold. Seldom are the sweet things of life unalloyed. Buffering and sorrow are the salt to human affairs. The death, and especially the circumstances asso- ciated with it, of the doctor brought grief, pain, and vain regret in its train to those few who mourned him. Coria was the heaviest sufferer, her terrib-C dream recurring with its full and awful meanin- now. Tragic incidents fascinate the human mind, and the doctor's funeral was a spectacle which few who saw it are likely to forget. Curiosity prompted half the inhabitants in Havana to witness it.</p>
        <p>--- - The deceased was referred to oper.ly as "the mad doctor," for it was surmised that he, not content with the attempt to deprive Vargas of his title for honesty, had endeavoured to take his life. The strain on the Cardonna family was severe. Mrs. Cardonna suddenly died. This took place within a week of the mad act of her sorel3 -tried husband, and her' death was due to the consequent excitement acting on a weak heart. As soon as yargai-9 was prorounced to be out of danger Leon called upon him, for he was anxious to fathom the mystery and clear away to some ex- tent the ignominy and shame which attached to Dr. Cardonna's name. At first Vargas, who, by the way, had entirely lost his sight, was obdurate, and pretended to be taken Aback by the nature of Leon's suggestions. Thereupon Leon rose and said: "Well, Vigo Lleren, alias Yargas, I shall not leave Havana till I have completely exposed you!" Fearing this disclosure and what it would lead tile feeble banker made a struggle to rise, but failed. The discovery of his identity 1•:-.2 overcome him, and he felt utterly helplee.''"iieThat do you 7tsquire for your silence ?" said Vargas. "That you admit your mistake about the iiocc‘s -- 74oney ; whatever else you will. It will be but a Icady reparation now." "You know you ask what I dare not do." r*Then I shall be compelled to arrest the man who ureg now was believed by everyone to be dead, to rainier for the life he took during a quarrel with bis wife in Madrid." "My God! do you know that too?" "I do; do as I say, or you must return with me to Spain to answer to the law." "You say you are leaving the island?" cc ye,. p&gt; "Then I will admit my euilt to clear his name.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <p>bat nis aaugnters must go wnii you :ma moo return." "That I can promise you I " added Leon in cow elusion. The confession caused a great sensation and, though it soon seemed to be forgotten, it Clare° the doctor's name for ever. A little later Vargas died, actually before the departure of Castilia and' Coria, to whoth, doubt from motives of justice and remorse, bequeathed double the amount he had stolen, frog' their father.</p>
        <p>, .•••• .. • . • Cardonna Villa was soon the happy home of thleo 1 two sisters. One bright and sunny morning bel rang out a merry peal; Castilia and Leon were tu4 happiest pair on -earth. It was their marrise morn, and at last the twain were now one, after fio the triala and misunderstandings. Coria Was in high glee*, for it reminded her the time when _a bright-eyed sailor should Co' across the seas to claim her would soon arrive. V, bridegroom presented Coria with a kolai lot and chain bearing the initials "C. C," in pearls. will be a dainty souvenir," she said to herself "when I too have passed through the golden gate' and some day I may have someone to leave it to, whose initials are the same, with the addition of F." She blushed with hope and pride. 0 When the bride and bridegroom and the gueeta were gone, doria felt a trifle heavy and sad, iln r when she went to rest she could not fall asleep, fe many thoughts disturbed her. That night she dreamt as she had only dreael once before, and her dream -was quite as vivid al depressing. ' She imagined she was married 011, away with Huesca on the high seas to see 0 people whom he talked so well and strangelf, j about. The first few weeks were very happy. One afternoon, when long, grey, gaseous cloo., rolled along, her husband became a little strange' his manner, and asked her to go below. She afraid that a storm was nigh, but he tried to sure her. He suddenly became serious and or&amp; her ,below. She refused, Looking. angri, but fending to be light-hearted, he said: 'You'. Sorry you didn't soon; there's to be lively wo: The decks were quickly cleared, and the I crouched under the bulwarks with their weal handy. She would willingly have gone below. • Huesca, the only man exposed to the enemy's Yi did not give her the opportunity now. He watching the sails and the sky. The pr vessel gain4cl rapidly upon the runaway. Both vessels soon bumped together. To horror, Castello stood on the bulwarks of the ship, and Huesca sprang up 0 cut him down his sword. Her husband missed his footing fell intothe sea, and Castello took her away; they were married! Coria's head was aching when she awoke. A letter arrived that morning, however, w' cheered her, for she had not heard from Huesca a long time. The voyage had so far been very f cessful, and he hoped within the year to come claim his winsome bride.</p>
        <p>Not having been so successful as he had Psted, and fearing to undertake responsibilitiet might be unable to properly discharge, Huesca actually -returned to his old piratical ways.; ways of his boyhood, and of his father before It was a dazzling but illusionary road' to fort and, like most short cuts, a sure way to speedy cess or total failure. This accounted for irregularity of his letters and the different Oslo which he visited lying far apart. Many place dared not touch at. He kept his secret well, 'eo secrets have a way of leaking out. He had 13e01 easily won over to the wicked life, for he 1,°„0 adventure. For a year or more now he had the terror of the South Pacific, intercepting,s,, plundering vessels bound for San Franco,s seriously harassing and hampering trade with tfoi port. But one calm day the gay pirate WO I-0, down, and Qoria six months later read the fell°t: ing in the slitipping column of a Spanish newspar,,e , "A despatch from 'Valparaiso, dated —June,' tbe states that Captain Castello in command otiud Spanish cruiser Trintos, sighted the pirate Cloud, one hundred miles off San Franr'qbe latitude 25, longitude 105. After a hard fight ati. vessel was captured, with the loss of three trord The gallant young officer was killed by the of Huesca °Ferrol, a Spaniard, who left many years ago. Next morning he was ordere'cot the court-martial to be shot, his body beieg into the sea. Commander Castello, whose pr°so ing career was so suddenly cut short, Wit,,e4 buried at sea. One of the pirate crew eo0"-;net that the schooner was sometimes painted gref,,tiorl sometimes carmine, and volunteered infolt„aer which went to shew that the chief acts of I,l°—bed in the Southern Seas during the past two years off been planned by, the deceased pirate. The mealeni of Commander Castello should be revered by el navigator in the Southern Seas." • The frenzied girl burnt the paper ; she _llea nothing more would be discovered respecting 0° Ferrol. f . • Corm was missing next day, and no trace whereabouts was to be had during the succee sbe six months. As time wore on it was aPPP°Beci had drowned herself or left Spain on learnia sad news.</p>
        <p>[ ,</p>
        <p>, Four vearalater a careworn, wasted I^' neither old nor young, spoke to Leon. "Don" know me ? " she said familiarly. "I don't, I assure you." "Oh, you don't recognise me? Very welt" laughed. "Are you quite sure ? " oeB "Quite; but who-axe , you, woman?" he .11 indifferently. 11 • e "Is my presence so odious that you must cat tell woman? If you do not know me, I shall 110,01' you!" Then she left abruptly, crossing the way and entering an old, disreputable-looking shop. Next day. Leon read in a paper: "Last ni woman was found insensible on a doorstep, ALIO being removed to the hospital she died. ;Pry, her neck was a gold chain and locket, Isl ~,,,M letters 'C. C.' set in pearls. Her name was and her associates believed she had come of a r family." i! ertiell A Leon threw down the paper, saying: was Cone who spoke to me last night. Thar"' her miserable life is ended!"</p>
