      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>"CAPITAL" CHAT.</head>
        <p>"CAPITAL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>If Lord Wolseley were to accept the Governor- I Ueneralship of Canada it would involve the resig- nation of his present office of Commander-in-Chief, Whi.ch has still two and a half years to run. The Position of head of the army is one which crowns the ambition of a lifetime, and I think I can say that he would resign it with regreG if not reluct- !nee. At the same time, the acceptance of the minadian Viceroyalty means a salary q £lO,OOO a YOar, with certain allowances, agains! £4,500 a year Without any allowances whatever. Tbe tempta- !lons, therefore, are strong, especially when backed °I the fact that Lord Wolseley has many Canadian heaociations. Ilia resignation will give rise to a teaewal of the intrigues as to his successor that attended the resignation of the Duke of Cambridge.</p>
        <p>, The Italian Government proceeds steanily from bad to wors.e, and if its efforts to set back the eleek a Century or so in dealing with freedom of !Peech and freedom of the Press are met by coun- Eet violence, it will have only itself to thank. It ttittinues to repress all the Socialist, clerical, and Industrial associations it can come upontas the only raeans that occurs to its intelligence for meeting the dor, -r-seated discontent at the present anarchic and repressive form of official rule. As if this were not "Ikeirgb, it proposes to make the laws dealing with "ue Press and the right of association still more re- pressive, and in fine, to govern Italy permanently Under the equivalent of a state of seine. The people 111,4 ay, and do, starve, while rich and poor alike are 'eepoiled to maintain preposterously bloated arms- bleats ; but,if the Government can prevent it, they hall not be suffered to make their grievances heard. 'act this policy of sitting on the safety valve con- tact last, and if the Government continues as it has 'eat* going on, not only the Radial Ministry, but tore enduring institutions will be .swept away by the uprising of a people who are being taxed and Persecuted beyond human endurance.</p>
        <p>The Parliamentary —Golf Tournament has now roached its final stage. The play has this year been delayed more than most by the Parliamentery en- gagements of those who entered, and the deciding rim 06,s will not take place till the autumn. Mr. fl 'eton-Karr, by defeating Mr. Wan. klyn on Monday :Itt Mitcham, has secured Lancashire a place in the !nal, which will thus be a contest between the k, rounty Palatine and Yorkshire,Mr. Fason,the mem- ber for Doncaster, playing for the latter county. Mr. ;lets:in-Karr won easily on Monday by 5 and 4 to play, has he was giv ng two strokes in the round He 'ma never played golf so well as he has done this season He has had four stiff matches to face, and 45 has in every case won decisively;while on Satur- day, playing at Tooting against the homa club for ..the House of Commons, he was one of the very 44W members who won their matches. The final With Mr. Fison will probably be played at St. An- drevvs in September or October. The delay is conse- quent on the member for Doncaster having left town for a few weeks' trip to Norway. When the match espies off the Lancashire player will have to tonc.ede his opponent seven strokes in the round, if be keeps up his game he may be able to do ',Mt mid win.</p>
        <p>I nd ofu th eratand that the explanation given in Paris e despatch of so many additional troops to. ,harda the valley of the Upper Nile is that they are tended not to reinforce the Marchand expedition Out to take the plare of certain companies of Sane- Ralese who have either already completed or are !about to complete their term of service in the far Interior. Meanwhile it is to be noted, notwith- standing the confident tone of the French papers, that the latest from Captain Marchand shows that ae was still on the Saab river at the beginning of "ecember, where he had arrived about the end of A .ugust with portions of the single steamer which gures as the" "in the forecast of French successes to which the public on the other side of the Channel are treated.</p>
        <p>The War Office has reinstated the various officers Who were cempelled to resign their commissions in consequence of the Jameson raid, with the excep- tion of Sir John Willoughby and Colonel Rhodes. The. restored are four in number, and, looking to the insincerity which has marked the whole conduct of the Government in this business, from first to last including the burlesque of a parliamentary inquiry, WaS about the least Ministers could do. They have allowed the archlintriguer to go scot fre&gt;,and almost to be overwhelmed with honours, and they fastened upon a number of underlings for punish. tent. It is well that the hypocrisy should cease. /..„Ye campaizn will now be directed to rein-tating Nl' John Willoughby and Colonel Rhodes, and pro!), ably next year their offence also will be condoned.</p>
        <p>The cordial reception given by several members ,cll the Licensing Commission last week to the pro- Vftis of the Church of England Temperance ;ociety's bill has evoked considerable discussion in Lord circles. The friendly criticisms of 'ord Peel, however, have suggested to one or two teading members of the Society whether the till not in one or two respects amended wqb advantage. Mr. E. Star- ,Ord iloward,who e g xplained the bill to the Commis- oo, has accordingly given notice that at the July keeting of the Executive of the C.E.T.S. he will atibmit certain alternative proposals. These are— that the reduction in the number of licenses pould be carried out at once ; (2) those granted in ,uture should be granted for a term of five years ( they should be offered to the highest bidder ,hiect to a fixed licence rental to be assessed by the licensing authority; (4) at the end of each Period of five years an opportunity should be given to the people of every parish, under the provisions ' of the Local Government Act, of voting whether any licences should again be --ranted or not; (5) if a kofficient msjority—two-thirds—declare against any licenses none would be granted; (6) the money raised in this way could be appropriated towards cn x-ennlpeusating those v, ho may be deprived of the a b:Tal Of their licences, in the first instance upon .^"8 to be det ermined by Parliament, and after arde it could be appropriated for certain public Purposes. The meeting of the Executive at which "e Proposals will be brought forward has been fix for July 12, and an important debate is anti- `-,Pated,</p>
        <p>The sensation among Church people with refer- ence to the retention of Mr. Hooley's gift of a gold ?onlmunion service to St. Paul's Cathedral is becom- Ing very acute. Canon Scott Holland has added Fonsidetably to the uneasiness of tender consciences °Y the touch of sophistry with which he defends tthe retention of the gift, which is valued at 12.6 tween £2,000 and £3,000, and a public subscrip- tion is now talked of to substitute a new service of Raid plate for Mr. Hooley's. It is not improbable that if the dean and chapter were to keep hold of tlleir gift—and they are very unwilling to sur- !end ir it—they might find themselves called upon t° answer a demand for its restitution.</p>
        <p>Lord Charles Seresford has at last succeeded in treating something like a panic among whose who Ilte Imperialistic in instinct rather than from any rpert knowledge of the condition of the fleet, and Goschen will have a warm time of it now with reference to the obsolete guns with which so many ;lour vessels are provided. This candid friend of he Government puts the number of battleships which are provided with antiquated and slow-firing Inns at a fourth, and he states that the total num- er of vessels in the fleet which are furnished with :eh guns is no fewer than 45. All this must have ~411 known to successive Boards of Admiralty, and one after the other, they have allowed the to sleep in a fool's paradise, The shipbuild- evote cannot too soon be brought under discus- ,c)n. Even Lord George Hamilton's optimistic rh? ances in recent years are now seen to count r nothing.</p>
        <p>„in a statement issued from the Foreign Office ;"i8 week Mr. W. S. H. Gastrell, commercial attache '0 the British Embassy at Berlin, makes a striking 03parison of the export trade of Germany with ttat of England and France in the first quarter of ".s Current year. While English exports,compared ptit the corresponding quarter of 1897, showed a Irainution of about £1,600,000, and French ex- torts a decrease of £625,000, the export trade of ttla German Empire showed an increase of no less ~tuall £3,664,500, although Germany, equally with 47” nations, was suffering from the effect of the , 'W United States tariff. At appropriate common- ' ,t4tY on these disconcerting statistics is supplied by ItiP.a.ssage from the report, also issued to-day, of the atfitish Consul at Genoa. "Amongst the causes." he " which militate against us in competing 41'n continental nations, notably Germany, may be 1 .-entiosed the unwillingness of British firms to Ts,o Credit to the few travellers employed by them Ns,i"„Dared with the legion hailing from Germany 1,24 samples, and with quotations in Italian cur- for Italian weights and measures, and for de- m, free of charge."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <p>I</p>
        <p>OUR NAVY -- CRITICISIII3 BY LORD CHA.RLS</p>
        <p>BERESFORD.</p>
        <p>AN URGENT APPEAL. The Press Association has been requested to pub- lish the following letter from Lord Charles Beres- ford, respecting the inadequacy of the navy and the steps necessary to make it efficient : Sir,—l have always sought to study naval requirements as much from the view of the poli- tician as from that of the naval officer, Parliament being really responsible for the defence of the empire. On many occasions large additions to our naval defence have proved that the policy which I had previously advocated was a necessity. Not since the year 1888 have I felt so alarmed about the gate of our naval defences. The reasons for such arfirm are apparent. The estimate for new construction for last year tell short of the minimum considered necessary by responsible authority by no less a sum than £2,270,000. 2. We have a decrease of £961,303 on the normal estimate of 71 millions for the last two years if we are making up £1,400,000 of the deficit this year. 3. Parliamentary return No. 206 of 1898 clearly shows that not only are we falling behind the proportional standard of numbers of 3to 2 laid down by authority as necessary, but we have actu- ally not even an equality of numbers with France and Russia (numbers refer to built, building, and projected). 4. We are confronted with most unfriendly actions abroad. 5. We are alarmed with the most dinuieting speeches at home made by members of the Cabinet. There is a sense of uneasiness in the country. The only optimists are the Ministers responsible for our defences. to the actual facts f Position. I appeal to my It is timed a2o t ro s stir o f the tohurpro ouubihi c P the medium of the press,fell ow to co unt, considerym through situation. I am satisfied if its dangers are known the Government will be forced to propose a supplementary estimate. Accord- ing to present appearances and official !utterances there is no intention whatever of bringing in a sup- plementary estimate. I consider delay is criminal. 3w8487448,79t1h,e04e35. estimate Now to prove my statements. The eat constrnction imate for new forwas l aB9great for innledw96c 90 7n swt ra us c on' outcry in the House of Commons and the country at this apparent decrease last Year,but the First' Lord argued that,the two years should be taken together a ad coo!: the t year's programme had b preceding Year. His ex- normal average planation gives a estimate of £7,037,957 iLdritphu at rpso oze yppotrateioe for new construction in each year. The public were not satisfied,and the Government promised if any increase its foreign r rangnnaents took place the vote should be a bpVrotnise was fulfilled and £400,000 added, mate. la July last supplementary estimate iynocarre a thissed bringing the total vote for 1897-98 for new construction to £7.191.043, or taking the average of First Lord the two years as requested by the, a normal vote of 7i minions per annum. As a matter of fact,the programme r fell £2,270,000 below the estimate, and new icaosntstreitction was eerily represented by £4,920,252 The authorities have told the country that they are going to make up £1,300,000 of this deficit in 1898 99, If this was really to be done it would make the estimate £8,650,000, taking the normal estimate at 7± millions. i But the actual estimate is only £7,638,697. It is there- fore only £538,697 over the normal average estimate for the past two years. These figures looked at from any point of view are alarming. Last year the political horizon was clearer than now. Last year the Dual Alliance was not making extraordinary preparations. Last year there were no particular signs of irritation abroad. Other nations had not se,cured positions of advantage or inflicted diploma- tic defeats upon us, because we then believed in the honour of assurances given. Yet,although we are short of what the Admiralty considered necessary oa last year's peace programme, we are failing to make up the whole of our deficit, and we are reducing our shipbuild- ing programme for the present year, it being impossible to use the £1,400,000 both for making up part of the deficit and as new 1898 99 construction. The want of businesslike management with regard to the administration of the services was very apparent. Lately the Chancellor of the Exchequer has shown him- self willing to give the heads of those departments all that they need to mike our defences adequate. Our position must be known to authority. Is it gonna fin- ance or cemmoneense to have devoted £1,505,000 to the relief of taxation, which, though perhaps important,can in no way compare with the necessaries of the defence of the Empire. If the Admiralty had represente I the case as it is there is no doubt they would have bad that money. The war between the United States and Spain has taught us the absolute necessity of complete and thorough organisation previous to war instead of trusting to chances. It hae taught us the value of a proper sup- ply of cruisers. It has taught us the ineff?.ctiyeness of obsolete guns against the range and accuracy of modern weapons. Any Government that gent out man to firht in blue water, or relied for harbour protection on the 45 vessels in the 13,itiun Navy armed with muzee-loading gone, would certainly get a short shrift. No other nation In the whole world has a ship on the effective list armed with muzzle-loading guns. Parliamentary return 218 of 1838 made out that we had 87 armoured voese's,againet 85 of France and Russia combined. I pointed out in the House on December 18 of that year that the return included eight non-elf c'ive yeasels, two of which.the Lord Warden and the Reprise, wore actually placed on the elf sctive list and advertised for sale as old iron at the same moment. Parliamentary return 206 of 1893 shows a deficit in armoured ships of twelve, even whm including ships treated as obsolete ten years ago. SIDINIA.RY OF TFIE NEW REFURN. Great France and</p>
        <p>13—ritain. France. Russia. Rusgia. Battieshins .................. 64 ...... 35 Armoured cruisers.... .... 26 Coast defence ships ..... 15</p>
        <p>Total... ............... 105 68 49 117 These fi'tures are mi3lea3lnt to the public, as 37 of the British arrnoored ships are armed with muzzl,loading auns—viz., 18 battleships, 9 cruisers, and 12 clout defence ships. The whole of our colst defence ships are useless for employment on the enemy's coasts,and armed as at present aro useless for harbour defence in our own ports. The coast defence vessels of France and Russia are real daneerous antaYonists. SUMMARY OF VESSELS CLASSIFIED. CLASS A.--Heavy armament, speed 16 knots or more. eutek-firing armament, and a displacement of 9,000 tons and upwards. Great Britain. France. Russia. 3 New projected (a) 3 Jenas (b) 7 Peresvyets (e) 3 Formidahles 3 Charlernagnes 2 Three Saints 6 Canopnses I Henry IV. 3 Poltava; 9 Majesties 4 Carnots 2 Mimi Valekts I Renown 1 Jawlequiberry 1 George Victo- rious 1 Navarin</p>
        <p>Royal Bo rereigns 1 Brennus Niles Centurions ......</p>
        <p>34 13 16 (a) Six not laid down yet. (b) Two not laid down yet. (c) Five not hid down yet. CLAW; B 1 —Modern battleships of 4.000 to 9,000 tons in- ferior in radius of action but powerful and, heavily armed— Great Britain. 0---</p>
        <p>Russia. 1 Twelve Apostles 3 Oushakovs. CLASS B 2.—Bittleships of over ten years of age, and not so good in armour .or for other reasons— Great Britain-1 Sansparetl, 6 Admirals, 2 Colossuses, 2 Conquerors-11. France.-3 Magentas, 1 Roche. 2 Formidables (ARM), 1 _Duperre, 2 Devastations (ARE), 2 Carmans (ARE)</p>
        <p>France. 4 Valmys.</p>
        <p>-13. Rands. —3 Sinopes, 2 ANtander 11-5. (A.RI4 denotes reconstruction o, rearmed nadir. CLASS B.—Refitted battleshins, rearmed thronghout Great Britain. France. Russia. 2 Devastations 1 Recloutable 0— I Runert I Furious 0- 1 Bellerophon 0— 0- -</p>
        <p>. 2 0 Summary of Eff active Line of Battleships— Great Britain. France. Russia. Class A Class B 1 .... ...... .. 0 Class B 2 .......... .. 11 Class C</p>
        <p>Totals 49 57 0 Battleships not included in tbia list are absolutely use- less under present conditions. We are therefore danger- ously inferior in numbers. lam quite willing to admit tbat in the new ships our vessels have a greater fighting value so far as radius of action is concerned, but it must be remembered that in many cases the French and Ilosssian are better armed, and, in some cases.as heavily artnimred, while our superiority in individual vessels is met by the fact that under any circumstanles our tac- tics would necessarily be to capture and destroy the enemy in his own waters, where he is nearer his base, and we lose our advantage in greater coal supplies and are more open to tprpldo attack. The accuracy of a torpedo is now certain if properly laid and provided with the gyroscope attachment. These facts make it necessary ' that we should have superiority in numbers as well as a superiority in class.</p>
