      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>EXAIVIINER, FRIDAY, JUNE 17, 1898.</head>
        <p>THE EXAIVIINER, FRIDAY, JUNE 17, 1898.</p>
        <p>ARMOURED CRUISERS. CLASS A.—Speed 20 kw ,ts and over. protection on the water line and to guns. Great Britain. Francs. Russia. Dressys 6 Montcalms 1 Grenoble( 3 Duenlelas 1 projected 1 Jeanne D'Arc 1 Dupuy de loose</p>
        <p>7 Auroras</p>
        <p>- - CLiss B.—gorse-116 knots awl. over. 1 Pothers 1 Rossiya 4 Latouche Tre. vile 1 Rurik</p>
        <p>7 5 2 °CASS C.—Othcrs efficient unler 16 knot 3— • Great Britain, Fr Ince. Russia. 2 Imperieux 2 Vcillbans 1 Machimor ...... I Vladimir Monomach 1 Panyat Azov% 1 Diuitri Donskoi</p>
        <p>2 2 4 Hare again we are actually below the standard of the next two Powers and in no way approach the ideal 3 to 2. In unarmoured cruisers we are well ahead, except that we have no vessel fit to cope with the French pro- rated 23 knot cruisers and tho Russian volunteer fleet. We might need ten very high speed projected cruisers for tips purpese,but not vessels of the size of the Power- ful and Terrible. 1.1 tve the Government at present a standard ? If o,what is it? I have conclusively shown that it is not a standard of 3 to 2 as laid down some years ago. Our shipbuilding programme must be based on the construction (built, building, and projected), of foreign countries. It is useless for the authorities to tell us they are watching what other councries are do- ing. What the Empire needs is men who are working !up to and over foreign programmes. The Admiralty were either wrong in their programme last year and asked too mue,h,or they are wrong now and ask too little. The fact is, they have abandoned the steady working programme covering a space of time, and have reverted to the old policy which means patchwork and panic. We bevelled experience of the utility and effectiveness of a five years' programme,viz., Naval Defence Act,1386. We ought te have a similar programme for five years made out now. Its advantages are tremendous. It is more businesslike. It gives manufacturers a chance, and they know what is wanted. Ships will not be delayed for armour, guns, mountings, beilers, and engines, as they must of necessity be with a one year's programme. Ex- perience has taught us that with a settled programme other countries do not attempt to compete v.ith us. Directly we finished our last programme and waited a year or two to make up our minds over one or two ships, other Powers began to try and overtake us. It is no use finding fault without submitting a proposal. The following is what I believe is necessary for our needs : Tonnage. Class. Per ton. Total. Nine bsttleships 15,000...Form'clables .. 67 ... 9.000,000 Twelve ditto - ... 67 8,040,000 Twelve armoured cruisers Twelve ditto 7,000... Fifty destroyve, £50,°00 esch — ••• Eight steam col- liers 4,009... ... 33 ... 1.000,001</p>
        <p>-- -. 60 -. 8643,010 -- -. 60 -. 4,500,003</p>
        <p>". __ _... _. 2,500.000</p>
        <p>Total £33,690,000 If £1,500,000 were added to this for re-arming the 17 useful old ironclads, as I have frequently advocated, the total would be brought up to nearly £35,000,000, which seems a large sum,but is really only based on the normal estimate for the last three years for new construction. The colliers are an all-important question when coal is the breath and life of the fleet, and being able to coal quickly will seal the fate of a campaign. The colliers should be 16 knots. These and the re-arm- ing of the old useful ironclads should be placed in hand at once by a supplementary estimate for as much of the work as could be carried out. Toe remainder of the money snould be spread over the next five years. I I earnestly trust that the press and the public will ex- amine these statements and carefully weigh them. If the Government do not seriously grapple with the question of new construction, the only resource will be to arrange a series of meetings throughout the country, when the irresistible force of public opinion would probably pro- dnce the same effect as it did on rrevious occasions. (Signed) Yours faithfully, CHARLES' BERESFORD 2, Lower 131”keley-street, Portman.sq itro, W. June 10. h, 1893.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>STOCKTON REA3II. ----</head>
        <p>STOCKTON REA3II. ----</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEA.Ta ME MS. BY " 0113ERVER."</p>
        <p>THE CHURCH SCHOOLS.—Some few weeks ago a statement appeared in the " Liverpool Ex- press," presumably written by an inspired corre- 6pondont who resides in Stockton Heath, to the effect that the girls attending the Church Schools had been sent home because they had their hair in curl papers, preparatory to their taking part in an entertainment in a room not connected with the church, and drawing attention to the fact that this had not been done when the children were going to take part in similar entertainments connected with , the church and held in the Church Room. The following paragraph published in the "Stockton Heath Parish Monthly" this month throws a cliff-Ir- ene light upon the matter: " We would draw the parents' attention to the following notice written by the Inspector in the School Log Book. 'Girls should not be allowed to come to School in curl papers. No girl would be allowed to attend a High School in such a state, and there is no reason why Public Elementary Schools should encourage middle class slovenly habits."' •</p>
        <p>PENNY 'BUSSES.—It would appear that the age of penny 'busses to Stockton Heath has . not yet arrived. From what can be gathered a gentleman faithful to his word, did put on an extra 'bus with a view to establishing penny fares, but he was promptly threatened with a summons for obstruc- tion by the local police if he continued. Fancy ob- struction and in Victoria Square too. It is also stated that an anonymous letter, has been received by the police authorities of Warrington, pointing out how dangerous this traffic was on the Cause- way. Here is a nice piece of work for the Parish Council to take up, seeing they are all so intent on bettering their ever increasing village. If only to keep them from falling out at their meetings, they might see to it. It would be of more advantage to the village than even the suggested supplementary fire brigade. It may be that Stockton Heath is above penny busses ;—but what with rates going up, and 'bus fares dearer than to any other suburb of Warrington, one would think it is rather an ex- pensive place to live in. A CONUNDRUIL—What shall we do if the engine fails—and it looks delicate—or the wire rope breaks, at the Swing Bridge? Answer Parish Councillors—one at once please. JUBILEE SEATS.—From latest reports the Jubilee seat at Lawrenson's Brow, planted in the tield,has taken root and is expected to bloom in due course. Perhaps a few cuttings may be got from it and more seats propagated. I am pleased to observe that the difference which has existed between the Tree anh the Jubilee seat on;the "Village Green"— note new name for about twenty feet of grass— (title registered),—is now at an end. For some months they stood aloof from each other, but now we find that the seat has folded the tree into its arms or the tree has crept there, but whichever has happened they now exist together in harmony, and it is hoped also that the residents near by will also settle down and forego the lamp. JUBILEE CELEBRATIONS.—Another conun- drum: When will the Accounts of these be fur- nished? FOOTPATHS.—From the report of the Parochial Committee it vvill be seen that it is intended to ask the County Council to take up the stones at the end of Walton-road and repave with chequered tiles. Anyone who has any acquaintance with this kind of footpath, knows what a nuisance tiles are, particu- larly in vue6 weather, when they become loose and do an unexpected squelch up your trousers leg, Why not go in for flag, ? I believe in the borough they have decided not to pave footpaths in future with these tiles. FIRE APPLIANCES.—Anyone will see from the reports of the Parish Council meetings lately held, that Stocktton Heath is great on fire appliances and all that pertains thereto. A covered stand is to be erected in Victoria-square to contain appliances. Oh! won't the " young Heathens" rejoice at more accommodation being found for them to practice jumping and climbing I—They have been vety Con- tented up to now with the seat, but will readily appreciate the stand, which will afford more scope fgr carving.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>NOTES AND COIIENT3. BY E.E.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND COIIENT3. BY E.E.</p>
        <p>It is satisfactory to note—from the report of their last meeting—that the Par i Parochial Committee o i f Stockton Heath are inquiring into the rateable question. It is a question which is of vital impor- tance to all who reside in the place—whether they pay rates direct themselves or pay rates indirectly through their landlord.</p>
        <p>In considering the question of rates in Stockton Heath, it should not be overlooked that the district is quite distinct in its character to most of the other portions under the same control, viz., the Runcorn It Rural District Council. Stockton Heath is resi- d theyeayone half. lis obvious then to are agricultural. Now residences pof the rate,. while agricultural districtsanyt h ia t le,w full f onlyamount anyone who studies the question that the Stockton Heath ratepayers are paying nearly double to the Union what many of the ratepayers are in several other portions of the Union.</p>
        <p>The whole of our rateable value which is nearly £7,000 and is increasing rapidly is with the excep- tion of rather less than £lOO rated under that head of" Buildings." It will thus be seen that almost every ratepayer in Stockton Heath contributes to the Union the full amount of the rate as against one half paid by many others who live in other pot.. tions of the Union and who are rated under the head of" agriculture."</p>
        <p>Now what the Parochial Committee should do in order to help them to arrive at a solution of the question is to see that they are furnished with a return of the other portions of the Union. flow much is rated as " agricultural land' and how mnch is rated as" Buildings." Also the total amount of rates each:District furnishes to the Union.</p>
        <p>If this matter is gone into carefully and per- severingly I firmly believe that our Parish Council will either demand a re-adjust- ment, which would lead to a consider- able relief to the ratepayers, or that they would advise that wa break away from the Union. Stockton Heath for years has paid evidently more in rates than she ought to have done and she has fHoreatthheißs the co (1 been so"farmed"that she has very materielly helped to keep down the rates in other portions of the Runcorn Unions. The simile which a ratepayer once men- tioned 1 is that Stoecgkgter rn Rural "° 3 that lays the golden 1 Dis- trict Council and this expresses our position pretty well. •</p>
        <p>The Stockton Heath ratepayers have been long suffering over this matter but now that their rep- resentatives have opened an enquiry it is to be hoped that they, will have the necessary perse- verance and persistence requirea to bring it to a happy termination.</p>
        <p>It, may be said by some that we have certain advantages which some other portions of the Union for separately—under a 'Special have not got. That is true. But what advantage we e advantage Stockton Heath obtains,the union Anynavetakes good care !)toekton Heath pays for itself.</p>
        <p>Much sympathy has gone out to the family of Mr. Down over the sad logs they have sustained, and in this expression of sympathy I feelingly join.</p>
