      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>*APPLETON THORN.</head>
        <p>*APPLETON THORN.</p>
        <p>They sang the song of Appleton Thorn, In years before the Bard was born; Of sturdy men and maidens fair Who frollick'd and danced the May-tide there ! And robust youths in blouse and breech, Whispering eternal vows in speech Of happy youth so gay and free, Gambolling round the sacred Tree! The feath.ed songsters poured their strain Of melody o'er hill and plain And trees, and flowers, and meadows green, Unite to cheer the syivan scene. Where are they now ! Ah ! lackaday, Child, youth, and man, have pass'd away ; And maidens into matrons changed, As Time imperative, has arranged ! The generations each, succee4, And ages over ages speed. The birds are singing as of yore, And nature wears the garb she wore ; The trees, and flowers, and meadows fair, Still see the oxen grazing there But happy, frail humanity. Thera is no staying here for the. - - JAMES MUIR.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>*Appleton is mentioned in Dove Any link, as Eppletane-with-tfull, nelr a great wool.</p>
        <p>WARRINGT')N_ BAN-KM:FPI/CY COURT,</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before Mr. Registrar Ridgway.</p>
        <p>FAILURE OF A WARRINGTON CARVER AND GILDER. William Lowe, junior, residing and carrying on business as a carver and gilder at 53. Sankey-street, Warrington, and previously residing at 50, Legh-street. appeared for his public examination in bankruptcy. The bankrupt's statement of affairs showed that the liabilties were expected to rink at £228 53. 41.. and after realising the assets there yeas a deficiency of £177 74. 7d.ln his state- ment to the Official lt,ceiver the debtor stated that he commenced business in 1893. without capital. He was in partnership, from 1893 to May, 1897, with an uncle ; and partnership liabilities were still outstanding. He was unable to file deficiency accnunt,not having kept Proper books of account, He first became aware of his insolvency last January, and had only, since that t'me, contracted two small debts now owing. The furniture, included in bill of sale, executed about May. 1897, was sold by auction in November last, and realised about £lOO, which, after payment of Bill of Sale Holder's claim £5O), rent, &amp;c.. yielded him £lO only. In answer to Mr. Potter (assistant official receiver), the debtor said he dissolved partnership with his brother, Robert Lowe, in April, 1897. The cause of insolvency was want of capital. He commenced borrowing from moneylenders twelve months ago. He first became aware of his insolvency in January, of this year. He was unable to pay some of his debts as they became due last summer. He did not think he was ins olvent when he had three executions in at the same time. He gave a bill of sale in May, 1897, because he wanted some money at the tirne.He could not say why the scheduled debts of £59 owing since MG were not paid long ago. He could not give any justification for going on contracting debts when he couldn't pay the existing debts. Ile was unable to explain his deficiency.—Cross-examined by Mr. Mos- worth : The business according to the statement he drew up was in a solvent condition when he dissolved litartnership.—lly Mr. Willett : He sold his furniture so that he might go from Legh-street to Sankey-street, and thus cut down his expenses.—Mr. Potter said the bank- rupt's conduct had not been satisfactory. When he was requested by the Official Receiver to do a thing he ought to do it. He had failed to keep app,intmlnti and to carry out certain Instructions, and had thereby given the Official Receiver much trouble.—The Registrar hav- ing. cautioned the debtor, the examination was closed subject to the signing of the shorthand writer's notes.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED NEGLECT AT WAIIRINGTON.</p>
        <p>MAGISTRATES VISIT DEFENDANTS' HOME. Before the Mayor (Alderman J. E. Wright). Alderman Roberts, and Messrs. Hepherd and Smet- hurst, George and Selina Foster. of Napier-street, appeared at the Police Court on Fridav on the fourth adjournment of a summons against them charging them with neglect of family. Since the case was first heard, one of the children had died, and a coroner's jury had found that its death was due to natural causes. Mr. Arthur Browne appeared on behalf of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children, and said that there had been no im- provement despite the frequent adjournments. Alderman Roberts said he, in company with Dr. Gornall, another magistrate, visited the house on Thursday morning, and said that he had found it much cleaner than he had expected. It was no worse than 99 out of 100 houses in Warrington, though there were signs of poverty. The Mayor and Councillor Sms%hurst left the court, visited the house, and on their return dis- missed the case.</p>
        <p>B IRDS, ANINIALS AND FISH STUFFED, AND mounted in Glass Cases any design. HEADS and HORNS mounted, also for sale. Birds made into Hand Screens or Fire Screens. Deals iii British and Foreign Birds and Animals' Skins. Butterflies, Birds' Eggs, Glass shades, Stags' Heads. Cabinets. Store Boxes, Nets and all requisites for Butterfly and Egg Collectors.—B. Comm. Naturalist and Furrier, 21, Renshaw - street, LiTerpool Established 24 years.</p>
