      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>IMPERIL P' ItLIA.NENT.</head>
        <p>IMPERIL P' ItLIA.NENT.</p>
        <p>liOU3i OF LORDS.—MoNDLY. In the House of Lords on Mon is'', Lord ()LURING-TON and. the Duke of ABERCORN asked questions relativo to the restoration of their commissions to the officers who took part in 'he Jameson Raid. The Marquis of LABSDoWNE intimated in the course of &amp; long speech that the commissions of the officers would cc restored. subject to certain conditions, with the exception of those of Coloael Prank Rho ies and Sir John Willoughby, who had taken responsible parts in connection with the conspiraov. Lord CARRINGTON maintained that the position taken up by the Secretary of State for War could not be legally defended Lord SALISBURY, replying to the Bari of Camperdown as to the lease of territory by this country near Hong Kong, said what had happened was that oppor- tunity had. been taken to remedy a want which had been felt for a long tims, not only to give a slight extension to tha co'ony of Hong Kong, but also to make it strAtegically safe.</p>
        <p>_ HOUSE OF COMMONS- —Momos.V. In the House of Commons, Me. Baonsfot replied to a series of questions as to the War Mee and Piper Findlater. and intimated that it had been decided that the sum of £5O might be granted by way of pension to soldiers who had won the Vietoria Cross, taste id of £lO which had turompanied it since its institution —Other questions were in reference to the Belfast riota, the Spanish aeents in Canada. Government departments and phoshoreseent matches, and the situation in I Swat land.—The house went into committee on the Finance 818, and Mr. J. A: PEASE moved the omission of the first clause, for the purpose of raising the question whether the £1,120,003 at the disposal of the Chancellor of the Exchequer should not be applied to the reduction of the duty on tea rather than the tobacco duty,hte contention being that the re- lief should be given in f svour of the article which waa con- sumed by the greater number of people. Blr WILLIAM Hiss- COURT said he believed that a reductien of the tea duty by 2d in the pound would only cost one million, and that the whole benefit would be to the consumer. Sir JsMN WILLox contended that the ke:nnese of competition in the trade would ensure that the full benefit of any increase in the profit of tobacco manufacturers would go to the consumers. The CIIANCELLOR of the EXMIEQUER said that if they reduced the tea duty it would be necessary to reduce also the duty on coffee and chicory, and the abolition of those duties would relieve from taxation all below the income-tax limit who neither used alcohol nor smoked. He certainly would not be responsible for such a proposal as that. Mr. Pease's motion was rejected. HOUSE OF LORDS.— TUESDAY. In the House of L axis on Tuesday. the Marquis of LANs- DOWVE moved the second reading of the Reserve Forces Bill. This measyre. he said, provided that men of good character belonging to the first class of the army reserve should, if they so agreed. he liable during the first twelve months of their service in that resrrve to be called out on permanent service abroad without such proclamation as was required under the Reserve Forces Act, provided the number of men so liable did not at any time exceed 5,003. It was also proposed, as an additional means of strengthening the home battalions, to allow trained men of the militia to serve with hem for one year, and the bill would further permit of militia battalions serving abroad. The bill was read a second time. HOUSE OF COMMONS.—TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons. on the motion for the second reading of an Eleotrin Lighting Provisional Order relating to Marylebone, Mr. ORIPPS moved the rejection of the Bill, which, he said, involved the question whether.when a private company had obtained a concession for forty-two years and was admittedly fulfilliog its obligations, the loos' authority should step in and, by means of a rate-aided competition, be allowed to crush out the private company. After some de- bate the motion for the second reading was carried by 198 votes to 114 A proposal to refer the bill to a hybrid Com- mittee was withdrawn and the bill will now be referred to an ordinary Private Bill Committee. —Mr. Caaminiataier. reply- ing to Captain Begot, said the QuEgar had approved of the appointment of Sir David Chalmers. late Chief Justice of British Guiana and foreterly Chief Justice of the Gold Coast, as Commissioner to enquire into the circumstances connected 1 with the insurrection in the Sierra Leone Protectorate and ' generally into the state of affairs In the colony. The instruct- Mons given to him were lengthy, and perhaps it would be more convenient if they were laid upon the table.—Mr. CURZON, answering Mr. R. G. Wehster, said the negotiations which had for some time been proceeding with the Chinede Government for an extervion of the boundaries of Hong Kong resulted in a Convention which was signed at Pekin on June 9. The area which is leased to Great Britain for ninety-nine years includes the waters of Mire Bay and Deep Bay, the peninsula and promon- tory of Kowloon, and the islan 1 of Lantau.— Sir J. Gent. in moving the second reading of the London University Commission Bill, explained that the effect of the measure would be to secure the appointment of a body of Commissioners with power to make statutes and regulations to enable the University to combine teaching and examining functions, Mr, G. HAUWOOD moved the rejection of the bill, but after a long debate he withdrew his amendment. The bill was then read a second time, and referred to the Grand Committee on Law. HOUSE OF COMMONS.—Werresapt.Y. In the House of Commons 011 Wednesday the Nonconform- ist Marriages (Attendance of Registrars) Bill was considered in Committee. Aci amendment, moved by the Attorney General, to clause 6, providing that no'person should officiate at or solemnise a marriage in any registered building unless certified as duly authorised by the trustees or governing body, and unless his name had been previously communi- cated to the Registrar General and to the superintendent registrar of the district, was agreed to. After some further amendments had been made, the Bill was passed through Committee and was ordered to be reperted to the House— The Pauper Children (Ireland) Bill, the object of which is to extend the system of hoarding-out peuper children to Ireland, also passed through Committee and was read a third time.— The House then went into Committee on the Common Employment Abolition Bill. Mr. RENSILSW propose' an amendment allowing " contracting out." After some debate the amendment was rejected by 134 votes to 104. dome further amendments having been agreed to, Sir A. B. FOR- WOOD moved to insert a new clause to provi to that the expression " employer " in the Act should include a body of persons corporate or unincorporate, and the expression " workman" meant a railway servatat and anyiperson to 1 whom the Employers and Workmen Act, 1875, applied. ' This was rejected by 184 votss to 47. The Bill WM then " talked out" by Mr. TONILINSOtt, Amongst the Bilis with- 'drawn were tile PerliernentAry Yrinchile (Women) Bill and the Parliamentary Pr snchise (liztension t. 4 '.lsn4tes) Bill. In a Bill that came before the Police and Sanitary Com- mittee of the Houle of Commens the Corporation of Liver- pool sought for power to "Mamie boys under 14 and girls un- der 16 who engage in etre t trading, to send unlicensed chil- dren who traded to an industrial school or to the care of some fit person, to provide municipal lodgings for the licensed children, and to have inspectors, if necessary, to carry out the Act. Some interesting evidence at to the ex- traordinary state or affairs which prevails in Liverpool was given by Captain NoTr.Bowita. the Chief !Constable. The committee agreed to pass the clause subject to certain moils illations. By those the Corporation most grant a 'iota" to every applicant who fulfils regulations drawn up by the Cor- poration and approved by the Home Secretary. TheV Must appoint inspectors to look after the welfare of the children. but before any licence is issued or by-laws made the Corpora- tion must arrange for and provide suitable lodgings for tha children who may be licensed._</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>GLA.DNESg COMES</head>
        <p>GLA.DNESg COMES</p>
        <p>With a better understanding of the transient nal lira of the many phcsical ills which vanish before prooer efforts—gentle eff nts, pleasant eff irts—rightly directed. There is comfort in the knowledge that so many forms of sickness are not due to any actual disease, but simply to a constipated condition of the system, which the pleasant family laxative—California Syrup of Figs— promptly removes. That is why it is the only remedy with millions of families, and it is everywhere esteemed so highly by those who value good health. Its bene- ficial effects aro due to the fact that it is the one remedy which promotes inters! cloanlinnsi without debilitating the organs on wilier' it acts. If in the enjoyment of good health and the system is regular, then laxatives and other remedies are not neded. If afflicted with any actual disease, one may be commended to the most •,kilful physicians; but if in need of a laxative, then one should have the 13Pst. and with tho well•informed every- where California Syrup of Figs stands highest, is most largely used. and .gives most general satisfaction. The title Syrup of Figs has heea freely aparopiated by unscrunnlnus imitators ; to Tover.tt disappointment voit must insist anon having Calzfornta Syrup of Figifs (ask for it in full), manufactured by the Califortita Fie Syrup Company. Of chemists, is. li. and new LA.RGE SIZE Is. 91.</p>
        <p>_ E xor ISM PATENTS .--The tOIIOW pants nngst others were applied for, and publishsd in the Official Journal of Patents, during the week ending June 4th, 1898: Nos. 12,208,F. El. Royce and E. A. Claremont, of Manchester for" improvements in brakes and clutches for crane boisrs and lifting machinery generally," 31st May, 1898 ; 12,306, J. J. Smithier, for "Improvements in apparatus for stipplying copper bottoms with paste," ISt June, 1898;12,381,A es and Company.Limited,and J. Clegg, for"lmproverdents in means for weighting the front top rollers of ring and throstle spinning frames " 2nd June ; 12,400, R. J. C. Mitchell, Waterfoot, for "An improvement in or relating to rollers to be used for wringing. scouring, washing, and other purposes," 2nd June; 12,450, Frderick Flnywood, Manchester, for " An improvement in or relating to means for securing the lids or covers of cardboard or similar boxes," 3rd June ; 12,466, A. Ashworth, and J. Burger, of Bury, for " Irn- provernents in the production of colouring matters."3rd June; 12,547, J. E. Thornton, and J. P. Lea, for " Im- provements in fluid pressure engines or motors," June 4th, 1896.—List supplied by George Davies, C. E., and Son, Chartered Patent Agents, 4, St. Ann's Square, Man- chester.</p>
        <p>THE LITE MR. THOMAS CROSFIELD Oir LIVERPOaL. With the" rinancial Reformer" fo.i this month there is given an excellent lithograph portrait of Mr. Croefield, and in the journal itself there is an interesting memoir of the deceased gentleman, in which it is said that " Mr, Crosfield was a good example of that class of active, yet unobtrusive citizens who are found in every part of the land, and who have contributed so much to make British political, social, and commercial life what it is. We , have, as a nation, been mourning the loss of Mr. Gladstone, yet even his talents and abilities would not have been able to accomplish those reforms that have conferred such a benefit upon the nation were it not that there are in every locality men like Thomas Crosfield fighting fearlessly for treedom and inatine.',</p>
