      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>GENERAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>GENERAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Thirty persons are believed to have been killed by the fall of three building,' at Riot, near Cannes. The Empress Rugenie is said to have resumed the writing of her memoirs, on which she was engaged before her Illness. At Sanderlaud on 'Monday, George &amp;Insley was committed for trial for the murder of an ex-policeman named William</p>
        <p>B Ireton. At Dorchester on Saturafty,six sailors of her Majesty's ship Raleigh were sentenced to six months' hard labour for a murderous assault on three policemen. •• - -</p>
        <p>Liquid hydrogen is described as being colourless. Helium having aims been liquefiel by Professor Dewar. no repre- sentative of the permanent gases now remains.</p>
        <p>------- An award of £15.000 was made in the Admiralty Court'on Tuesday for salvage services rendered by the Warren line steamer Roman to the French mail steamer La Champagne. At Guildford ABSIZ9S on Monday, two Italian organ " grinders," charged with tha murder by stabbing an Eng- lishman, were found guilty of manslaughter. Sentence was postponed. On Monday evening a by named Harry Williamson, re- siding In Dryden-street. Bootle. whit +t bathing on the shore at Seiforth. in company with a number of other boys. was carried out of his depth and drowned. At Dublin ma Tuesday the Lord Chief Justice sentenced William Meates and John Barrett to pay fines of £25 and £2O respectively for frauds in connection with the lodger fran- chise claims for the St. Stephen's Green division. A destructive fi-e occurred on Saturday afternoon at the sawmills of Messrs. Joseph Rawli 'son and Sons. Wellington street, Oarstoo. Toe mill was almost completely destroyed damage to the amount of about £lO,OOO being caused.</p>
        <p>The bodies of the two men drowned in the Colwyn Bay I boating accident were recovered on Tuesday, that of T. Morris, of Manchester. being found near the Rhos pier' and that of H Morgan, of Rhos, one of the rescue party, atLlan dudne. The plague at Hong Kong appears now to be rapidly decreasing. According to a telegram received at the Colonial OfiSce there were only twenty new cases during the past Week, and the deaths from plague during the same p3riod numbered twenty-one. At Plassey, near Limerick. on Saturday evening, a young man named Thomas Cooney. aged 19, went to bathe in the river Shannon with several others. He was carried away by the tide and drowned, notwithstanding the efforts made by his companions to rescue Lim, At Guildford Assizes on Saturday. Alice Trigg. 20, single was acquitted of the charge of murdering her infant, A child was found embelded in the mud f a ditch, and the defence was that there was no pro A that it was Trigg who was seen near the spot, or that the deceased was her child.</p>
        <p>At Huddersfield on Tuesday John Potts Dryden, black smith's assistant, was committed for trial at the assizes, chroved with having attempted to drown two of his children in the canal at Low Westwood. Golcar, on Saturday last,and with having at the same time attempted to commit suicide. On Saturday night, on Salisbury Plain, near the military camp at Bulford, a man named William Ward, one of the labourers at the Government reservoir, was found dead. The body was much mutilated. Another labourer named George Newman has been arrested on a charge of causing Ward's</p>
        <p>death. The latest boon whioh Lord Wolseley has conferred upon the soldier is that whereby he will in future be rid of the heavy headgear. whether it be helmet, busby, or what not on field days and in hot weather. The full head-dress is only to be worn when the men are paraded in review order or on guard. It is now settled that the marriage of the Princess Dorothea of Saxe-Coburg-Gotha to Duke Ernest Gunther of Schleswig- Moistein-Sonderburg-Augustenburg will take place at Coburg in the month of August, in the Catholic Church of Saint Augustin when the ceremony will be conducted as quietly as possible. Lord C. Beresford has written a letter on the subject of our naval defences, in which he asserts that Great Britain is falling behind in the raze with France and Russia. He states that he has not been so alarmed about the state of the navy since 1883. and that a supplementary estimate is an absolute necessity. The Lord Mayor of London, sitting at the Mansion House on Tuesday. said that, so far as the Mansion House was con- cerned, the report of an application against Mr. Chamberlain for contravening the Wild Birds Act by partaking of lark pudding at the Cheshire Cheese with Mr. Asquith was an absolute fabrication. The body of a gentleman was found on the railway at Heston Chapel, near Stockport, on Wednesday. the heal hav- ing been severed. Letters in the packets led to his indenti- fiestion as James Boyle, whose father WAS for many years manager of the Stockport Branch of the Manchester and Liverpool District Bank. On Tuesday, William North. solicitor, of Pudsey. near Leeds, was struck off the rolls by Justices Day and Law- rence, forming a Divisional Court of the Queen's Bench, for having misappropriated a sum of £3. entrusted to him by a client to pay into the Brad'ord Bank, in liquidation of a debt owing by his client to the bank. Mr. Joseph Charles Woodin, a member of the firm or Turner ana Co.. commission. merchants. Liverpool. and local agent, for the Royal Niger Company, died on Wednesday morning in Chester Infirmary from injuries sustained on Tuesday night by prematurely alighting from the train by which he w,s travelling frorn Birkenhead to his residenoe at Heyfield, Little Sutton, near Ledsham. It is understood that Mr. Woolf Joel has left personalty to the amount of £.4,030.0)0 sterna e., including his interest in going concerns. His widow is to teceive £53 a week in arldi- -1 tion to the present proaevls of property and shares to a very large amount sett ed on her sort directly after his birth. , This eon and B. Barnato's children are each to have 2150,000. Three men were terribly injured at Newcastle. county , Down, on Tuesday by the accidental ex p'osion of a charge l during blasting operations in connection with the new water- works extension for Belfast city. One man named Charles had a leg blown cff, besides sustaining other shocking in- juries, while the other two. named Alige3 and Sharkey, ars likely to 1410 tWr eyesight, The Admiralty hate sold by auction at long Kong the old battleship Victor Emmanuel. She fetched 4 ' 4,000d015. and was noualit by a Chinaman. The Victor Emmanuel was launched at Pembroke in 1835 as a screw wooden battleship of ninety-one guns, with a displacement of 5,157 tons, She we, the receiving ship at Hong Kong, where she has been superseded by the Tamar. At Wolverhampton on We Inesday William Sedgwick. pro- prietor of Sedgwick's menagerie, was fined £5 and costs for cruelty to a small Chinese hairless dog: The defend ant kept the dog for exhibition purposes in a eye with two monkeys, and the latter, whenever biscuits ware thrown into the cage. scratched and bit the dog in a moat ferocious manner. Its body was covered with bites and scratches. On Sunday morning Harte's Amphitheatre, Dewsbury, which is used as a music hall, was completely gutted by fire. The alarm was given et a quarter past one. The efforts of the firemen to save any portion of the building were fruitless. The damage to the building is estimated at over £2OlO Seven alligators and seven snakes, valued at £.1.57, ths property of Madame Paula, were boiled alive. The Vienna correspondent of the '. Daily Mail" tele- graphs :—A journal published here states that Count Badenl the ex-Premier. his gone mad, his bealn having been affected. by the worries he endured while attempting to govern this turbulent empire. It is further stated that his madness takes the most curious &amp;sem ; he is continually offering his services to other European sovereigns as public hangman I Foot-weariness, invariably experienced by standing workers, has recently been investigated with the result that, relief is aseertained to be obtainable if the standing place Is made ever so slightly springy, so that when weight is shifted from one leg to the other, a modified corresponding motion takes place in the floor itself. The discovery originated in someone observing that better work was done by a set of henchmen in a wood-fioored shop than by another set in a concrete floored shop. In the Queen's Bench on Tuesday, Mr. John Thomas, engineer, sued the proprietors of the .. City Press " for alleged libel contained in the reoort of some police court pro- ceedings In which plaintiff was charged with obtaining £l5 ,by false pretenses. It WAS said that defendants published I counsel's opening statement without giving rebutting evi - I dence. which entirely exonerated plaintiff, against whom the charge was dismiseed. the latter fact being duly reported. The , jury awarded plaintiff £350. The Parsons turbine motor is thought so highly of by the Admiralty authorities that two vessels have been ordered to , he built on similar lines to the Turbinia. A speed of 35 knots l an hour is guaranteed. Lord Kelvin has expressed an opinion that reciprocating engines are likely to be superseded by steam turbines. Evidence given in support of Mr. Parsons' application for an extended patent, the inventor had experi- enced was granted in April, shows that, up to that time, a loss of more than £l,lOO over the matter. A sensational incident occurred on Wedeesday night in front of the German Embassy in London. The doarkeener had called a conveyance, and the First Secretary Count Arco-Valley, was about to step into it when a min Who was unknown to him fired two shots In rapid succession. Both bullets struck the Count, but happily neither inflicted much Injury. A constable on point duty WAS attracted to the place. and the Count's assailant fired upon him also. For.. tunately, however, the progress of the bullet was stopped by a notebook in the policeman's breast pocket, and he escaped Injury. By the' collapse of a building 200 years old at Blot, in the South of France on Monday, 27 persons lost their lives, amongst them being a whole family of 12 persons, whilst three others were severely injured. The calamity was of a heartrending character. A gathering was being held in the house of M. Pellegrin, one of whose daughters had celebrated her first communion on the Sunday. and some of the best- known local families had assembled. Suddenly the house, which was a five storey building, collapsed, burying the whole of those gathered in it under the ruins. eEllarbour defence In war time, according to the latest American plan, is to be carried nut by stretching bare metal cables across the mouths of rivers or similar coast openinae. The oables are to be charged with electricity at enormously high voltage, and any iron ship coming into contact with them would assuredly be blown up by sparks produced at Innumerable points where short circuiting would be set up. The severance of the cable by a shot would not be an easy matter for even the best guener ; in fact, alter dusk the position of the cable would be difficult to detect at all. A recent epidemic of diphtheria among the school children in a large city led to an investigation of its causes, with the remarkable conclusion that the disease was communicated from one child to another by the pencils and penholders, which were gathered together at the close of school and given out again next day, being used first by one child and then another. The worse feature in connection with this indis- criminate use and handling of the Same article, is in the habit which almost every chili has— though it is not confined by any meant to children—of patine Isere or pencil in the mouth, either through want of something better to do or as a matter of convenience. A far-reaching sea beacon light has recently been tested at the C.S. lighthouse depot. Staten Island, New York. It is fitted with two lenses, each of 911 diameter, set at opposite sides of the lantern. Between the lenses is an electric are light of 9,003 candle-power. Each tense is made up of 190 segments arranged to throw the beam of light out as a column of parallel rays so brilliant that the beacon would be theoretically visible in clear atmosphere at a distance of nearly 150 miles. The lantern, Which wears several tons, has a hemispherical lower end, and this rests in a reoettacle of mercury, floating partly submerged upon its surface. A very easy bearing is in this way provided, and the clock work is employed to give six turns to the lantern per minute, thus making a gash every five seconds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>MINERAL WATER MA:NITFACEIJRERS AND THEIR BOTTLES.</head>
        <p>MINERAL WATER MA:NITFACEIJRERS AND THEIR BOTTLES.</p>
        <p>1 ImpoRrANT PROSECUTION AT WARRINGTON. , At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Monday I before Mr. F. Monks (in the chair), Alderman Harrieon Councillors Shaw Green and Smethurst,and Messrs. Bir- chall and Wright. James Wilkinson, mineral water manufacturer, of Longford-street, Warrington, was summoned at the instance of the Liverpool and District Mineral Water Association, for having on the 25th of Mw,' sold two bottles of ginger beer bearing the trade mark ot Messrs. Fairhurst, Greenough and Co., contrary to toe provisions of the Merchandise Marks Act, 287. He was also charged with using bottles belonging to Messrs.Eidowes and Co.,and Messrs.M.Walker and Son, both of Warrington.—Defendant pleaded not guilty. Mr. Edwin Berry, solicitor, of Liverpool, said be appeared to prosecute on behalf of the Liverpool and District Mineral Water Association. The defendant was a mineral water manufacturer carrying on business in Warrington, and he was charged with three different offences on the same date. The facts of the case were exceedingly simple. One of the (objects of the Associa- tion in Liverpool was to proi.eet 'manufacturers of mineral waters, and the defendant was for some time a member of the association. He mentioned that to show that he was perfectly well aware of the rules. It was well known that a large number of the bottles sent out to mineral water manufacturers were not returned. That had occasioned a great loss to the manufacturers, and some few years ago what was called a bottle exchange was established in Liverpool, the idea being that when one manufacturer got bottles belonging to another man he returned them and was allowed a certain small sum per dozen. On the other hand he was entitled to take away any bottles belonging to himself. On the 25th of last month Mr. Bestow. the inspector of the association, visited two people who sold mineral waters in Warrington. The first was Elizebeth Forster, from whom he procured two bottles of ginger beer. The bottles bore the name and trade mark of Messes. Fair- hurst, Greenough and Co., and Mrs. Forster said she had obtained them from the defendant. The inspector then went to a shop occupied by Mary Coultbard, and obtained from her two lemonade bottles with the name and trade mark of Messrs. E Idowes and Co., and two gingerbeer bottles bearing the name and trade mark of Me srs. M. Walker and Son. These bottles had also been supplied by the defendant. He had been systematically using thee° bottles instead of supplying his own, and by that means was saving a sum of money. It was well known that some hundreds of pounds were lost every year by manufacturers through the non-return of bottles. He would prove that the bottles themselves were not sold, and were not even charged for. It had been found abso- lutely necessary to adopt some such course, otherwise , such prosecution as the present one could not takeplace. The bottles were not sold to anyone. He apprehended that there was no doubt about the law on the subject. Fred Bestow, inspector of the Liverpool and District Mineral Water Association, gave evidence with reference to the purchase of the ginger beer and lemonade. He said he afterwards called upon the defendant. He saw him loading a cart, and with his permission examined the bottles on the vehicle. He afterwards had a conver- sation with defendant, and charged him with filling other people's bottles. He said he could not help it ;he had cautioned his workpeopte about it. —Defendant : Did not I tell you distinctly that I had told my people not to fill other people's bottles ?—The Inspector : I say you did.—The bottles belonging to Messrs. Fairhurst, Greenough and Co. and Messrs. Eldowes and Co. were produced in court and identified. There was no repre- sentative present from the firm of Messrs.M.Walker and Son, and Mr. Berry withdrew that cherge. Mr. Barry said this prosecution had been institu`ed more for the principle of the thing than for anything I else. Speaking in defence of himself the defendant said for ' weeks and weeks he had given his workpeople to under- stand they they must not fill other people's bottles. Two months age he happened to meet with an accident, and he had never been himself since. It had never been his wish to fill other people's bottles. He had thousands of bottles of hie own. It had been a mistake, and a great mistake and he was very sorry that such a thing had happened. Through reverses and misfortune his place was in liquidation. He was not his own master. The Chairman : That is not a question for us. IY,fendant ; I hope you will be as lenient as possible. I am a po,:r man through ot'ser people's dishonesty. The Chairman said defendant had been a member of the association, and knew the rules. He knew he had no right to fill the b)ttles with his ginger beer and sell them. The Bench were quite satisfied that he had com- mitted an offence against the Act, and would fine him 20s. and costs in each case, or in default of perment he would have to serve a month's imprisonment. Defendant said he could not pay the flue.</p>
