      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>WARRINGTON COUNTY COIJItT.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY COIJItT.</p>
        <p>THURSDLY.—Bgore Honour Judge Wynne Pfoulke3.</p>
        <p>TELE CLLIII Br A RANNRUPT'S Tonsvoc.—Joseph Scott, Liverool, trustee of the eatate of Joseph Matthews, builder, formerly of Warrington, bankrupt. claina.d from Samuel eta&amp; trading as the Manchester Furnishing Company at £°.2.kily-Street. Warrington, and other towns, the stun of 430 for timber sold.—The defendant presented a countermialan of £3O damages for breach of contract for nou-delievery of the the plaintiff, an Mr. A. tinaher. -Mr. F,nstriyf ropment.d Brown&amp; LID heariuSO !IV t2l, vs fta journed from the court iie... a montn w,hlt evidence was given by a slumber of p.lalsi7.!" witnesses.—Mr. Forshaw now called Robert Clarke, a sawyer In the employ of Messrs. Clare and Ridgway and formally employed by Mr. Matthews who said he was present when defendant bought the timber. He sawed the whole of the timber, and het ..ed to load ft.—Caves- examined : The whole of the timber Lought by Clan was deliatered- Arthur Peseock. coal merchant and contractor, said he delivered the timber to Claff I. Ile was present when , Mr. Ciaff offered Mr. Matthews a cheque for £l5. Hr. Mat- thews wanted £2O to pay a creditor and he refused the £l5 Cheque. Cross-examined : He was a creditor of the plaintiff He had never threatened Sir. Claff —For the defence, Mr. Browne said his client admitted that he bought the timber but contended that it had not all been delivered. The counter claim was in rega-d to his client having to go to Liverpool, , and buy other timber—Samuel Chaff, cabinet maker and furniture dealer, carrying on business in Saukey-strwat said Matthews asked him to buy some timber, and he agreed, and , went to the yard the following day. Only a small quantity lof the timber he bought was delivered. He had consequently to boy timber elsewhere.—Abrahana Rosenberg, the defendant's manager, and Isaac: Cohen, foreman cabinet maker, also gave evidence. —His Honour said the case was a very unsatifactory one to decide. Any decision would be unsatisfactory to himself and equally so to either party. He had some dmbts whether the whole lot of timber was delivered. There was, however, positive evidence that all the timber in the yard was delivered. The business was conducted in the loosest possiole way on both sides, and of the two, considering his expe-ience, the defendant was to blame. These must have been wilful untruthfulness on one side or the other. Judg- ment would be given for the plaintiff for the amount claimed. and there would also be a verdict for him on the counter- claim. If any injustice were done in the matter it was the defendant's fault.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>CARD.</head>
