        <p>TILE EXAMINEE,</p>
        <p>JUNE 17th, 1893.</p>
        <p>__- SUMMER is once more with us, in The Holiday Season. its glory, and along with it comes a firmer conviction that no other European country has the provision for travelling, .for its own sake, been reduced to 1/11ch an art as it has with ourselves. And, as the .atolouncements on many hoardings show, that art includes within its broad domain the long-day ttiPper,and the tourist of the globe. Side by side with the long-day trip has grown the three days', seven days', ten days' excursion ; and the circular to°ll7 ; and the Continental tour; and a far-east sur and the tour round the world; and as soon a „„science can guarantee, with any certainty, a return, the public may expect tours to either ole or a continuation tour embracing both. Whenever that offer is made the untiring Englishman may be safely counted upon to aet out with a light heart on the journey. llensure with him is a secondary consideration. Ihe same racial temperament which enables him to eladare eight to twelve hours in a third-class rail- /74Y carriage,with all the discomforts,succeeded by the burly-burly of a greatly overcrowded watering Place, merely for the sake of saying "I did it,' carries the less restricted tourist through half the</p>
        <p>capitals of Europe in an incredibly short space ok time, or drives him over the desert on a camel, or to the topmost heights of the Himalayas or the Andes. Travel he must ; space is no object. He is bound only by considerations of time and the wherewithal to pay his fare and maintair himself by the way. Anyhow, he must be content only to get over as many miles within the twenty-four hours as the most liberal of railway companies will carry him. When happier fortune shines he speeds krther afield. Land and ocean, hill and dale, nlountain and prairie, are all the same to him ; travel he will, incited, rather than discouraged, by the discomforts and toil of the journey. It:certainly cannot be truthf ally alleged that this characteristic of our race is a now one. Seven centuries ago all who could afford it started off ler the Holy Land. They called themselves erusaders ; but nowadays they would be regarded 4,3 nothing mare than the long-distance tourists 'er whom the railway companies so abundantly .ter, When those State-aided tours died out the conscientious Englishman, not to be baulked in Ins miming for travel, set out on his own account a;ith "cockle hat and staff," and als3 " sandal and Fleoon," calling himself a Pilgrim. But what was there in a name? He was, after all, but the globe-trotter" of his day and generaticn. When crusades and pilgrimages failed there was always a chance of joining in some adventurous ,War with Franco, and as the years went on and 40ve of travel took a more seafaring turn, con- tinuous excursions from East to West came in by which the roving life was stimulated. assuredly the good QUEEN 8E94 was a typical English-woman, and truly her fatuous Royal " progresses " were but Icing-distance excursions under another style, elaborated as became her majestic notions, l'et following out a national love of travelling to and fro. Then, a century or two later, as those 'Who are versed in writings of FIELDING or Sssor.- EeTT will know the great delight of our great gtandfathers and our great grandmothers was Journeying about the country, the ladies in their 111111bering coaches, and the gentlemen on their tail-docked, surefooted, an not very handsome ilteeds. It is admitted that in those days the long distance trip was reserved,f or "the quality;" but the explanation lies on the surface. It is the 3-ilvvay train that has,f or the masses of the people, Illade much travelling possible; but the inherent Q7'3 of travel awaited only the advent of the lass carriage and the cheap trip ticket. It Ilea been a case, not so much of evolution i's of adaptability to circumstance and from this point tie nothing is more natural than that the !akee which has produced the greatest explorers, the greatest colonists, the greatest adventurers,on and land, should also produce, what to the : adering foreigner must seem the phenomenon the long distance, and more especially, the (alg-day, English tripper.</p>
        <p>%r A. B. Forwood, M.P., has a very poor opin- ac)a of the British seaman, the degenerate descen- of the "Jolly Jack Tar" of ancient renown. 14 answer to a correspondent, who had pointed _°l.lt the mixed nature of the crews of British 'aatchant vessels, Sir Arthur says" From my own. 113:1'sonal experience of the average modern British ,llor, lam not surprised at owners preferring of other nationalities. Drunkenness, insub- -0 pttlination, desertion, or neglect to fulfil their en- tage,taents, are the characteristics of the so-called tltish sailor at too many of our ports." What a Charge These " characteristics" are not those ta.a. have for centuries so fondly associated with our tull°ta, and most of us would be glad to find evi- . ece.rebutting Sir. A. B. Forward's sweeping do- , a_arat.ion. Whether our merchant sailors have t'ater.iorated quite to the extent he indicates or ,}(,),!, it is a fact which does not admit of dispute, t,e7t. they are not the best men available for the Ilea in which they are engaged. And to have , admit this is arather bitter pill, when we have looked upon the British sailor as the sailor 'pence. Such a stigma on our maritime badly wants removing, and it will be a - much congratulation if iu future years</p>
        <p>to 80</p>
        <p>I)ar</p>
        <p>e.reellence,</p>
        <p>4upretnacy</p>
        <p>ilitter</p>
        <p>the younger generation of British sailors can raise , the tone of the mercantile marine to its once high standard.</p>
        <p>Volunteers, after their recent unpleasant ex- periences under canvas, will be interested in the question put before Parliament on Tuesday. Mr. Mellor, M.P., asked the Under Secretary of State for War if he was aware of the defective condition of the tents supplied to the Lancashire and Cheshire Volunteers during their encampment at Conway ; the discomforts arising therefrom; and whether he would take steps to see that the tents should not be issued on any future occasion. Mr. Brodrick's explanation was that tents continued to be issued for use until "worn out," and that these tents in particular, while being in the last stage of wear, were pronounced serviceable before being issued. He gave an assurance that they would not be issued again, which ia in a great measure satisfactory. But it would be much the better course if the War Department could be prevailed upon to alter their rule in this one par- ticular, i.e.' that the tents be not constantly issued for use until worn out. There is a stage, scarcely definable perhaps, which precedes that of being worn-out, and when the health and may-be lives of our volunteers is at stake, an effort should be made to do away altogether with the possibility of such necessaries as bell-tents being issued when quite on their last legs, so to speak. Now that attention has been officially directed to the recent state of matters, the War Department though slow moving, should have a better system in vogue ere the next encampment comes round.</p>
        <p>A very unpleasant disclosure was made at the inquiry into the death of a little boy by drowning in the Canal at Earlestown last week. The evidence revealed the fact that the unfortunate i boy was seen to fall n the water and drown by at least four or five men, one of whom remarked upon his inability to swim. No effort was made to save the little fellow's .life, and we can only hope that these participators in an act of such cold-blooded cowardliness did not bear the honoured title of Englishmen. Even worse does their conclact look in the light of a statement by one witness that to save the child they would only have had to go knee-deep in the water. The Coroner had some severe comment to make on the matter and regretted, as everyone else with an atom of Immanity in them will, that the police were unable to discover the identity of the cowards who so heartlessly. stood by while a little, but valuable life, was tragically terminated. They deserve exposure, and if the "still small voice of conscience" is not active within them they must be more queerly-constituted beings than any which have previously been unearthed.</p>
        <p>Over twelve months have elapsed since the Qeeen's Gardens in Palmyra Square cameinto the possession of the Warrington Corporation, but still they remain closed to the public. After the Town Council approved of the design for laying out the gardens considerable progress was made in the work,but practically nothing has been done during the past few weeks. The flower beds are tastefully laid out, the plants and flowers are thriving wonderfully well, and the grass borders which surround them are neat and attractive. Notwithstanding this the walks are still composed of brick-bats, and of course are unfit for visitors to make use of. During the recent spell of fine weather the gardens would have been an ideal place to spend a few quiet and pleasant minutes, and ratepayers would have had less reason to be- grudge the £7OO to be expended on them. But at the present time there seems to be a total in- difference as to whether the gardens are opened this year or next, and if the present supineness continues they may never be opened at all. It is perhaps just possible that with a little prodding of those in charge of the, work the public may be allowed to sit in the gardens, say about November or December I</p>
        <p>In another column we publish a letter from a cyclist who has apparently recently suffered tor- tures near akin to those of the Middle Ages, inveighing strongly against the condition of the main roads in the Borough. The writer has our full sympathy, and we join heartily with him in remonstrating with the authorities for their lack of action in regard to such a crying evil. This is no new complaint, in fact the state of the roads our correspondent enumerates has been a disgrace to the road-makers of Warrington for years past. We might further point out the rapid deteriora- tion of tho surface of the Arpley New-road, which was, before having the misfortune to pass into the hands of the Warrington Corporation, a highway of surpassing excellence from the point of view of a cyclist. Prompt attention would be effectual in saving this important road from sinking to the level—or absence of it—which distinguishes the other main outlets from the town.</p>
        <p>Those who have read "Through Darkest Africa' by H. M. Stanley will remember the ill-fated rear- guard of the Emin Relief Expedition of which Mr. Bonny was the gallant leader. Mr. Bonny's ill- fortune seems to be following him to the end. The latest report concerning him is that he is dy- ing of consumption in Fulham Workhouse Infirm- ary. A subscription is being raised to secure his removal to a private nursing home. Workhouses have long been asylums for British army and navy veterans, and it now appears that exploration heroes must be added to the list.</p>
        <p>It evident that the St. Helens Corporation does not intend—without a keen struggle—to deal with its fie wage in any other way than at present, which, as is now well known, is practi- cally the simple and inexpensive one of turning it into Sankey Brook with unpleasant results as far as Warrington is concerned. Last week in the Court of Referees at London, the St. Helens Cor- poration pleaded that Warrington had no locus standi in opposing their Bill now before Parlia- ment. Mr. Marshall Stevens, Q.C. (instructed by the Town Clerk of Warrington) very clearly showed that Warrington had, and it is a satisfac- tion to know that the Court dismissed the objec- tion of the St. Helens Corporation. The Bill will , be considered a month hence, and Warrington will then have an opportunity of airing in full their grievance with reference to Sankey Brook, and will ask that the St. Helens Corporation be di- rected to deal with the sewage question befcre they are granted any further powers from Parlia- ment.</p>
        <p>1 t_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>PENKETII.</head>
        <p>PENKETII.</p>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY VicARAGE.—The work of demolishing the old vicarage and erecting a new one is about to be proceeded with. A Faculty for the purpose was granted by the Consistory Court at Liverpool on Tuesday last on the application of Mr. W. D. Jeans,junior, solicitor,War- rington. Mr. P. Silcock, the architect for the new work and Mr.Aitken,the Chairman of the Building Committee, atteude 1 to explain the plans and specification which were approved of. The application for Faculty was made on behalf of the Rev. E. S. Jackson, the vicar,who heads the subscription list with a gift of £5OO.</p>
        <p>For NAME BROOCHES, solid silver name brooches 10d any name (Government stamped. Presents or Articles for yourself, you may visit DENG ars, 10. (111URESH-ST,IERT LIVERPOOL, where you can get hundreds of articles such as Wotehes, Clocks,Jewellery,Electro-Plate, Cut ery, Purses, Pocket Knives, etc., at wholesale prices. See advertisement Illustrated Ohrenlgraph Wateh, Bs. 11d., etc.) on page 7 of this raper.— Ida.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JUNE 17, IS9B. DISASTROUS FIRE AT WORK I NOTES ON GAMES AND</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JUNE 17, IS9B. DISASTROUS FIRE AT WORK I NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>£l,OOO DAMAGE. At five o'clock on Tuesday evening, an alarming fire broke out at Yale View Cottages, Moss-lane, Moore. The property, which belongs to Mr. Gleave of Moore Post Office, was of the old-fashioned stamp and adjoined a brick built house, and at the time of the outbreak four houses were in occupa- tion, whilst one was used as a store room by the owner. An alarm was raised at five o'clock, and although endeavours were made to cope with the outbreak for about half an hour by the residents they failed in their object. As a consequence the Wilderspool brigade were telegraphed for, and although they only received word at 5.40 the brigade, under the superintendence of Mr. lrice, with their engine were on the scene of the con- flagration seventeen minutes after having received notice. On arrival little hopes were entertained of saving the cottages, which were almost gutted, and attention was directed to the adjoin- ing property. Another house, but of a more valuable class, had meanwhile eaught fire, and the efforts of the brigade were required to save this house from total destruetion Part of the roof was destroyed,and two rooms more or less damaged. Meanwhile tee cottagera had been busy in saving the furniture, &amp;c., and it is satisfac- tory to note that not much loss has been suffered in this respect except in one instance where the occu- pants were so overcome by the occurrence that the whole of the furniture etc. was destroyed, and no- thing whatever was saved with the exception of a few blankets. The cottage property was a total wreck, but the brigade did not leave the scene until after nine o'clock, at which time the fire was totally extinguished. Mr. Weave had been making preparations for re-felting and tarring the outside of the cottage which he used as a store room. A fire had been made in the grate ready for heating some tar which was in the room in a bucket. Some shavings were in the room and also some of the old felting which was in a very dry inflammable condition. Mr. Weave had to heave the cottage. He only expected to be away a minute or two, hut was detained, and atioue tea minutes later his attention was drawn to smoke issuing from the cottage. lie hurried to the spet, and found the cottage filled with smoke and filmes. The probability is that a spark from the fire had set alight some of the inflammable material in the room. Mr. Gleave devoted all his energies to removing the dangerous material in the place, and checking the fire. For a time he succeeded in holding the fire in check hut it began to make headway and spread, and the WildersPoo.l brigade was sent for. The cot- tages were at one time insured but owing to the in- flammable nature of the .structures the company refused to continue the insurance. The loes is es- timated at £l,OOO. Mr. Gleave sustained some severe burns on the face in 1..?9 efforts to extinguish the fire. Much sympathy is felt tor him in his misfor- tune. The homeless cottages have been temporarily housed with neighbours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>RIINOORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RIINOORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>ST. EDWARD'S CATHOLIC MISSION.</p>
        <p>On Sunday, the Rev. Father Cullen, of Freshfield, preached both morning and evening on behalf of the Foreign Missions. Thera was ale,' celebration of the blessed Sacrament with procession in the evening. The congregations were good throughout.</p>
        <p>BETHESDA CONGREGATIONAL The usual meeting of this brotherhood was held on Sunday afternoon and proved:as usual, a bright and en- joyible hour. Mr. D. Bisbrown preside' and in a few appropriate words expressed his best wishes for the con- tinued prosperity of the society—commending its broad and Catholic basis. The Rev. Davies of Carmarthen, stu- dent at Lancashire Independent College gave a useful address on "Habits "and Missiessie Thomas of Widnes sang two solos both of which were greatly enjoyed.</p>
        <p>PATE OF WIDNES PARISH F.C. On Friday evening last, the fate of the Widnes Parish Football Club was deeided at the adjourned meeting of the players and supporters of the club. For some time gest general dissatisfaction hag been expressed owing to the inadequate support the club was receiving, so after due consideration it has now been decided to disband the team altogether. This is a great blow to local fol- lowers of the associatlm game as the games afforded by the parish team who were members of the Liverpool and District Combination were of no mean character. RUNCORN WESLEYAN CYCLE CLUE. On Saturday the club accomplished a most enjoyable run to Beeston Castle,by way of Acten,Weaverham,and Cuddington, and after speeding a few enjoyable hours they returned hy way of Tarvin, Mouldsworth, and Frodsham. The Wednesday run for the above club was to Nortkwich. A goo,' number turned out, and the route taken was througn Comberbaeh, Great Budworth, Losteck to Northwich. Tea was provided by Mrs. Griffiths, and was much enjoyed. After tea the places of interest were visited, including the Park,Hartford and Greenbank Nurseries, and the return journey was through Hartford, Wineington, and Bunton. Home was reached about 9 3'3. after a most enjoyable run. Mr. B. Cooke (captain) was in charge.</p>
        <p>PREFERMENT FOR THE VICAR OF WIDNES. The Vicar of Widnes, the Rev. W. Hodgson, M.A., ha accepted the living of Emmanuel Church, Liverpool. Mr. Hodgson is the son of toe late Rev. W. Hodgson, who was vicar of Christ Church, Coins, Lancashire. for 37 years. He took first-class honours in the 'lt:mingles' School at Merton College, Oxford, and holds the inter- mediate B.A. of London University. After obtaining his degree In 1887, Mr. Hodgson was appointed curate of St. Peter-le-Bailey, Oxford, under the Rev. F. J. Chavasse. He 1890 Mr. Hodgson received from Canon Robson, vicar of Christ Churn. Ciaughton, the appoint- ment to the charge of St. Michael's Church, (Naughton, where he laboured until 1893. gaining for himself the high esteem of the cong.regation. He subsequently suc- ceeded the Rev. G. H. Garrett, 8.D., to toe living of Widnes, and he has worked with untiring zeal to pro- mote the well-being of his parishioners. The vicarage of Emmanuel, according to the Clergy List, is in the hands of Mrs. Anderson. The population is 7,958, and there is church accommolation for 1,000. The value is given as £BOO. The population of the parish of Widnes is 18,000, there is church accommod stiou for 950, and the gross value is £290. It is expected that Mr. Hodg- son will take up his duties at Emmanuel Church very shortly.</p>
        <p>WIDNES TOWN COIINCIL.--.REDDCTION TUE PRICE Or Ges, .</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of Widnes Town Ca,mei was held on Tuesday, the Ildayor (Alderman T. W. Ware- ing) presiding. The arplication of the Widnes Foundry Company with reference to the proposed construction of a level crossing at Lugsdale-road was refused alter al sharp ffiscussion.—Aldermau H. S. Timm's, in moving the adoption of the Gas and Water Committee's minutes, said that for the past year there had been a net profit on the gas account of £2,072 143 51, and he was in a position to put before them a proposition which would be sufficient to make the gas committees of other towns green with envy and breik the heart of all the gas managers in the ooontr7. From the Ist pr c. the price of gas to all consumers within the limits of supply of the Corporation would be reduced by 21 per 1,000 cubic feet of gas, which was equal to a charge of 13 41 and is 6l per 1,000 cubic fel, a charge which could not be approached in point of cheapness by any other town in the country. The quantity of gas supplied by prepayment meters would be increased from 33ft. to 351 t. per id. The reduction in the price would mean an annual saving of £15,000 to toe consumers in the borough.—Councillor Shores said that he found from a small calculation that he had made that the concession was tantamount t 3 a present of 31 to every small con• sumer.—The minutes were adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[EY A. CORRESP3NDENIj.</p>
        <p>Considerable speculation is rife as to the success or non-success of the new venture proposed by those responsible for the safe conduct of the annual sports in connection with the Runcorn Football Club, and the opinion is being expressed by many people that the extension to seven miles radius for the local events will not bear the fruit it is expected to, as it is considered that for every foreign entry they will lose two native ones, and consequently local interest will abate. I myself, think that to make the competition a little keener will be a good thing.</p>
        <p>It is gratifying to note the amount of enthusiasm infused into the customary march out by the Run- corn Volunteers on Friday evening last, when there was a record attendance, under the comman,l of Captain G. Ashton, and Lieutenant Morris, The boys' brigades from the Parish Church, Camden, holy Trinity Church, Brunswick and Halton also took part in the manoeuvres and fully deserved the high compliment paid to them by Captain 0, Ash- ton, at the close of the drill.</p>
        <p>It is not always my task to point out the things that require improvement—though it has of late been often necessary to do so—for occasionally there is a nuirked improvement in something or other to record ;or if not exactly an improvement perchance it is a step in that direction. The event that it gives me the great pleasure of recoruing this week is that which is being carried out on the Runcorn Rugby Football ground. As is well-known the side of the field of play next the grand stand has for years been of a decidedly swampy charac- ter. This is being remedied, and the first of the work will be apparent enough when the season opens again; though perhaps some of the "old 'uns " will be sorry to have no more mud in which to seat an opponent.</p>
        <p>Tbe continuation of the beautiful weather which has been experienced this week has made a marked improvement in the agricultural and horti- cultural aspect of the. district and crops on all sides are beginning to assume far more promising aspects than they did at the commencement of May, or indeed at any time dur- ing that unusually blooming month. The advent of the reaper has alsc.. to be recorded, and already hay-making operations are being conducted in many of the fields and meadows in the neighbour- hood.</p>
        <p>Looking backwards vve see on record the fact that at one time Runcorn was a pretty little water- side village with a beach el no mean pretentions. Then in the march of progress,there came the Ship Canal and gradually the beach was lost sight of, and Runcornians began and still continue to go elsewhere for their day at the seaside. Taking a stroll in the direction of the old ferry one can still see hundreds of children playing upon the beach, enjoying themselves without let or hindrance. With just a little attention this small piece of beach might be considerably improved, and facilities erected for a safe bathing place, for that motley, noisy crew,of laughing gleeful youngsters,who now hold undisputed sway upon what remains of the shore of Runcorn Bay.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>BRUNSWICK SCHOOL AND THE LATE RIGHT HON. W. E. GLADSTONE.</head>
        <p>BRUNSWICK SCHOOL AND THE LATE RIGHT HON. W. E. GLADSTONE.</p>
        <p>The following correspondence has been received by us for publication,as having passed between the Brunswick Wesleyan School,and the Right Hon. Herbert Gladstone, M.P., upon the occasion of the famous statesmae's death : Runcorn, May 23rd, 1598. Herbert Gladstone, Esq-, M.P., Hawarden, Btu Sir,—Brunswick Wesleyan Sun- day School.—l am dc lired by the memt &gt;rs of the Young Men's Class in connection with the above Sunday School to convey to Mrs. Gladstone and family tbe expression of their profound sorrow and heartfelt sympathy in the great loss which the nation has sustained in the death of the greatest Christian statesman of the 19th century.— Yours sincerely, Alfred Riley, Secretary. Hawarden Castle, Chester. June 9th, 1898. Dear Sir,—On behalf of my mother and the family, I beg to thank the Brunswick Wesleyan Sunday School for their very kind message of sympathy.—l am, very faithfully yours, W. H. Gladstone. A, Riley, Esq.</p>
        <p>CYCLISTS USFI PILLAR BRAND. Lubricating and Burning Oils and Cycle Enamel, Enamellette.and Brunswick Black. From all Dealers. AGENTS : AICIAAN AND CO., 13, SEEL-STREET, LIVERPOOL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>DETERMINED SUICIDE OF AN OLD MAN AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>DETERMINED SUICIDE OF AN OLD MAN AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A SAD STORY OF TROL BLE. On Sunday morning a.great sensation was caused by the discovery of a man hanging from aanbleammaidne th: kitchen of a house in Cavendish-street, and upon inves- tigation the facts disclose a very sad stortnyoronfintgroaubbolue.t It appears that a man named Robert Gale, lodging larriddgniin:;, 3155r00,,Ccalsaovicieknnndooitstil:innsgtursewtaais gttiolhitenua:faootrtnlhim Sundaym e about the house for a little while, than all became (Filet and it was supposed h! had left the house. Upon drs, Ann Reid, his landlady, coming down-stairs, however,at auxat 7 o'clock, she di covered Gale hanging by a rope from the kitchen roof. Mrs. Reid called for help and a fellow lodger of Gale's came in at once and cut him down. The Police were El nnarmed and P.O. Gregory on examining the body found life extinnt. Gtie has not been in regular work for some time, and was heard to ' expresi to his landlady that if he could not find work, they would find his body in the canal one of those Idays. Oa Tuesday last he was further depressed after a I visit to his wife, who is confined in the Chester Asyltirn, and for the last day or two has been drinkin-, heav'lv ' and no doubt com.nitte I his nets act whilst CI a state' oi mental depression. TilE INQUEST. _ _ _</p>
        <p>On Tuesday raceway. Mr. J. E. Wonder, held an in. quest on the body of Robert G at th Court Hou,?e, Runcorn, before a jury of which Mr. John Dutton was the foreman. o Mrs. Ann Ried, said she resided at 39,Cavendish-street se Runcorn, and knew the deceased very wen. He was about 63 years of age, and lodged with her for the last three years. Oa Sunday morning he g,-) up about five nut when she came downstairs about (at theli little le Asylum)st ra ne n rl seven o'clock she found the rleceases hir,ging from a ao'ncylothckinagn i unusual,l it tlie fire. They in the hou did not notice hook in the roof. She ealled to Dennis, another lodger and he came and cut him down. Gale had been drink- ing of late, ever since Whitsuntide, but as a ru!e was not, drinker. he alsosee Whit-Tuesday upset him very much, and he rernark6d he would soon j3in her there. His wife hsd been in the his sways at ri all;is going and to Asvlum ever since he CCM to lodge with witness.—By the foreman : Did he .not go to see a doctor in Liver- pool the other day.—Witness : No .sir.—ad he carry the impression that his :wife thought it was through him that she was confined in tne Asylum ?—Witness : I have heard it said so.. Ge3rge Dennis, said he lodged with the last witnesr, who called him on Sunday morning and on going down- stairs he found Gale hanging in the manner described and cat him down. He was quite dead but not quite cold, and had evidently hien hanging for two hours. Be knew Gale very well but deceased had never com- plained of his troubles to him. The police were sent for, and P.C. Gregory was soon in attendance. Mrs. Emma 13 woe% 39, Cavendish-3troet, wife of Mr. ja,s.Basnett,gavo evidence oca conversation she had with deceased after his visit to his wtfe in Chester Asylum. He complainad to witness that sho " never asked after his wife." She replied she was sorry,begged his pardon, i and then asked " Bow s She ?" Be answered " very much worse, indeed." And although Gale appeared strange,at the tima she did not think there was anything to be alarmed at. George Wainwright, foreman, at Wigg's Work?, Run- corn, said during the last week or so he obvrved that the deceased was quite melancholy and depressed, but he managed his work all right. The Coroner, in summing up said it was a very sad case, indeed. Here was .a rnan who was very fond of his wife who was a lunatic confined in Chester Asylum. On the occasion af a visit there he had been known to say " would soon join her. From the evidence ad- duced it was quite clear that the deceased had met his death by hanging hionself,and from what they had heard there was little doubt but that the deed was done whilst he was in an unsound state of mind. The jury returned a verdict accordingly.</p>
      </div>
