      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>FETE, FAIR, AND SALE OF WORK.</head>
        <p>FETE, FAIR, AND SALE OF WORK.</p>
        <p>Oa Church m rcrhs.of St Wednesday afternoon the three days fete—sad fair, John the Evangelist, was formally opened which i 5 being held at Weston in connection with the Whittaker, Brookfield, Runcorn. The Rev. Canon Wood (vicar) introduced Mrs. Whittaker wheal he said was a mist ardeot church-worker, always de- , sirous to do all that was possible in furthering the work 10f the churches in Ruacora and district, after which she announced the fête and fair open, which was taken as a general signal that the programme of the day should commence. Then followed expositions of Maypole danc- ing, given by a troop of specially trained dancers ; the Linaker party in the school- room. Throughout the day large numbers continued to arrive and by the evening quite gains largeorfa lnivdiconcoursecnognecheerostsf b; action drill exhibition,etc.followed a g pyti o thep a Were present and the fun grew fast and furious. The room a ombians y. which and gay tb ispalpeeaori arak ~ a -stocked with articles both ornamental and useful. Amongst the minor attractions eldstparlel stalls e n were ted we u were sideshows, fortune telling, aonkey riding, shooting galleries, cocoa nut shives, etc, etc., and in a specially was hthe erected tent there was ample accommodation in the way of refreshments. The Weston Church Brass Band dis- coursed sweet music during the afternoon and evening. On Thursday the show was practically repeated, being opnied by Mr. W. Mather, J.P. (chairman of the Cast- ner-Ki-Ilner Alkali Works), and with slight alterations will be given a third time on Saturday. work</p>
        <p>RUNCORN " A" v. RUNCORN JUNIORS -On Wednesday evening these teams pitched wickets on the Runcorn ground ,in a friendly eacounter. The Juniors winning the toss put the " A "team in first, and considering the defective light a fair show was ma ie,t 3 being restated belore the team was die- ' missed.TheJaaiars followed and wickets fell "wry ouickly,and I there is no doubt they would have been more easily defeated 1 but for the unsportsmanlike behaviour of the Juniors, whose 1 captain caused some unoleasantness by disputing the umpire's time. With the last three balls of the match Hulse captured three wickets, being those of Moores, Cotterall and Cooke, and will b 3 presented with a " cap; Score : - RUNCMIN. JUNIoRS. flurrows b Jolley Wright run out I Williams run out 5 Jolley b F Burrows 1 Leathwood b Jol.ey F Burrows run out 0 Stanway b F Barrows ... 0 Hulse b Jolley 0 T Leach b F Burrows 0 Green 5' Moores 1 Pye not out 8 Humbell not out 0 Cotterall bit Weldon run out EC Davies st Stanway b Cooke b Hulse Leach Forrester b Moores 4 - . -</p>
        <p>Exteas 8</p>
