      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>C RICKET .</head>
        <p>C RICKET .</p>
        <p>On Saturday at Arpley, Warrington had the double satisfaction ot defeating the Bootle team, and wiping out the defeat they sustained from this team on the occasion of their fixture with them three weeks ago. The visitors winning the toss elected to bat first. Johnson and Crouch opened the innings, opposed by Dr. Smith and E. Connor. The first wicket was taken by Dr. Smith for four. GillmoreßlO next and a change was necessitated in the bowling, Bibby and Thewlis taking up the attack. With the addition of nine tuns Bibby met with suocess, bowling Gilmore, whilst his first ball to Spence defeated that player. Price arrived, and after placing Bibby somewhat luckily for four saw Crouch taken at short slip by Clarke at H. Price was next to leave, Bibby bawling the Bootle professional with a fine ball. H. Williams and T. S. Johnson were now associ- ated and both batted freely. The bowling changes were rung frequently, but all came like to the batsmen. At 84, J. B. Morgan and W. H. ['Amore were tried. John- son, however, was the first to leave, and at 95 he skied one to mid-on, Thewlis accepting the catch. Jones left tho same total, whilst in the following over Williams was caught on the boundary by Dr. Smith, his 40 having boon a masterly display of free cricket. The innings now came Co an abrupt conclusion, Morgan and Podmore each taking a wicket, the aggregate reaching 101.</p>
        <p>G. Clarke, and E. Connor opened the Warrington in- nings, the bowling being shared by Price and Airey. Runs came at a fast pace at the outset, until at 16 Con- nor was taken at third man. 'Ehewlis stayed whilst seven runs were added, and was then caught in the slips, With Clarke and Dr. Smith together a change was tried. Price crossing over and Smith relieving Airey. A separation was effected by the new b)wler. , Clarke having his off stump disturbed. J. W. Bibby I after driving Smith for four was bowled by Price, and four wickets were down for 45. L. A. Voisey joined his captain and drove Airey for two fours in quick succes- sion. The score reached 64 when Voisey was caught at cover point. W. El, Padtnore was ruled leg before with- out scoring, and Dr. Sinith at the same total-72 —was caught at pint, his 22 having been a faultless display. G. Hammon quickly added nine and was then caught at cover point, but an unexpected stand was mado by B. Silcock and I. Bowen. Both man played carefully taking no liberties, and the Bootle score was gradually overhauled. A. couple of fours to Bowen sent the hundred up, and the same hitter placed the last ball of Airey's over for two,Warrington winning amidst cheers. Both batsmen now hit more freely, and Bowen was eventually bawled at 11S, his 22 in a reat measure giving the locals the victory. B. Silcock, however, defied all the efforts of tthe Bootle bowlers, and with J. B. Morgan kept his wicket intact, seven o'clock arriving with the score 125 for nine wickets. Silcack showed very promising form for such a young player.</p>
        <p>Warrington second team visited the Beetle second team on Saturday and found the homesters in form. After a fin- display of batting, Crompton tocAr Jones's wicket for 43. and 'Nilsnn was run out for 35. Plowman contributed 18 and Sckweitzar rattled up 33 when his career was stopped by a catch off Duckworth. Parr made a grand stand for 39 (not out) and with the addi- tion of some smaller scores the Bootle captain decided to close the innings with the score at 205 for 8 wickets. Adamson made a good start with 14. Crompton bat- ting excellently increased the score by 33 before Soh- weitzar bowled him. Three more wickets were taken for an additional II runs. Then Pickton joined Pierpoint, and added another 14. The time limit arriving,the game was declared a draw, Warrington having lost 5 wickets for 74 runs.</p>
        <p>The Cheshire Linee Engineere had a a leit from P. Walker and Slim on Saturday. Hackett opened the homeaters innings auspiciously with 14 runs. Brown and P. Lawless batted well aced looked like sticking when in partnership. Brown's freo hitting laid him open to a oath which was accepted by Fairhuest, aftee ecoring 21. E. Allen next diepeeed of P. Lawless for 17 the result of some neat batting. The while team were despatched for 69. The first four wickets of the Brewery eleven fell for low figures. T. Alien checked the tile of disaster for a time by making a determined stand. He was eventually disoesed of fer 19 runs. The other wickets gave no trouble, and the innings of the Brewery team closed for 53. The second team of the Engineere Mee accimpliebecl a vietery over tha Brewery snow] team.</p>
        <p>Sim e excellent play WM; shown in the return match hetw3en S. Elphin'sand Padgate on Saturday at Dalton Bank and which created great interest. The Saints batted first. To Riley's credit it must be said their ulti- mate victory over Padgate was due solely to his masterly exhibition of batting • and despite the frequent changes of bowling, he carried' his bat out for 65. Tones made a neat 11, but the rest of the batsmen were got out at small expense. The fielding of Padgate was rather lo somas eleven were made in extras. Padgate made a praiseworthy attempt to win. Riley and Scarisbrick were put on to bowl and accounted for the whole of the wickets. Walker and Hutchings batted splendidly for 34 and 22 respectively and Boyd showed gold form for 14 runs. Padtrate totalled 105 runs altogether agains• 117, St. Elphin's. This match brings into prominence some promising players belonging to these teams.</p>
        <p>Sankey Wire Mills experienced an easy victory over Platt Bridge Independent Methodists on the grouni of tha latter on S3turFw. The Wire Mills hatted first. Marghall, Lavell, and Woodcock, wore in grand form, and found no diffieulty in scoring. These player scored respectively 50, 36, and 26. The innings was closcd for the loss of Ave wickets, total runs 124. The IMthodiste were dismis4ed in short order for 40 runs. Of these Hobson accounted for eight wickets for 19 runs.</p>
        <p>Hatton ani Whitecrosa e—nc—ounterel each other on Saturday. Whitecross were dismissed for 31. Thanks chiefly to D inn, whoge battina was very effective, th,, inninv of the Hatton eleven reached 45 thus securing thetn the victory,</p>
        <p>--- Grappenhalre fissure was with Irlev Park, and hilt for Burrows who carried his bat out for 20, would not have made much show. Newhall and Smith were thp beat bate of the Arley team whose venture totalled 46 runs against 37.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath administered a severe reverse to Davenharn on Saturday on the ground of the former. Shaw acid England were in rare form for howling and di9loiel of the whole of the oWtora for 29. Barber opened the homesters innings with 13 run., for whini he batted well. England compiled 13. and Stock:ea Heath eventually won by 45 runs.</p>
        <p>Parr' Bank found High L.gh in fine form on thAir vieit to them on Saturday. Hughes 20, Lavington 14, and 130IMeg 11. were the chief contributors towards the total score of 70 for the visitors. Si!cock and Broughall led the attack against the homesters. Gorton who was in splen- did form cut and drove the bowline, to the tune of 51 c runs,before Silcook adinistered the cent" Ze grace. Hulme increased the total kr good batting to 15 More. C egg Ilfollowing the good examples set him compiled 31 rung before Br-metal( found his wicket. Platt carried his het out for 26 runs. High Legh closed their innings for 128 altogether, for three wickets. Parr's Bank must look to their laurels on the occasion of their next match with High Legh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>CYCLING.</head>
        <p>CYCLING.</p>
        <p>_ _ At tha Macclesfield C.C. sports on Saturday-,w. O f Warrington, wen the third nrize in the two miles bicycle race. Be started from 190 yards mark. The performanco is the na,re'moritorions inasmach as Collin rode from Warrineton to Macclesfield on his machine, and on arrival had only just time to strip and enter his heat.</p>
        <p>A good number of members of the Warrington Cycling Club mustered for the run on Saturday to Liverpool. Tea was partaken of at the "Sr and Garter's and afterwards they went by boat to New Brighton. and the evening was spent in the Tower grounds. There was a good turn out on Tuesday for the run to Rostherne. On I both occasions the weather and roads were good.</p>
        <p>--- Arthur and F. W. Chase (tandem), the well_known professional cyclists. set up a new fiftv miles (11nPaced) road record over a straight course on Monday afternoon. The new figures are 2hr. 2min. 25iec.</p>
        <p>lady cyclist has invented a new road game. As a variant from terrifying old ladies and wayward children she is recommending chickens for the running down past time. Having read that many riders have been thrown by chickens, and PC outinz such an explanation, this sportive sceptic tells a cycling jlurnal she has " made it her business ever since she learnt to ride to try and run over some fowls, but has not succ.eded up to the present." It is a pity we cannot get the chickens views of the lady cyclist.</p>
        <p>Tom Linton has won one and lost the other of the two first important cycle races be has started in in America. In a3O miles paced race with Ekes at Bos- ton, Tom Linton led throughout and won easily in the good time of 56 min 50 1-5 SPC. In a match with Titus, the American middle-distance champion, however, the Welshman suffered defeat, Titus, it is said, showing faster pace now than he has ever done before.</p>
        <p>A well-knawn amateur crack of long experience Of speed work in liftieg and other hurryings on the highway has quaintly characterised the several degrees of "balmi- ness." Win you are going well you sit up and watch the pacing machines back-riders' saddle ; when your eve drops to the hub of its hind wheel you are beginning to feel the pace; and if you are hanging on with difficulty you can only just keep a glance +lt the tyre ; while when you are truing all out in agony,you keep your eye on your own bottom bracket.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>MAGAZINE CLIPPINGS.</head>
        <p>MAGAZINE CLIPPINGS.</p>
        <p>HUMOURS OF THE BATTLE. The battle abounded in picturesque and even amusing incidents. Pakenham, for example, who commanded the "Invincible," was a daring but somewhat reckless Irishman. lie drifted through the smoke an a French ship, and opened fire on her with great energy. After a time, the fire of the Frenchman died away, while that of the " Invinci- ble "grew yet more furious. Pakeuttam, however, ' was dissatisfied with the circumstance that the Frenchman made no reply, and he hailed her to know if she surrendered. The Frenchman replied, energetically, "No !" whereupon the gallant Irish- man inquired in tones of disgust, " Then, —you, why don't you fire !" Gambier, another of Howe's; captaias,was the exact opposite of the hare-brained Pakenbam, a fine sailor, a brave fighter,and of sober and puritanic temper. His ship, the "Defence,' of seventy-four guns,fought gallantly, and had two of her masts shot away ; when, through the smoke,lie tall masts of a French three-decker were visible bearing down upon her. A lieutenant hurried to the quarter-deck and cried to Gambier, "-- my eyes, sir, but here's a whole mountain coining down upon us ! What shall ws do 9" To which the unmoved Gambier answered by asking how his officer dared at such a moment as that to come to him with an oath in his mouth. "Go down to your guns, sir," he added. "and encourage your men to stick to their guns like British tars !"—" Cornhill Maga- zine."</p>
        <p>A LOST ART AMONG YANKEE BOYS. Every American boy finds many Isses for his jack- knife. It is equally indispensable whether be is cutting darts out of shingles, making willow whistles, or trimming kite-sticks; to say nothing of carving initials on fences and desks, or playing mumble-peg. But whittling as an amusement is probably not so common now as it was half a cen- tury ago, when toys of all kinds were much less numerous, and the pocket-kuile—often the boy's only store-bought possession—was accordingly much more important as a source af entertainment. While every one should rejoice in the many sports , and varied devices which at present contribute so much to a boy's menta: and physical development, and give him the steady and skilful hands so useful in after life, still it is to be regretted, if in our days of baseball and tennis, of amateur printing-presses and " kodaks," the ingenious use of the jack-knife that has made the Yankee boy proverbially a clever whittler should become a lost art.—From St. Nicholas" Magazine. THE FIRST TORPEDO.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. 0. Crowley, formerly electrician of the Torpedo Division, C.S.N., contribntes t.) the " Century" an account of "The Confederate Tor- pedo Service." After describing the organisation of the first Torpedo service, Mr. Crowley says: Having our system now perfected, we established a torpedo station, some five or six miles below Richmond, by submerging two iron tanks,containing one thousand pounds of powder each, in twelve feet of water, leading the wires ashore, and connecting them with a galvanic battery concealed in a small hut in a deep ravine. From the battery-house the wires were led to an elevated position near by, where the mln in charge could keep a lookout for passing vessels. The position of the torpedoes in the water was indicated by two sticks, planted about ten feet apart on the bluff, and in a line with each other and the torpedoes; and the watchman's instruc- tions were to explode them by contracting the wires as soon as an enemy's vessel should be on a line with the two pointers. All this being prepared, we awaited the approach of a Federal gunboat. As was usually the case, one came when leas:-, expected, on a beautiful</p>
        <p>clear day, when our entire force ex- cept the man stationed as lookout was absent in Richmond preparing other war material. We were apprised by telegraph of the rapid ap- proach of the gunboat, and immediately hastened towards our first station;but we arrived too late.Tbe man in charge had not see the United States Ilag for a long period, and never having previously seen a {gunboat so near, lost his presence of mind,and fired one ot the 1,030-pound powder-tanks when the gunboat was at least twenty to thirty yards distant A great explosion took pale, throwing up a large column of water ton considerable height; and the gunboat by her momentum plunged into the great trough, and laught the downward rush of a wave on her for ward deck. The guards were broken away, half a dozen men were thrown overboard, and other damage to the gunboat was caused. The steamer then turned about as quickly as she could, and pre- pared:to retrace her route down the river, after picking un the men who had been washed over- board. There was a brilliant opportunity to accom- plish her total destruction by firing the remaining torpedo as she passed bock over it. But alas! the man bad been so astounded at the first explosion that he had fled precipitately, without waiting to see what damage had been done, and the gunboat was thus enabled to return down the river in safety.— " Century Magazine." EIGHTEENTH CENTURY CRITICISM IN DUBLIN.</p>
        <p>Could the dramatic critics on the Dublin press o, the preseat day hand their editors "copy equal to the fcllowing ' notice" of the first appearance in the Irish metropolis of Mrs. Siddons,which appeared in the" Dublin Journal" more than a hnndred years ago ? The Italics are our own. " Yesterday, Mrs. Siddons' about whom all the world has been talking exposed her beautiful, adamantine, soft, and lovely pars no for the first'titue in the Theatre Rnal,Smock Alley, in the bewitching. melting, and all-tearful character of "Isabella. From the repeated pane- gyrics in the London papers we were taught to ex- pect the sight of a heavenly angel, but how were we supernatnrally surprised into the most awful joy in beholding an earthly goddess. The house was crowdad with hue edS More than it could hold, with thousands of admiring spectators who went away without a sight. This extraordinary pheno- menon of tragic excellence, this star of Mel- pomene, this comet of the stage, this sun of the firmament of the muses, this moon of black verse, this queen and Princess of tears exceeded expectations, went beyond belief, and soared above all powers of description. She was nature itself; she is the most exquisite work of art • in short, she was the bouquet of Parnassus. Several persons fainted before the curtain went up ; but when she came to the scene of parting with her wedding ring, oh, what a sight was there ! The very orchestra,"albeit unused to the melting moad:"wept like hungry children. . . . Nature sure, in one of her beautiful moments, in one of .her humane leisure hours, in one of her smiling days, in one of her weeping months, made this acme of perfection. Oh, happy Hibernia ! blessed isle, what bright excellence of excellences stand on the turf of thy fruitful earth! From Cork, from Killarney, from Galway ; from east and t rom I west, from north and south, shall thousands come to Smock Alley to see this woman! True it is, Mr. Garrick once refused to engage thee. True it is the London audience once did not like thee; but what of that! Rise, thou bright goddess, and-coar to im- mortal regions, for " envy will merit as its shade pursuo."'—"Cornhill Magazine" RELICS OF THE SPANISH ARMADA.. _ . .</p>
        <p>One of the most fatal spots for Armada ships had been Sligo Bay. When Geoffrey Fenton went to view this scene of disaster, he found Spar.ish guns sunk half a horseman's staff in the shifting sand, and "numbered in one strand of less than five miles in length above eleven hundred &amp;ad corpses of men which the sea had driven upon the shore "; and the country people told him " the like was in other places, though not of like number." Somewhat later, the lord deputy, on his way, as he expressed it, to despatch 'those rags" of the Spaniards which still infected the country, saw with amazement the ' masses of wreckage scattered along a beach on the same coast—timber enough to build "five of the greatest ships that ever I saw, besides mighty great boats, cables and other cordage, and some such masts for bigness and length as I never saw any two could make the like." A copy of the sailing directions given by Medina Sidonia, found perhaps in some Captain's sea-chest among the wreckage, fell into the lord deputy's hands. A grim smile must have flitted over his face as he read the words: "Take great heed lest you fall upon the Island of Ireland for fear of the harm that may happen unto you upon that coast." An Armada relic still more touching than these instructions is part of a letter written off Dingle by a Spanish captain,begging the President of Munster for friendly treatment. Into a bundle of state papers which tell, in the language of the victors, the awful story of shipwreck and bloodshed on the Irish coast, this fragment, rusty and stained as if by salt water, has strayed like a Century Magazine." wail from the vanquished.—"</p>
        <p>C Y C ITI 13 r 8 USE PILLAR BRAND. Lubricatlng and Burning 01le and Cycle Bnansel, Etigasellecteould BrunswicA Black. From all Dealers. AGENTS: McLEAN AND CO., 13, e.BBL-STUIDST, Lwattroor...</p>
