      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>WARRINGTON CRICKET GROUND COMPANY, LIMITED.</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON CRICKET GROUND COMPANY, LIMITED.</p>
        <p>The that statutory general meeting of the above Co, ay was held in the Pavillion of the Arpley Ground on Thursday evening last when a large number of shareholders were in attendance. The Chairman Mr. R.T.Fairelough explained that the meeting was called to fulfil the legal obligation which made it necessary for all limited Companies to hold their first general meeting within four months after registration. He had pleasure in informing them that the purchase of the ground was now an accomplished fact as the conveyance of the land to them had been completed and the ground was now their own absolute property. He also read a state- ment shewing that all the capital required with the exception ot about £320 had been subscribed for. and he trusted that all new members of the club would take at least a few shares until this sum of £320 was also raised and they courd then go along absolucely free from debt, and he assured them the directors would do all they possibly could in order to get this remaining £320 subscribed for. The following gentlemen were then unanimously elected directors of the Company for the ensuing year :—Mr.R. T. Fairclough (chairman), Messrs. IL H. Silcock, William Richardson, William Pierpoint, John Mainwaring, W. H. Robinson, and J. L. Tun- stall. A pleasing feature in connection with the meet- ing was the presentation by Mr. Thomas Birtles te the chairman of a large framed portrait of the pro- moters of the purchase of the ground, viz., Messrs. R. T. Fairclough, R. H. Silcock, William Richard- son, J. Mainwaring. C. Leigh, the bon. secretary, Mr. 'C. 11. Unsworth ; and the Chairman thereupon presented the same to the President of the Cricket Club, Mr. William Pierpoint, who duly acknow- ledged same on behalf of the members of the c/üb. The portrait has been hung in the pavilion, end is a beautiful example of the photographic art. Messrs. Voisey and Worthington, chartered accountants, King-street, Warrington, have been appointed auditors to the Company, and Mr. C. n. Unsworth, of 11, Lyme-street, is the secretary. It is hardly necessary to say that the members of the Club are much gratified at the successful manner in which the purchase scheme has been carried through.</p>
        <p>..apai ;ticket</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>PAD(TATE.</head>
        <p>PAD(TATE.</p>
        <p>INeuesT.—Mr. Jones (deputy coroner) held an inquest relative to the death of Elizaketla Carpenter, at the Padgate Stocks Inn, Padgate, ea Saturday afternoon. John Carpenter, husband of the deeeased—the only witness called--said he awoke at 6 o'clock on the Sith inst. and noticed the deceased was not in bed. He went downstairs to the kitchen and found his wife in a sitting posture with a rope round her head and suspended by a rope attached to the ceiling. He immediately cut her down and sent for the doctor. In answer to the coroner the witness said she had never threatened to take her own life, but she had 1 suffered from iroomnia.—Without retiringt the jury 1 brought in a yerdiot of" Suicide whilst temporarily , in- sane." • -</p>
        <p>MEMORIAL TO TRE LATE MR. HOYSTON.—The parish- ioners having sub4cribed for a memorial to the late Mr. William Royston. it was decided to present a solid silver communion service to the church of which he had been such a faithtul servant, having been churchwarden for about 40 years. Designs were submitted by local and other jewellers, and ultimately it was decided to accept that of Messrs. Eustance and Co. of 43, Sankey-scraot. The design is known as the "Camden" Service. On the base of the flagon and chalice is inscribed " In memory. cf William Royston churchwarden for many years 1897." The work has been carried out in an eminently satisfac- tory manner, and the chaste design and beauty of fin- ish are highly creditable to the firm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>WEEKLY REPORT .ON THE HEALTH</head>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT .ON THE HEALTH</p>
        <p>OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Week ending May 28:b. Durinr; the week the following births and destlift were registered :</p>
        <p>Births. Deaths. Warrinaton Registration District 51 24 The excess of births over deaths was 27. Birth Rate 422 per 1,000 of the population. The death-rate from all causes was equal to 191 per 1,000, against 181 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 12.8 the corresponding period of last year. The death. rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 01 per I,ooo.against 1.4 the average rate of the previous four weeks, and against nil the corresponding period of 1897. The ages of mortality were- 0-1. 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-85 65 up. 2 5 1 1 10 5 24. The deaths included-1 from diarrhoea, 3 from phthisis, 8 from lung diseases, 1 from injuries, 11 front all other causes. The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week :—Nil. Total number of patients in hospital, week ending 28th May—Nil. METEOROLOGY.—The mean temperature was 52 Fah.' and therefore 4 deg. higher than the preceding week. The maximum temperature 69F. was recorded on the 25th inst., and the minimum temperature 39 F. on the 28th inst. The rainfall measured .58in. The general direction was N. 8., the reading of the anemometer denoting 545 miles.</p>
        <p>JOHN H. GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health. -</p>
