      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>THERE IS ONLY ONE.</head>
        <p>THERE IS ONLY ONE.</p>
        <p>There is only on, "First" in a race, and it is acknow- ledged without doubt that GWILYM EVANS' QUININE BITTERS, The Vegetable Tonic, is The Best Remedy of the Age for Weakness, Nervousness, Indigestion, Loss of Appetite, Impure Blood, Chest Affections, Low Spirits, nd Itifluecza. This remedy of world-wide fame strengthens that part of the system which is weakest or 1:13E1 been weakened by disease,and therefore more liable to colds and then attendant ailments ; it purifies the blood and stimulates the circulation, assists and pro- motes digestion, and improves the appetite. It braces the nerves and fortifies the muscles, rouses the sluggish liver, and thus enlivens the spirits ; it removes all im- purities and obstructions from the human body, and gives tone to the whole system. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is purely vegetable, for the formula includes the Tonic properties of Sarsaparilla, Saffron, Gentian, Bur- dock, Lavender, and Dandelion, in addition to Quinine, and is suitable to all ages, from the infant to the adult, and confidently recommended to those who have to devote themselves to study and brain work, to all who work long hours in close rooms, to those who breathe impure air, and all who have to stand exposure of the weather. People that suffer from brain fag and sleeplessness, who feel " out of sorts "and "run down" lad rapid and permanent relief from the use of Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. The occupations of some men keep their whole faculties continually at their furthest stretch, and life is all hurry and worry. Little wonder is it that this extreme tension leads to an unusual wear and tear of the nerves and that these hard-worked toilers require an extra sustenance for body and brain. Now, Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters feeds both, and men are wise if they look ahead and preserve their al:length by taking this infallible feeder to all vital powers. There are five fixed facts about this renowned preparation—let, used by medical profession; 2nd a purely vegetable com- pound ; 3rd, absolutely free from mercury ; 4th, tested for a quarter of a century ; sth, The Best Tonic. There are many medicinal tonics offered to the isublic,but they may be classified under two heads :—The Best and The Rest. GWILYM EVANS' QUININE BITTERS IS THE BEST. Caution, beware of imitations. See that you get Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, with the mime Gwilym Evans" on label, stamp, and bottle. Sold everywhere in bottles at 2s. 91. and 4s. 63., or in cases containing three 4s. 63. bottles at 123. 6d. per case. Should any difficulty be experienced in procuring it the Proprietors will forward for the above prices, carriage free. Sole Proprietors : Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales. GWILYM EVANS' QUININE BITtERS. GWILYM EVANS' QUININE BITTERS. GIVILYAI EVANS' QUININE BITTERS.</p>
        <p>SATURDAT.—Before Mr. T. Birchen (in the chair) Mr. E. T Fairclough and Mr J. W. Hayward. ALLBGED :THIFT Or JEWELLERL—Henry Daecev Pitz- 'humans and James La Velle (soldiers) were charged with breaking and entering the shop of Mr. J. Nichni, Jeweller, 30, Buttermarket-street, and stealing six gold plated watch chains of the value oft 43. 6d.—The Chief Constable said he had to ask for a remand for a week to enable him to com- plete the case against the prisoners, as all the stolen property , had not been rezovered. The men were charged with break- ing sad entering the lack-up shop of the prosecutor. ejl Thursday at iliac o'clock Er. Nichol loeked his shop up end went home t.c his residence at Padgace. The window of tie shop was plate glass and not protected by shutters. On Pi- day moraing about ten minutes past four P.C. Edwards was on duty in the street and his attention was attracted by tlie broken window. He noticed the stock in the shop had bean dietarbed and seat for Mr. Nichol. Assistance was rendered to the officer by a man named Chrietopher Fagan, who s'ns standing at Market Gate. and who heard a noise of breekint glass down the street. He saw two men in uniform and a man in plain clothes. The man in plain cloches struck at the window with something. P.C. Edwards was on duty the same day in Horsernarket-street, when he saw the prisoners. They were unable to give a satisfactory account of them- selves and were locked up. They made statements incrimi- nating each other.—The case was remanded for a week. SLEEPING OUT —Edward Hardy, a labourer was charged with sleeping on the premises of Idessre. Kenyon and Co., Bank Quay, that morning Defendalt pleaded guilty._r.s. Brans said at 6.15 in consequence of a complaint received from the manager of .the company, he went to the Spatter Works and taunt defendant asleep.—Defendant promised not to offend again and the case was dismissed.</p>
        <p>MoNris.r.—Before Mr. F. Monks (in the chair), Alderman Harrison. Councillors Shaw-Greea and Smethurst, and . .</p>
        <p>Messr. s Birchall and Wright. _ --</p>
        <p>RETURN OF THE MILITIA.—ThORISS Ntannerley, iron worker, 35. Warburton-street. was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Market-sheet en Saturday night. When requested by a police officer to go home prisoner told him he would not go until he had had" a pop at the bobbies."—A fine of be. was imposed.—For a similar off secs in Bridge-street Thomas Slater, who described himself as a phrenologist. was seat to gaol far 14 days.—Patrick Berke. last from Manchester ; Samuel Jones, Howard-street, and Sarah Jarvis, 281 Winwick-street, were each fined ss. for drunkenness and disorderly conduct.—The Chief Constable said the return of the Militia from camp had occasioned a batch of eases. A WIFE DES ERTEN{ SENT TO PRIDIN —James Butler, chair moulder, last from Chester was summoned for deserting his wife and family on the 27th of April, and leaving them chargeable to the common funds of the Warrington Union-- Prisoner said he had been away with the Militia.—Mr. B. She:seer, releving officer, stated that prisoner's wife and two children were admitted to the Workhouse on April 25th. A warrant was taken out against prisoner, and it was found out that he had gone with the Militia. He was discharged on Saturci sy. lie (Mr. Shearer) thought prisoner and his wife connived at this. She took her discharge on Saturday with the object of going to meet him. It seemed rather hard that tlee Union should have to keep bee while her husband went with the Militia. Prisoner had been up six weeks, and the wife had been in the house seven weeks.—The Chief Con. stable sail prisoner had been before the Court for various offences on thirteen previous occasions.—The Chairm in (to Mr. Shearer) : Do you press the case.—Mr. Shearer: Cer- tainly. I think an example should be made of such a man. —Prisoner wai committed to gaol far a month.</p>
        <p>YOUTHFUL GIMESTERS.--Thorrias Humphreys, 6, Quav Fold; George Haywood and John William Haywood, 14. Quay Fold ; and Thomas Oultram, 31, Liverpool-road, all youths, pleaded guilty to a charge of playing a game of cards called "banker" in Atherton-street, on Sunday, the sth June. —P.C.Delahav proved the case.—The Chief Constable said that on tlde previous day a dozen offl'ers were out in plain clothes On account of numerous complaints received of this kind of conduct. Cases were reported week after week in the news- papers, and these youths must know that card playing for money was wrong.— A fine of be. and costs, lie. in all, was inflicted in each case. .</p>
        <p>THE MUZZLING ORDER.—Charlsa Mounfield. Horsernarket• Street. was fined Is for allowing his dog to be at large un- muzzled on the 7th June.—The Chief Constable said he was in hopes that they were now within measurable distance of emancipation from the order. TUESDAY. Before Mr. Robert GarnAt (in the chair), Councillor Shaw Green. and Mr. Thlmas</p>
        <p>A &amp;nom Crirnacrfca —Thomas Mitchell, a militia man, was charges with being drunk and disorderly on Monday night, and with assaulting P.C. Clark.—Prisoner pleaded guilty to the first charge but not guilty to the charge of as- sault.—P.C. Clarke said from information received at 9.15 p.m. on the day in question he went to Town Hill. Both Mitchell and his mother were drunk and carrying on in a disorderly way. He had same trouble with Mitchell but took him into custody with the assistance of P.C. Edwards. The prisoner struck witness in the mouth.—P.C. Edwards corro- borated. He said that when he go; to the scene Clarke was on the ground and the prisoner was kneeling on his stomach. —The Chief Constable said he was in the pollee station when prisoner was brought In. He was mad drunk and calling tile officers the most frightful names. It was his 13th appear- ance.—Tho Bench sent him to prison for six weeks with hint labour without the option of a fine.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAT.—Before I'dr John FaircTough.(in the chldr), and</p>
        <p>Messrs. Geddes and_Blre-Pvtli.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED FALSE ATTF.STATIIN.—Jo,eph Stockwell Crosby of the 3rd South Lancashire Regiment. stationed at Sea- forth iwas charged with making a false attestation on th 3 11th June.—ft was stated prisoner had receive two days pay by the I attest Won —The Bancis remanded him until Thursday.—When the CUD was again heard on Thursday. the magistrates decided to dismiss the case as the Command- ing Officer had not sent the necessary authority. INDECENT CONDUCT.—Joseph Kelly. a single man. for 20 years employed by the Cheshire Lines Committee was charge with indecent conduct in Bewsey-stree., ow Saturday. Defendant pleads I guilty -nu Beach sent him to gaol for one month with hard labour.</p>
        <p>TRURSDAY.—Ba!bra Mr Chasidley (in ths chair), AG 1 Mr James liepherd. ALLEGED TRAVELLING WiTanta A TICK ET.—ItAl Rh Greaves, was charged with travelling from Wigan to War- rington on the London and North-Western Railway, without paying his fare —Defendant pleaded gulity.—De.c.ive John Hovells said they received a telegram at Bank Qaav station with the information that deiendant had jurnoed on the train as it was leaving Wigan. Shey folnd him in a horse box.—Greaves informed the polio that he had come from Blackburn to Wigtn.—The bench remanded Cis case for a week,</p>
        <p>Daysic AND DISIRDERGT And.rsoa last Lois Liverpool, pleaded guilty to this offence. The esidence of the police olncer was thtt defen tent WAS In 'company with two soldiers In virfnwick-st., on Weinesdav nlisht at twenty min tes pis&amp; eleven when pssdnit the cshmerei shsiter• sh 9 struck one of the alonen. Shs usel sten/ obscene tannage. She was dent to goal for seven days.</p>
