      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>BEFORE THE ,ALIG-ISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE ,ALIG-ISTRATES.</p>
        <p>A VIOLENT MOTHER-IN•LLW.</p>
        <p>On Friday at the Police Court before the Mayor (Alder- man J. E. Wright) chairman, Alderman Roberts,Coun- eillor Smethurst, and Mr. James Hepherd, 3annah Chadwick 5, Fountain-street, was charged with assault- ing Samuel Hunt 38, Lythgoes-lane on the first of June. —Mr. F. R. Roberts, solicitor, who appeared for the complainant said it was an assault by the mother-in law on the son. The complainant was a very young man, and he married a very young woman, and it had lead to a very great deal of unhappiness aggravated by the mother-in-law. Coming home on the day in question, he found his wife out and from inquiries made he fol- lowed her to the Rodney Inn. She was having a drink paid for her by a strange man. They went away and half-an-hour after the mother-in-law called him a b— sod and followed it uo by knocking him against the fire. She made some didgueting remarks about him and his condact to his wife. His client did not wish to press the case, he would be quite content if his peace was assured.—Tames Herbert Hunt, said on the day in question he had been working, and got home about 6.30 and found his wife out. He followed to the Rodney Inn, where he found her, with her brother, her mother and a strange man. She was having a small rum with the man, and he objected. His mother-in-law used foul language to him and struck him. His wife tried to stop her. Be went outside and his mother-in-law and wife followed. His mother in-law began using foul language again and to swing her bonnet.—Mrs. Glover.Lythg,ees- lane, said she was present on the day in question at the house. The man was telling his wife not to let it happen again, when his mother-in-law went for him and struck him, and he had to run out —Mrs. Chadwicks, Turner- street, corroborated. She said in her opinion the defen- dant would have been much more hurt if they had not stopped defendant.—The defendant in defence said on Wednesday she went to the station to meet a friend who was coming fram Manchester. Her daughter having been rather ill that day, she asked her to have a glass of wine and she paid for it.—The bench bound her over in £5 to keep tne peace for 6 months and nay the costs. I POLICEMAN ASSAULTED —I YOUNG MAN TO TEM</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>REscrz.</head>
        <p>REscrz.</p>
        <p>_ At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Monday morning, before Mr. Monks and other magistrates, Martin Purcill. a farm labourer, of Fearnhead, was charged with assaulting P.C. Bradley whilst In the execution of his duty in Butter- enarket-street, shortly after 11 o'clock on 'Saturday night.—Prisoner pleaded not guilty--P.C. Bradley said that shortly after eleven o'clock on Saturday night he saw a crowd of harvestmen standing on the footpath at the corner of Bank-street. He asked them to go away, as they were obstructing the footpath. Tracy began to use bad language to him, and one of them struck him from behind. He turned round to see who it was, and he was then struck behind the neck by the prison r. Witness then caught hold of prisoner, and three others attacked him. I. young man named Jeffers came to his assistance and they teak prisoner into custody. The prisoner bit witness across the knuckles.—Tbe Chief Constable : It is just as though a dog had gnawed him. —Bradley, continuing, said that Jeffers got kicked, and he-lost his cap in the struggle.—The Chief Constable : These strapping men rather trade upon their numbers and physique.—Michael Jeffers, John-street, saidhe was in Buttermarket-street on Saturday night when he saw P.C. Bradley assaulted by three or feur men. lie also saw the prisoner bite him, and when the constable tried to blow his whistle they knocked it out of his mouth. Witness assisted in getting the prisoner to the Police Statien.—The Chief Constable : It was a good perform- ance on the part of Jeffers.—Jeff era said he lost his cap and got kicked.—The Chairman said prisoner would have to go to gaol for one month without the option of a fine. The /tench were much obliged to Jeffers, and they would allow him ss. from the poor box.l A JAMESON TROOPER AT WARRINGTON. At the Warrington Police Court on Monday morning before Mr. F. Monks and other megistrates, James Shaw, last from liampatall, was charged with being, a deserter from the Ist Battalion RoyalfArtillery, stationed at Cspetown, Smith Africa, since Sep-ember 2al, —The Chief Constable said prisoner stated he was one of Jameaon's troopers and had taken part in the rail . Alderman Harrison was that the celebrated raid ? The Chief Constable : Yes. Prisoner had been wounded. He had on him when apprehended a discharge from the Matabeleland forces. Prisoner was remanded to Strange- ways to await an escort.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT. FRIDAY.—Before the Mayor (Alderman J. B. Wright) chairman. Alderman Roberts, Councillor Smethurst and Mr. James Hepherd. A DOMESTIC MSTURSAISCR.—SamneI Hunt. 38,Lvt.hgoe's- lane. was charged with beating his wife Mary Hunt, 5. Fountain-street, on the sth June. Mr. F. R. Roberts appeared for the defendant, who pleaded not guilty. The complainant said they went for a walk on Sunday night and met a woman who was drunk. Her husband teok the woman home and left her. When he came back he said the woman was "no good." At night he thumped her in bed twice across the head and said he would " swing for her. He made her very nearly deaf with striking her. She left him on the Monday night and he said if she would oily come hack he would apologise to his mother-ia-law for what had previously occurred. Witness said she did not wish to live with him another day. She had only been married to him ten weeks, but had a life like a dog with him.—Mr. Roberts sail he was given to understand the girl's mother had Induced her to leave her husband. It was very easy for a woman to make a statement of that kind, and it was not very easy to have witnesses under the circumstances. His client utterly and totally denied the assault. There wrs no doubt the.wife was entirely under the influence of Vie Mother-in-law and he asked them to dismiss the ease. The Bench after due consideration, dismissed the ease.—The Mayor remarked to the defendant that he had known his father very well, and he advised him to try and live with his wife in peace. FAMILY Disacinktmaiirr.—Patrick MoCloughlin was charged With assaulting Ann Mceloughlin on the sth Jnne.--Both re- sided at 1 Talbot-yard.—Tho evidence given was that Patrlek -Mc Oloughlin started fighting with his brother aid their mother interfered. Patrick took his mother,the complainant by the throat after " clouting" her on the head.—The defend- ant said be was sorry,he was in drink at the time.—The Chief Constable : He has been here three times before. The bench lined him 10s and casts or 11 days hard labour in default. Marsh, complainant I Maianrasnht- I.TeIS4 TIM FATS.—Elizi.beth Harper, Turner-street, was :rinthe for three months on June 3rd. T e de, charged With using threats itponCos thhfeaogdrrnl iiitlthttyehe first time street.—Defendant P ie bre st e ant used very eiad she spoke to Harper Mrs. h f foul language to her • id only lift up her and told her if she svOu into halves. She simply hand, she would tear her to keep the peace.—Bvidence wished her to be bound over sves given in corroboration.—Mrs. Harper denied using the wherethrts and id he alleged it trhaolc not fh,e,:in down Haydock-street eight months. WitnessPs were called for the defence who corroborated the defendant's Statement.—The bench bound the defendant o:er in one the costs• surety el P.. 5 to keep the peace a p„..y</p>
        <p>To MOTICISTus.—Mrs. WII-nsloves Soothing iyrup has been used over fifty Years by millions of mothers for their chil- dren while teething with per feet success. It will relieve the poor suffervr immed natural quiet iately. It Is pleasant to taste : it pro- duees and the little cherub awakes tt as briiitt as a button," Of all BI.T. by relieving the child from pain chemists le. lfd. per bottle.—Advt. '</p>
