      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>WARRINGTON COMA' PETIT SEiSION3</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON COMA' PETIT SEiSION3</p>
        <p>WEDNESrlY.—llefore Colonel Rldgersy (chairman), and Me. Clanda Morels.</p>
        <p>DRUNKAD DISORD,IRLY.—FIr tiaese offtnees the follow- ing were dealt with :—Jolin Fisher. on the 11th Inst. at Penketh, 5s incleding costs ; John Riley and James Halliwell. on the 4th J. 1,10, at Bartanwool, in Chapel lane, ss. including costs. A CYCLIST YV/I0 CJITTAIV'T AIII3YOft, Of Pertketh. was charge I with haviug no the 26th Ra 7. ridden a bicycle on the highway at 11-30 p.m. without a lighte4 lamp.—PC. Coupe stated tint h 3 W 43 on ditty at Pinketh at the time stated when he stw the clefiodant riding a bicycle without a lamp Witness (I,.ew his attention to i its facc., and told him he would repo:t him. Witness did not see the defendant until he role abreast of him—Cross. erarotned : Defendant did not tell him he couldn't, ride the machine.—The defendant. s'atej tint a friend of his asked him if he would wheel a bicycle home for him on the way from Warringtou to Penketh. He consented to do so. There was no lamp Alt, shed, and witness wts unabie ta rids a bicycle. He was attempting to do so when the cons'able saw him.—Ths Chtirmtn said there VISS n doubt the defen- dant was riding at the time tha c nubble saw him. He wou'd be fined ss.</p>
        <p>ASLEEP WHILST IN CHARGE OF A VAN.—Frederick Bennett, Hammy-street, Warrington, was summoned on a charze of being asleep whilst Irocharge of a confectioners's van on the 28th May at Penk,th,—P C. Coupe proved the offenne, and said it was some time befare he WAS able to awaken the de- fendant.—The magistrates impaled a fine of 5.1. and costs. AN UNLICENSED DOG, John Dixon, of Burtonw.io I, woe charged with having on the 9th May at Bortonw rod ke?t, dog without s llcense.—Defendant pleaded guilty, and stated that he was working whea the last Court WAS C. Endes gave evldenne In support of the charge. arid the de- fendant was fined 15s. including costs. THE LONGFORD HOTEL MANAGER ASSAULTED —Timothy Halliday, a labourer. reC antly employed at Orford T tnnery, failed to Answer a charge of having on the 33th May at Win- wick assaulted Joseph Hatton licensee of the Longford Hotel and also with having at th 3 same time and place been drunk on thn licensed premises and refused to quit the Barns when r .quested te do so.—lt was stated that the defendant asked the nasgistrates to proceed with the case in the defend- had left the neighbourhood.—Mr. Browne, who prosecul el, ant's absence. The defendant went into the Longford Hotel about four o'cloalc in the afternoon of the date metathetic&amp; He was drunk. and Mr flatten the licensee eefuse Ito serve him. He ordered hint out, but defendant refused to go. M. Hat- ton then put him out. Ile struggled. and struck Mr. Hatton twice. A fow minutes afterwards defendant re-entered the house, and without any wailing aommeneed to throw stones about the olace. One of them caught Mr. Hatton in the eye and another hit a Customer in the enr. Other stone; which defendant threw made indentations in the walls. Mr. Rattan caught hold of defendant and locked him in a stable He then sent for the police. He (Mr Browne) submitted that land- lords ought lobe protected wh'ie att ,mpting to con toot their business In a proper manner.—Joieph Hatton, licensee of the hotel. and Benjamin Hatton. his son, gave evidence confirm- ing Mr. Browae's statement.—Defendant was fined 40s for the assault and 5s and r ests for dtunkenness. THE ALLEGED THEFT or A COLT AT WooLeToN.—Patrick Smith WAS eisargc lore remand with having sto:en on June 4th at Woolston a man's blank oat, valued at 2s 6d, the pro- perty of James Horrn.—Defendant expressed a wish that the case should he tried at the sesslons.—The Maglstr 103' Clerk informed him that he would have to wait six weeks, and he replied that he could stand thet." Eventually he changed his wind. and elected to be dealt with summarily. —Sarah Horn, widow, livlng at the Old Toll Bar House. Woolston, soli the -coat belongs. 1 to her son. Defendant, who h:cl lodged with her for sometime, left her on Saturday, and when loch d gone she miser 1 the coat She had not given or lent the cent to defendant.—P.o Bellinger stated that he arrested defendant in a lodling-hotase In Warrington, and charged him with the then. He replied •• I have the coat. but I was going back on Tuesday.. Defendant said he had worked in prosecutrix's garden several nights, and as she had no money she gave him the coat.—The Chairman said the case was a suspicious one.but defendant had previously borne a good Character. He would be bound nye- 0 °nate up for judgment when called upon. A YGUEGi ITALIAN'S WANDERINGS.—LegIiriI Dail% a yOing Italian, vac charged on remand with having fraudu- lently travelled on the London and North-Western Railway between Liverpool and Newton on June Sth. He was also charged with having stolen six bicycle infl %tors. the property of the company. Ths C ,se Was adjourned from the last Newton Petty Sessions in order that an interpreer might be eng-ged,as the defendant WAS unable to speak Eng- lish.—lnspector Atkin, of the Company's Detective Depart- ment, Liyerpool,stated that the evidence with regard to the second offence was lot of a, very definite otharacter, and he ' proposed to withdraw the charge. —The foreman porter at Newton, said he met the passenger train which left, Liverpool , at quarter-past eleven on Tuesday nightlie noticed when the coaches were being shunted into siding that prisoner . was in the front an,d'e ran. If. ftl 1^ sVAA joh,r 9</p>
        <p>FUR.—SKIN RUGS Greased. Cleaned, 11," Fur alterations of .very detori“tlon. B. Wanufaeturirg Furrier, 21, Renshaw-street, T.. rPOO" ."3" ablished 24 years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>EXAMINEF, FR]</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINEF, FR]</p>
        <p>the Weal shelf, and he had a package eontaining the bicycle I inftatore in his right hand. Witness called to him to come out. He was leaving the van, when he turned beck to pick up the package of inflators. They made out from him that lee had corner from Liverpool and %Ma going to Manchester. The only English words he could get out of the prisoner was that he had no ticket and no money. Prisoner now made his statement through an interpreter. He said the consul at Belfast paid hi* fare to Liverpool. as he (prisoner) told him he thought he could get good work there. Not finding any work in Liverpoool he wanted to go to Manchester. He ran away from his parents in Rely, and journeyed to London via Paris. He had a litt!e money when he came to London. He was willing to work for any- one, but had learned no trade.—The Chairman I wonder whatever he came here for? This is a very peculiar CASS Mr. Taylor. police court missionary, said he could take the lad to the Firewood Factory, and they would employ him there.—Prisoner on being asked by the interpreeer whether he would be willing to go to Liverpool preparatory to being sent home said he did not want to go hems. He was anxious to find work in England. He would like, he said, to get something to do en a steamer.—The Chairman : We cannot do anything for him in that way.—ln answer to the megis• trates, Mr. Taylor said lee could get Iprisoner temporary em- ployment in Liverpool. and would also andeav ,ur to obtain. work for him on board a steamer.—Prisoner through the in- terpreter, thanked Mr. Taylor very cordially.—The Chairmen said the case would be dismissed, and Mr. Taylor would take Charge of the prisoner.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY 5E3310N3.</p>
        <p>VONDIY. Timmins.</p>
        <p>SCHOoL dvans was fined be. including costs for failing to oomply with the School Board regulations. AN EMPTY EDUIE WITH TWO TENANT3.—Fur sleeping in What was described as an .• empty house 0 in Mill-street, Runcorn, Robert Collins and Elizabeth Barker. were 'en- tanned to one month's imprisonment with hard labour.— Both were old offende,s. Barker having been about 50 times before the court and Collins 10. .* PULLING FACES AT A NEIGH3OtTa."—Mary Clarke, of Canal-street, a married woman. was summoned by a neigh- bour, Lydia Rutter for assatut.—Rutter stated that the defend sure daughter Mary Ann came and scraped her feet on the step she had just cleaned,and pulled faces at her. Upon Witneas remonstrating with her she went and told her mother, who came out of the yard and struck her with a brush. She sent her daughter for a poker and used very bad language.—Defendant denied the charge in tots). saying the boot was on the other leg.—lsabella Russel. of Wlvern-place, gave evidence to seeing the defendant with the brush, but no blows were seen to be struck.—Tne Baach dismissed the Cale.</p>
        <p>A. Tato or TRAMP3.--James Taylor, John Carter, and John Calvert, describe ias tramps, were brought up in custody charged with bagging in York-street at ten o'clock on Sunday morning.—Sergeant Bourne stated that at ten minutes past nine on Sunday morning the pruioners were begging fro n floor to door in Bilf stir-street. Calvert commenced singing, the hymn " There Is room for Mary there" in a pathetic strain. The prisoners knocked at all the doors in that street. They did the same thing in York-street. When apprehended Calvert had 2e lid, Taylor lo id, and Carter lid in their possession, all in pence and half peace.—The Bench com- mitted them to gaol for one month each Without optiou,wich hard labour.</p>
