      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>STRANGE ADVENTURE OF A ST. HELENS WOMAN.</head>
        <p>STRANGE ADVENTURE OF A ST. HELENS WOMAN.</p>
        <p>SERIOUS EFFECTS OF AN IMMERSION.</p>
        <p>It has often been noticed that a shock sustained in early childhood carries with it consequences of the most unpleasant character, which remain for years ; and in some cases almost make life a misery, life-long weakness or infirmity being frequ ntly attributable to an accident in childhood's days. A representative of the "St Helens, Newspapor and Advertiser 'recently had a conversation with Mrs. Sarah Robinson, the wife of a colliery engine winder, who resides at No. 4, Back Scheles-lane, Matto Reath, S. Helens. She is a young woman about twenty- five years of age who has only been married two years, and teem her rosy cheeks and plump appearance it is not difficult to imagine that she is in the boot of health and spirits,</p>
        <p>" When I was a litile child, and residing in Wales," said Mrs. Robinson, " I had a narrow escape from drowning. I fell into a pit of water, and my father just managed to rescue me as I was going down for the last time, as they say. It was with considerable difficulty that I was brought round, and then I was 11l for some time, a weakness following wnicia troubled me up to quite recently, and I was a trouble to myself and others. When I got up in the miming I would liaV a sickly feeling, my eys would be full of water, I would feel giddy and faint, and upon having my 131J1ii.9 everything I bad eaten would be vomited. Two years ago I got married, but my health did not Improve, althugh I , sought medical ad vice, bat the alignt relief I obtained was oaly temporary. "In such a condition my state was a miserable one, and what was worse was Oat I sulfated from palpita- tion of the bean. Indeed, it anyone spoke to tne laud. dimly I 'lied to think my heart had jumped in:e my moutboucla s hmk would I have. I gradually became flu; my face became deadly whirm.Aod my lips woe' I could not walk many yards without taking a rest and was entirely unable to perform my household duties." "And to what do you attribute your cure, Mrs. Robi- nson 2"</p>
        <p>rn Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People!' " Indeed, mil you tell ins how you first emits to hear of the pills?" " Yes. Aly husband was reading in the paper about the cures made by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People and he said 'Try those pills, there is such e lot talk about.' I decided to do so and got a box. After the first box the palpitation loft me,and after the second box the giddiness and sickly feeling in the morning went, and I could get up in a morning without any un. pleasant feeling. I have had five boxes altogether, arid ever since I commenced caking them I have found a gradual improvement. My work is now a pleasure to ruc ; my washing, which road to be done by otnerti,l can now do myself without any effort, whereas previously I was always sure to catch cold if I attempted to wash a few clothed, but that is not the case now." ." Then you have really felt a decided improvemont since taking the pills ?" " Yes ; and I am getting another box as soon as these are finished. All the neighbours have noticed the differ- ence in me, and the return of strength, and my husband has recommended the Pills to several of his fellow work. men, who have suffered trona palpitation or other aim- rn.-uts."</p>
        <p>During the time the reporter was in the house. Wil- liam Robinson Mrs. RoomsAfe brother-in-law, was present, and he said he could corroborate every word she had said, es he had Observed her condition both before and after taking the pills. As the reporter was Leaving, Mrs. Robinson said she was only too gli.dto.tell of her cure, ag she felt so gre at tul to Dr. Willian7 is Pink Pill; for Pale P.iople, and full permission ta pub!ish these facts in the interests ed the suffering p üblic were granted by the good lady.</p>
        <p>- t(4O-1.41 .tl2‘ I W " Id,</p>
