        <head>WAR lIINGTON.</head>
        <p>WAR lIINGTON.</p>
        <p>RE-PAVING OF SANKEY STREET. Since 2.30 a.m. on Monday morning a number of men have been engaged under the superintendence of Mr. Thomas Longdin, the borough surveyor, jO taking up and relaying a portion of the wood pave" merit in Sankey-street. The wood sets have beerl in use for many years, and have served an excellea purpose in reducing the noise of the traffic. Fresh blocks are not being used, and all that is being doves is to take up the old ones, chip off the tar, an" make the surface level, and place that part uppat- most which previously rested on the ground. BY this means a great deal of expense is saved, ant, the road will be as good as it ever was. It is irr tended to continue the wood pavement te Market gate. THE VERY LATEST DESIGNS nnd COLOURINGS In Millinery, Dress Fab, le+, Silks, Blouses, Sunshades. Umbrellas &amp;a.. &amp;o.—W.T.WILLiams, Sankey-street. and Legh-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>THE COMMANDING OFFICER AND THE DEcBNI ENCAMPMENT. The Volunteer Orders for the week contain the following reference to the recent encampment eV Rhyl:—" The Commanding Officer de,ires to record his approbation of the conduct of all ranks of tbe battalion when in camp this year. It was a verY, trying time, owing to the bad weather. In spite ot this, however. all duties were performed in a credi- tably cheerful and soldierly manner. The regimen' tal staff performed their duties well, and the Quartermaster's Department under Captain Hut- chinson and Quartermaster Ser,eant Potter, vaae Host efficient and contributed to the comfort of the battalion, The band under Mr. Armstrong,was aloe most efficient.</p>
        <p>011/4fB Crams. An exceptionally strong programme is put before the public this week at the Queen-street Circus: Warrington, and Mr. Obmy is to be complimented on the success the artistes have deservedli, achieved. The performing horse "Silver Shoe" and the Plight of Pigeons together gave a varied row attractive turn. Both horse and birds are excellently' trained and perform their tricks with a correctness and intelligence which is almost human. Claucle Ohmy's globe performance is both unique and ski': ful and receives due applause. Sam Watson ae" his performing bantams entertain the audienCe with a deal of fun. The bantams show a rare telligence and go through their tricks an astonishing manner and the audienCe thoroughly applaud their performance. Birde of: this kind are seldom seen in this capacity so all the more credit is due to their trainer. The Daytona are acrobats of great abiliti and skill. Their entertainment is a rapid succes" sion of marvellous feats which almost leave 007 breathless. It is safe to say such a combination 0 strength and agility is seldom seen. Miss Ada elicci ceeds with an exhibition of horse back riding Cruikshanks, the clown, entertains the house Wit, new songs and jokes which take very well. buck-jumping race for a watch each night gives rs opportunity to aspiring riders amongst the yout:t. of the town of showing their ability. Toney Clair gives a fine exhibition on the high trape%s Altogether the programme is a strong and varie., combination of talented artistes, Mr. Ohmy is eloi, dently well aware of the fact that a good Pr gramme each week is his test recommendation.</p>
