        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>E. McEr.noY.—The late Mr. Gladstone made use of the phrase you mention,—"l am sorry to say our old friend Peter has gone to the bad "—at Bank Quay Station on Frkay, June 25th,1888.</p>
        <p>THE LATE TRADES EXHIBITION IN WAR- RINGTON-WHAT WAS REALLY SHORT.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Although at the time of the excitement and great pleasure consequent on the introduction of the Exhibition, everything may have seemed reasonably perfect, worked by experienced and practical hands, I come to the conclusion rather as an after thought that there was something seriously and materially short- and that was the introduction of the animated photo- graphy. With this I feel absolutely certain,seeing the per- fection photography bas arrived at, that the reproduo tion of the interior of Cockhedge Mills,and the iron mills and forges,with the great patent hammers, and without any inconvenience and danger (all in full swing) would have been somewhat an easy matter and the interest of the Exhibition would have been considerably increased thereby.—Yours truly, F. D.</p>
        <p>-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>WANTED AN "AGITATOR."</head>
        <p>WANTED AN "AGITATOR."</p>
        <p>Sir,—After a perusal of "Runcorn Notes " by "a correspondent "in last week's " Examiner," I was very much struck to see a revival of the old question of a road to Weston Point, being emphasised as a necessity ; especially after such glowing statements on the part of your correspondent. about what might be done with a little judicious or "ordinary care in avoiding accident," as instanced by the remarks concerning the fatality at Latchford Locks. Yieur correspondent, although guilty of an apparent inconsistency, seems to grarp the right idea of the question,to my mind,when he states that the read is a necessity ,and makes light of the matter of pro- tecting footways on lock gates. For surely when one is crossing the locks,be is alive or ought to be alive to the extra risk. But in the case of the footpath he naturally thinks he is safe ; whilst, in the case under notice, there is much more danger, because it is not so apparent. I What is really required to secure a road is that some one ' or ether,who does not happen to be a poor working man should lose his life there,when perchance the event would be duly commemorated by the cutting of a suitable road. In the meantime something might. be done by an "agita- tor " if one could be found to come for% ard and chain- pion the cause,wh;ch is a worthy one. Before dismiss- ing the subject, I should also like to record my opinion that the subject of the protection of canals sad water- ways is one that might well be taken in hand by our County Council members with profit to the general pub- lic.—Yours truly, "A HUMANITARIAN."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>STATE OF OUR ROADS.</head>
        <p>THE STATE OF OUR ROADS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—May I call the attenticn of the responsible authorities to the shocking condition of the main roads ithin the borough ? I refer more especially to 'Saute- ford and Wilderepool roads, also Liverpool-road. It has been my misfortune to have to cycle over these roads this week somewhat frequently,and the difference between the main roads on both the Cheshire and Lancashire sides of the town outside the borough, and the condition of the same reads inside the borough, is simply appalling, There is no need of any signpost to tell the stranger when he is " within cur gates," and when he is not. His bike, by it squeaking and strain- ing and the utter impossibility of going any quicker than three miles an hour—except at the risk of breaking his neck through sideelips caused by the abominable holes which exist by the thousand—will soon inform trim where he is. Why should this disgraceful condition of things be allowed to continue so long ? I remember about a year ago several letters appeared in your paper upon this same subject, but to all appear- ances nothing substantial has been done to bring about any improvement. I would suggest that a meeting of all who are interested in the condition of the reads—all owners of horses, especially team owners aad cyclists— should be held with a view to some pressure being brought to bear upon the Corporation. lam sure cur worthy Mayor—of whom we certainly expected great things in the way of improved roads—cannot possibly be aware of the abominable state into which the main roads have got,or he would see this department,of which he is chairman, administered with much greater advan- tage to the community over which he presides than is at present the case. Hoping abler pens than mine will follow up this question until some appreciable Improve- ment is obtained.—Yours Ssc.,</p>
        <p>CYCLIST.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>WARRINGTON TRAINING COLLEGE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TRAINING COLLEGE.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO MISS GODDARD.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the annual meeting ef the Reading Guild of former students was held in the College at 3 o'cleck p.m. The occasion was a special one, because the pre- sence of such a large body of former students furnished a favourable opportunity for the presentation of a testi- monial to Miss Goddard, whe, in consequence of a seri- ous accident, recently resigned her post as lady superin- tendent, after having been on the cellege staff for a period of forty years. At three o'clock the members of the guild and a few other ex-students,to the rumber of 200 assembled in the students' sitting-room, where they were joined by the Rev. Canon Willis (rector of War- rington) and Mrs. Willis,the Rev.Canen Penrhyn (rector of Winwick) and Mrs. Penrhyin, Dr. and Miss Ethel White, Miss Watkins,representing Dr. Watkins,Newton- le-Willows (a member ot the committee and a former se- cretary), the Rev. Morley Stevenson (principal), and other friends, all of whom heartily congratulated Miss Goddard on being sufficiently recovered to undertake a journey to Warrington in elder to to present. The PRINCIPAL (the Rev. Morley Stevenson) gave a short address, pointing out the importance of the occa- sion, and explaining a few alterations which had been made in the usual routine. The most important of these was that the annual meeting of the Reading Guild was henceforward to take the place of the former triennial re- union:which had assumed such enormous porport ions as to be unmanageable. Us, therefore, hoped that all those present would make the fact known to as many former students as possible, asking them to send iu their name:, that they might be enrolled as members of the guild. References to various facts of interest, chiefly to old students, followed. Then came the most important part of the function. The principal spoke of the special object of the meeting ; namely, to testify to their affection tor, and the greatness of their 'tics in the resignation of Miss Goddard, who, during her forty years of residence, had won the love and esteem of all who had passed through the College. He then enumerated the gifts, which were as follows :—A silver teapot, case of silver teaspoons and sugar-tongs and a cheque for £5O from former students a silver cream-jug and gugat basin to match the teatoot from present students ; an easy chair and a cheque for £ll from past and present members of the Staff ; a silver butter dish and knife from the Principal and Mrs. Stevenson ; case of silver salt cellars from the Rev. A. B. Bater (lec-urer) and Mrs. Rater. Canon PENRISYN, a member of the committe?, in the unavoidable absence of the Secretary, the Dean of M in- ' cheater, presented the gifts from former students. He made a kindly speech, in which ho remarked that he hal been indirectly or directly connected with the Col- lege during the whole term of Miss Goddard'a residence. and uttered hearty wishes that she might have many happy years in which to enjoy the gifts which be bad the pleasure of asking her to accept. Miss FrantetAN next presented the 4ifta from past and present members of the staff, and in doing so remarked that at first the former were not asked to contribute ; but some had begged to be allowed to do so, in order that they might show their regard, and when the re- mainder were communicated with tney responded most heartily. The PRINCIPAL then presented his and MN Stevenson's gift; and on behalf of the students, MiS4 Florence Price, "the head girl," offered Miss Goddard a beautiful bou- quet of white and yello iv ft )wers, tied with oil gold rit- bon—the College colour. Canoe WILLIS, also a member of the committee added a few words expressive of hie pleasure iu being able to take part in the preeentatims; and after a fP w words of thanks from Miss Goddard, who was too much affected to make a speech, the gathoring diapers 'd. Tea was sirced on the lawn soon after four o'clock, and a brief ,bright service in the College Chapel followed at which the Principal gave a short address and a collec- tion was made in aid of the bursary fund for supplying help to necessitous students. The rest of the evening was spent in various games—tennis,croquet,bowls—and in conversation between reunited friends,many of whom find such occasions the only opportunity of meeting thoie with whom they were closely connected in their student days.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON HIGHWAY' BOA.RD. A meeting of the above Board was hela at the Bewley- street Chambers on Wednesday afternoon with Captain Withington chairman. There were also present Messrs. James Houghton, J. Clare. R. T. Lowe, Ball, %V. Fairclough. W. Rigby. Henr7 Thornton,and Robert Gar- nett with Mr. E. A. White (clerli),and Mr. J. Thompson (surveyor).</p>
        <p>TRH BOARD AND PENBETH. Mr. Garnett said he shauld be gltd if Mr. Thompson could sue his way to improve the condition of the road clue by the Penketh tannery as it was in a bad condi- Con, being beth narrow and dirty.—Mr. ThomPson Said he did not see how they could manage it as the new road in course of construction would take up all their money. The Clerk said the amount of the contribution from Penketh was £248 183 101.—Mr. Garnett said if the matter was not dealt with then, it would not bP done when they were out of ()Mos.—The VChaim," said the matter would then be handed over to the Rural Dis- trict Council. The subject then dropped.—The Sursey-,r in his report stated that preparations had been made for the Sankey new road.</p>
        <p>FINANCE. The Clerk reported that the balance in band Was £220 31. 81 Penketh, Croft, Cuerdley and Culcheth were the only townships that had paid the June calk. This was all the business, ana the rneetinj was brought to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JUNE 17, 198</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JUNE 17, 198</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>POLICE CASES.</p>
        <p>At the Earlestown Police Station on Monday morning before Mr. C.B.F. Borron,Ellen McCormick a married woman of Fairclough-street, Earlestown, was charged with being drunk and incapable in Viaduct-street on Saturday night, and was fined 2s 64 and costs.—Ernest William Holden of no fixed abode for Bleeping in a haystack on Mr. Alder- son's farm on Sunday was fined 23 6d and costs.— Edward Sutton, collier of Crow-lane, Newton, for being drunk in Market-street, Earlestown on Sun- day night was fined 2s 6d and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>NARROW ESCAPE FROM DROWNING.</head>
        <p>NARROW ESCAPE FROM DROWNING.</p>
        <p>It has transpired that about the same time that the boy France was drowned last week, at Earles- town another little fellow named Hall living with his parents on the canal bank had a narrow escape. He was playing near the Common locks, through which a boat had just passed, when by some means he fell in. An alarm was raised and Mr. Sankey's housekeeper living at the lock house,promptly pro- ceeded to his rescue and with the assistance of another woman succeeded in bringing him to terra firma in an exhausted condition.</p>
        <p>HOPS OF EARLISTOWN LODGE, LO.G.T. On Monday evenine the above lodge held their meeting, C.T. Bro. J. Taylor presiding. Bro. Barker real various communications, and an invitation from the Bonehill Branch Lodge, Latchford, to visit them on the 4th July was accepted. The lodge decided to invite the Ashton Excelsior Lodge on June 7th. Bro. H. Williams thanked the members for their kindness in falling in with the demonstra- tion at Haydock on Saturday. A programme fol- lowed and Bro. H.Williams presided,and the follow- ing rendered their assistance :—Piano solo, Bro. W. Willis ; sons. Sister Clayton; song, Bro. Stanley ; song,Sister Walton •, dulcimer solo, Bro. Hobbs ; and thanks were accorded to the entertainers. Bro. Wakefield received a prize, offered for getting the most members in the Order.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>ST, HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST, HELENS.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED THEFT PROM A PAWNSHOP.</p>
        <p>At the S. Helens Police Court on Tueeday, Margaret Lawrenson, 37, Fox-street, was charged with stealing a skirt, valued at Is. 3d., from the shop of Samuel Ball. pawnbroker, College-street. The article was missed after the prisoner had been in the shop. She denied all knowledge of it, and pleaded not guilty. The bench said the case would be dismissed. A YOUTH'S FOOLHARDINESS.</p>
        <p>- - - John Mooney, a youth, of Sandfield-crescent, was charged at St. Helens on Monday, before Messrs. W. Tyrer and J. Green, with a breach of the Coal Mines Regulation Act by taking six matches and four cigarettes into a coal mine on the 3rd inst. The Chairman said Mooney was very greatly to blame. They would, how- ever, deal with him leniently, and fine him 53. and costs or seven days. Mg. SETON KARR, M.P., AS A GoprEa. Mr. FL Seton-Karr, the member for St. Helens, and Mr. J. Leslie Wanklyn, the member for Bradford, played off their match in the semi-final round of the Parliamen- tary handicap on the Prince's Club course at Mitcham, London, on Monday. Mr. Soton-Karr conceded two strokes and won by five holes up and 4 to play. The final will not be played until October. It will probably be decided in Scotland. PAINFUL ACCIDENT TO A ST. HELENS BOY.</p>
        <p>William Clarke, a boy of thirteen, who lives at 2, Greenough-street, St. Helens, was admitted to the hospi- tal, last Friday afternoon suffering from severe burns to the left eye and face. The lad was employed at Megsrs. Cannington and Shaw's bottle works, when by some means or other a quantity of molten lead fell on his face, burning his left eye and the side of his face. He was attended by Dr. Clarke, and now lies in a critical state.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>PROPOSED FISH . MARKET. FOR. ST. HELENS,</head>
        <p>PROPOSED FISH . MARKET. FOR. ST. HELENS,</p>
        <p>_ At the monthly meeting of the Parka and Markets Committee of the St. Helens Corporation, a requeat made by a deputation of fishmongers for the construction of a fish market on land forming part of the Corporation depot in St. Mary's-street was considered. The Surveyor (Mr. Broom) did not think it would bo possible for sufficient space to be devoted at the depot for a fish market.—The Town Clerk said they could not grant a place for a fish market until the fishmongers came to a definite arrangement aa to the numb rof stalls that would certainly be taken. —The matter was referred to the sub-committee for more definite arrangement. COMING FROM A WAKE IN ST. HELENS. _ _ -</p>
        <p>_ At the St. Helens Police Court on Monday, James Barnes, aged sixteen ; Henry M'lntee, aged thir- teen ; William M‘lntee, aged fifteen ; James Wlntep, aged eleven; and John Parr,aged ten.iiving in Frederick- street and College-3treet,were charged with stealing cur- rants from the bakery of Mr. Lionel Swift, at the corner of Frederick-street and College-street. The boys pleaded guilty, and Henry Wlntee said they had all been to a wake at Dolan's' in Frederick-street.—Mrs. M'lntee said she had put the boys to bed, but they had got up after she had gone Co bed.—The Chairman (Mr. Fryer) repri- manded the boys severely,and discharvid them, urging the mothers mesant to give them each a good whipping.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>MARRIAGE OF A ST._ _HELENS CURATE.</head>
        <p>MARRIAGE OF A ST._ _HELENS CURATE.</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. E. Colegrove,M.A., who for the past five years has been curate of St. f.l.lens Parish Church. and who was formerly curate of St. Paul's, Plinee's Park, Liverpool, was married on Tuesday, at Christ Church, Didsbary, to Miss Evans, a Staff.3rdshire lady. The ceremony was performed by the Rev. J. W. Willink, vicar of St. Helens. Miss Dora Agard,of Didsbury,cousin of the bride, acted as bridesmaid, and he Rev. C. E. Warner, vicar of Bishop's CaStle,and lately curate at St. Helens,was the groomsman. Since his appointment to the curacy of St. Belens,the bridegrocm has successfully carried on the important work connected with S'Llliary's Mission Schools,and the congregation and day and Sun- day school workers intend shortly to present hitu with a cheque for a substantial amount.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>VIADUCT INSTITUTE SCIENCE AND ART C 1 ASSES.</head>
        <p>VIADUCT INSTITUTE SCIENCE AND ART C 1 ASSES.</p>
        <p>The following results of the examinations h.lll in con- nection with the above classes recently, have come to hand :</p>
        <p>SOCIETY OF ARTS—BOOK-KEEPING. John Graham, h P Kidd, A Lyons. It A Robinson, 2nd class. Id Bradshaw, E Dickons, T 11 Gee, Margaret Roberts, J Sankey, and John Yorke, 3rd class. UNION OF LANCASHIRE AND CHESHIRE INSTITUTES. HANDWRITING. First Cis s :—Alfreil Jones, Hannah J Price, Richard 41 Robinson. Second Class i—George Bunting, Arthur J Dendv, Ernest Dickens, John Graham, let Jeseph Green, Colin McHardv,Walter J Monks,John if Newman.D in it Owen, E tith E Partington, Alec Pim'', Ma"garet Roberts, Minnie Roberts, Tnomas Seddon, Bortha Smith, Benjimin Southern, William Stephons, John Walton. 'third Class A Cdaklev, Tom Cxko, Frederick Gannon, John W Gilmour, Esther Grirnshaw, Charles W Hindley, Fred Hutchings, George E Jackson, William Jackson, Emma Makeloam, Eveline Marrow, Harold P Mayor, Wfiliam Parry, Edwin Parry, Robert Harry Rylance. The following students worked the wrung paper in handwriting, thus disqualifying the.ns- selves from receiving marks, but Elementary Certificates will he awarded as follows :—First Class :--EiWard 0 Pierce. Second Clase :—Frederick Bolton. AliC B Green,WilliamL.Haynes,Arthur Parsons,Robert Strange, Walter Strange. Toird Class. :7-John Watkinson.</p>
        <p>DRESSMAKING. FirA Clasa :—Mary Birks, Mary T Bunting. Se.lond Clans :—Gertrude Chisholm, Gertrude Cooper, Annie Graham, Viz tho.th Liversage, Annie P3onington, Mary 81/on, Bertha Smith, Eleanor S Williamson. Third Class :—Elizabeth E. Cartwright. BOOK-KEEPING. Distinction :—Richard A Robinson. First Cl* Themasss : II Gee, John Graham,George E Jackson, Harold P Kidd, AP3ort Lyons, Edith E Partington. Margaret Roberts, Isaac Sankey, William Stephens, John York. Second Class : Arthlr J Deady, Ernest Dickens, Shir- lev M Finning, Harry Makeham, William P Singleton. Third Class :—Flertert Bradshaw, George Buntinc,,, Richard A Foster, John W Gilmour, Lewis Harvey, Joseph Flodgkinson, John E Latham, Daniel R Owen, Charles F Porter, Hannah J Price, C J Thorpe, Maria Worsley. SFIORTEIAND. Third Cass :—John H Latham, Minnie Roberts,Chris- topher Spencer. E. Pass :—John W Gilmour, Lilian Longworth, Mary Longworth,Joho E Muscroft. ENGLISH.</p>
        <p>--- First Clasq:—Georq,e Hunting ,Walter J Monks,Richard A Robinson. Secnicl Class :—Thomas E Clough, Helen Cruickshank, Mary Lonaccorth, Erntna Makeham,Harry Makeham, John Ei Newman, Charles F Porter.</p>
        <p>IfEMLRicArtLEI'm-SAWN; AT LIVERPOOL.—An in. quest was held On Wednesday night on the body of a boy named Williamson, who Was drowned at Seaforib on Menday. Williamson and two others were bathing in a treacherous channel, and cri.,[l to people on snore for help. An electrician named Wheeler swam to the rescue, and brour,ht two of the boys to land. One of them was itverisible, but Wheeler, placing him on his own chest and swimming no h's back, saved the bar's life,and also another boy. The third boy was dr)wned. The Coroner and jury warmly commended Wheeler, and in reply to the representations of the jury said he would place his gallantry before the Royal Hun3arte Society. A Swansea telegram says that the non-asaociatvd colliery owners in the weitern district have unanimously agreed to form an association ot steam and anthracite coal-owners, and also to reist any further demands tor an Increase of wages.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>I THE DROWNING FATALITY.</head>
        <p>I THE DROWNING FATALITY.</p>
        <p>INCIDENT AT THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>On :Ifonday afternoon during the progress of the funaral c.rtege in connection with the burial of the boy France who was drowned last week, an unfor- tunate accident happened. The hearse was drawn by two fins black horses, and on the way to the cemetery, near the Wargrave schools, one of the horses stood still and could not be persuaded to move. On examining the animal it was found to be ill, and it was at once unharnessed, and with diffi- culty removed to Mrs. Waywell'a stable. A veteri- nary surgeon was sent for hut the horse died in a few minates. The hearse had to be taken on to the cemetery by one horse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>VIE INQUEST. MEN'S UN MANLINESS.</head>
        <p>VIE INQUEST. MEN'S UN MANLINESS.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse, coroner, held an incosst at the Griffin Hotel, Earleatown, on Friday, relative to the death of Sydney France,aged six years who was drowned in the Sankey Canal at Eirlestown on Thorsday. Edward Owen France, father of deceased, living at 28, Rathhone-street, Earlestownoaid he last saw his son alive on Thursday at five minutes to two. Fie then went to his work, and en leaving some people told him that his son had been drowned, and at that time the body had been recovered from the water.</p>
        <p>Albert Edwards. aged six years, said he and Sydney France were playing on the bank on Thursday and had gathered some rushes. Deceased let some fall into the water, and the rushes floated away from the bank. He stepped down to reach them and in doing so he fell into the water. There were four or five men near who stood and looked on, and one of them said that he could not get deceased out as he could not swim. Witness went borne and askad his mother if his father was in as he would get him out. Tne mother replied that his father was net in, and witness then told her that Sydney France was drowned.</p>
        <p>In reply to a question by the Coroner, P.C. Asp len said the boy gave him that statement, and acting upon it he had tried to find the men, but could not. John Richards, of the lock-house, said he heard of the occurrence about five minutes past five on the Thursday afternoon. He hurried down, and reached the spot half an hour after hearing of the unfortunate incident. Deceased was in only abeut four feet of water, and noar the side. He must have entered the water head fore- most and got fast in the mud. and probalily did not rise again. When wanes.; got there two men were standing on the bank. He asked them where the deceased was, and they pointed out the exact spot. The men, who were strangers to him, did not sty they saw the boy fall in. Witness believed a man need not have gone more than knee deep to have effected the rescue of the child. Tito Coroner : What a shame ! .</p>
        <p>.--..-...- . Addressing the police officer presmt,the Coroner asked " Have you any idea who these men were ?'' P.C, Aspden : No, sir. We have done all we could to find out who the mu were in order to get them here as witnesses.</p>
        <p>Alfred France, the ten year old brother of the deceased, gaid he was close to the soot on Thursday where his brother fell in. Richard Thorpe, witness's companion, was pulling off his jacket to go into the water, but a mart pulled him back and would not let him go. One man threw his jacket into the water, but did not go in himself. There were a lot of lads bathing and swimming about at the other side of the canal. The jury returned a verdict that death was due to accidental drowning, the foreman remarking that they were poor men who were about at the time.</p>
        <p>• mTenb.e Coroner : P:rhaps you ought not to call them</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>[BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT]. If, as was stated at the inquest on the boy France who was drowned lash week, it is true that men stood on the canal ban E and did not do any- thing to rescue the lad, the strictures of the coroner were not by ally means too strong. It must be remembered however, that the statements were made by the children who were near the spot, and perhaps some cauzion should be exercised in accept- ing what they say. But if it is quite correct and true that such inhumanity was displayed i, it s to be hoped that the men concerned were not EarleBtown men. It is inconceivable that such callousness could be shown by natives, and as various rumours have been circulated snipe the event more or less calculated to contradict each other, one is led to hope that the children were mistaken. All the same, perhaps the remarks of the coroner may not be without good effect.</p>
        <p>It is a matter of surprise to me that the cases of death by drowning at this particular spot have not been more numerous. For children it is a veritable death trap, but what attraction there is for the youngsters to congregate at this spot, as they do, pass :s my comprehension. On the day on which the boy was drowned, a policeman had driven a group of children away who were playing by the water's edge, in fact a girl 13 years old was setting a very dangerous example to the younger children by sitting on the bank of the canal and dangling her feet. in the water, It is high time a fence was put up at this particular spot, and it would be as well if instructions were issued for the bridge which crosses the canal to be locked all day on Suaday. The great source of attraction,! think, is the swing bridge, but if this was locked, it might to some extent be a preventive against danger.</p>
        <p>lam pleased to notice that in spite of the poor tarn out last year, the Committed intend to proceed with the Cycle parade this year. The °llea is a good one, and as there are several hundreds of cyclfsts in Earlestown and Newton there should not be the slightest fear of failure. There seems to dwell deep down in the breasts of Earlestown people a prejudice against such displays ; they are ever ready to turn out to witness them, but to take part in them is quite a different thing. Such prejudice does not exist in other towns,where the parades are entered into with enthusiasm, and I hope this year to see a first-class procession made up almost entirely of Earlestown and Newton cyclists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>NATIONAL UNION OF GASWORKFRS AND LABOURERS.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL UNION OF GASWORKFRS AND LABOURERS.</p>
        <p>MEETING AT EtiItLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Wedni=eday evening a public open air meeting for the purpose of taking toe preliminary steps towards forming a branch of the ablve society, was held on the Market ground, elialeetown Mr. C. E. Brown of War- rington acted as chairman, and thera was a fair gather- inn of M9O.</p>
        <p>- The Chairman briefly opened the meeting by a des- cription of the objects of the Union. and he was follower by Mr. J. R. Clynes, organising secretary ot the Lanca- shire District, and Sscretary to the Oldham Trades Council, who in the course of his observations stated tbat the Won was the largest unskilled Trades Union Society in the klngdom, having a membership of 40,000. They had 45 branehea in the Lancaehiro district store id over the different towns. All these districts were welded together and amalgamated to make up the National Union of G.sworKers. In the case of labourers who had to etiVt from one job to another and from place to place according to the conditions which governed them, they would se m it was necessary to join a society which was open to receive any class of labourer no matter what he might be worhing at or where he might be workieg.They did not expect them to make a new start that night in the way of making a n'w Union, as the Union was al- ready made, all they wanted them to do was to form a branch.</p>
        <p>Mr. Peter Curran, general organiser. London, also addressed the meeting, entering into the position of the Union, and quoting stAistios as to Its finan 'es. and showing the benefit labourers would derive by joining its ranks.</p>
        <p>It was afterwards decilel to form a branch of the Union, to be called tee Etrleatown District.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>DRAW OF A LIBERAL M.P.</head>
        <p>DRAW OF A LIBERAL M.P.</p>
        <p>-- The death occurred on Monday morning, at .Dur- ham, of Mr. Matthew Fowler, M.P. for the City of Durham. Mr. Fowler for the past six months had suffered from a malignant growth in his throat, which had prevented his taking any solid food. He, howe 7er, felt himself well enough to return to Parliament after Easter for a few days, but was obliged to return home, and since then he bad never been out. The disease progressed rapidly, and the honourable gentleman passed quietly away at a quarter to six. The deceased was the eldest son of I the late Alderman Fowler, of Durham, and was brought up to his father's business of a grocer. He had always taken a prominent part in local public ! life, and since 1876 had been almost continually a member of the Durham Corporation. Mr. Fowler, who was 53 ,ears of age, was a widower, and leaves no family. The deceased was a very kind-hearted and charitable man, and was much beloved in Dur- ham, especially among tue poor, and his death will cause deop and genuine regret. He had sat for Durham City as a Liberal Home Ruler since 1892. In 1895 he was elected by 1,110 votes against 1,107 recorded for the Hon. A. Elliott, Liberal Unionist, thus gaining a majority of only three votes. The figures, as di- eared at, the noll, were—Fowler, 1,111; Elliott, 1,110, giving Mr. Fowler a majority of one. but on a recount this was iccreasod to three.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>DEATH AND FUNERAL OF MR. THOMA.t.:I CLARKE, ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>DEATH AND FUNERAL OF MR. THOMA.t.:I CLARKE, ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>A gloom was cast over Runcorn and district when the sad news of the sudden and unexpected death of Mr. Thomas Clarke, manager of the Red Oxide Works, Warrington-road, St. Helens, became known on Saturday morning. And general sympathy was freely expressed on all hands; for Mr. Clarke—who was a native of Runcorn, and managed the Red Oxide Works in that town before the cutting of the Manchester Ship Canal compelled the Company to select another site for their extensive works, at St. Helens—was generally respected by all classes with whom he came into contact, either in.business or in the social circle in which he moved. His demise took place under circumstances which make the sad occurrence the more shocking owing to the suddenness of its character. It appears that Mr. Clarke, who was only in his 43rd year, and a robust healthy looking man, had taken to cycling. About 7 o'clock on Cie Friday night he went with three or four friends to Rainford. When at the Wheat Sheaf they had a little refreshment and Mr. Clarke partook of a bottle of ginger-beer. Shortly afterwards on the return journey he complained of feeling unwell, and a little brandy was obtained for him. When near the Abbey Inn, Denton's Green, however, Mr. Clarke suddenly shouted out,and sank to the ground. On being raised he was found to be lifeless. Mr. Clarke leaves a wife and three children and universal sympathy is expressed with them in their sad bereavement, especially al his present wife be- comes a widow after only a few months of married life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>On Monday afternoon the funeral took place at the Runcorn Cemetery, Greenway-road, and was Largely attets led. The corpse arrived by road from St. Helens, and punetuilly at 230 left the house of Mr. Guy (deceased's father-in-law) at Church-street. wending its way to its last resting place, followed by those who desired to pay their last tribute of respect to the memory of Mr. Clarke. Amongst those present were Messrs. F J Norman (Old Quay Works, Runcorn), Duncan Watson (Mc Kechnie's Works, St. Helens), W Briers (White Cross Colliery, St. Helens), 11 Baxter, Rev. W Clayton, and Messrs. S Duckworth, and A Pearson (Methodist Free Church, St. Helens) ; Messrs. Walker, Gandy, U. White, T Parry, H O'Neil, F Clarke, (Methodist Free Church, Runcorn), Messrs, R. Row- land, W Moulton, W Jameson, R Rhone, 0 Maloney, G. Turner, J Ford, W Colquitt, W Shaw, D Howard T page. w Newton,J G Wilson,J Weedall,J Brother- ton, J Peel, W Chadwick, J Geoghen, F Feeney, M Fallon, J Fallon, Jeacock, R Fryer, and J Owen, 11 S Pears (employees at the Red Oxide Works, St. Helens); Messrs. a W Yates, J Holford (Winsford) and J Brown, &amp;c. The mourners were: Master Newton Clarke (son),Messrs. James Clarke and Dan Clarke (brothers), S Oily (uncle), F Guy (fatner-in- law); John Wilson,Joseph Newall,T Moores, S Guy, W Guy, F Guy, J Curson. and Taylor (brothers- in-law); Wilson Newell, Joseph Newell, J H Clarke; James, Thomas,Arthur, Nevvton,and Joseph Clarke; George, Joseph, and Tom Moores; Tom and k' arold Curzon, :Master Curzon, Master Guy Taylor, Tom Bowden, Charles Kineton and J Clarke (nephews). —Messrs. Moulton, Newton, Rowland, Maloney, Jameson and Weedall acted as the bearers.—The funeral service was impressively conducted by the Rev. W. Clayton and the Rev. S. Wright ( Methodist Free Church, Runcorn), the latter of ' whom feelingly referred to the untimely death of Mr. Clarke in a short address. Floral wreaths and other favours of respect were sent by numerous friends and workmen of the deceased, the coffin be- ing literally hidden in a wealth of bloom.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>TIMBERING OF ST. HELENS MINES.</head>
        <p>THE TIMBERING OF ST. HELENS MINES.</p>
        <p>WARNING AGAINST PATCHING OLD TIMBER. Mr. F. A. Jones, deputy county coroner, held an in- quest at St. Helens Town Hall, on Monday, on the body of Martin Murray, aged thirty-leven years,of 41,Wright- street, St. Helens, who on Friday was killed by a fall of roof at the St. Helens Collieries. Mr. Matthews, assist. eat Government inspector, was present, and the St. Helens Collieries Company was represented by Mr. J. 0. Swift, solicitor. James Wills, of 34, Devon-street, said he worked with deceased, On Friday morning in going to his work he passed through the shoat where the accident happened. He examined the road every morning, and saw that it was propped all right. At nine o'clock that morning Murray left the working-place with a full box of coal. Immediately he had gone witness heard a noise, and on running out into the roadway he found that the roof had fallen about twen:y yards; away and buried de- ceased. While he was away getting help another fall of roof occureed about three or four yards away. By Mr. Matthews : The road was propped and barred. It was gettine low at that point. On the Thursday the boxes caurtit the roof in passing, but he altered that. The props had been crushed down a little by the roof on top lof them,but he did not think there was anything alarm- ing. If the road had not been safe that morning,it was witness's duty to repair it, but did not notice any de- fect. He had seen Fireman Roper that morning, and he had left his name and the date on the spade to show that he had inspected the place. Deceased did not com- plain of the state of the road. Samuel Roper, night fireman. said 'that he inspected the road and roof at four o'clock on Friday morning,and it was all right. It was not a good roof. There was only ea) bar knocked out. Mr. Matthews : But that one bar left an 'opming 7 feet 6 inches wide. The Inspector proceeded to cross- examine witness as to whether the last accident of the same kind at these pits was in his district, and referred to some suggestions made by Mr. Hall. Mr. Swift objected to any reference being made to a previous accident or to Mr. Hall. The Coronet said Mr. Matthews was entitled to ask the qoestions. Mr. Swift : He may be entitled to ask it, but it still may be A little with the view of prejudicing this parti- cular individual. Mr. Matthews knows that the last accident was on a different road, in a different district, and under a different fireman altogether. The C)toner : A man is entitled to ask a questionond it does not matter What the effect of it maybe. _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift : It is a little indecent on-the part of a Government official to ask it of the witness in the box. The Coroner : Ido not think so. Mr. Swift: Mr. Matthews has ben down the pit to day, and he might have ascertained then. Mr. Mr.tthows (to witneos).—l tliink Mr. Hall had to complain of the last accident. Mr. Swift : Ido not cars a fi; for what Mr. Hall</p>
        <p>Mr. Matthews : I wanted tc kra,)w whether it was in this inn's district.</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift: You might have ascertained when you were down the pit. Mr. Mathews : The man was not down the pit at the time. The Coroner : I think Mr. Matthews is right in ask- ing the question. Mr. Swift : Fla may bs technically right. The Coroner : Legally right. Mr. Swift: Well, legally. Ho may be le;ally right, but he may be indecent. The Coronel: Well, I decide that he is legally right. Mr. Swift: We know the law as well as the deputy- coroner.</p>
        <p>The Coroner : You must to cmtent with my judg- ment, Mr. Swift, _</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift : Ido not disputa your judgrnant. Mr. Matthews was proceeding to make the uSual statement to the jory— Mr Swift : If Mr. Matthmws is going to maks any statement I think he ought to be sworn,and that I ought to have an opportunity of examining him. I object to these irregular statements.</p>
        <p>The Coroner : It is quite within my jurisdiction to permit it, but if Mr. Matthews likes to be sworn I am quite willing. Mr. Matthews : I would prefer to be sworn. Mr. Matthews then on oath said h 3 had inspected the place that morning. The bars at the place where the accident happened were a little lqw, and he found that some of them in that particular part had been crushed a little, and had to ho "jack propped;" that means they ' had put another prop in on either the upper or lower side, as it might be required. A large fall, eleven yards long, had occurred three bars away from the fall that killed Murray. In his opinion it any of the bars ap• peared to show that the roof had crushed and partly broken them new bars should be set,and the road relined, especially when weight was hanging over the bars, and it was a road where the men were going to and fro con- tinually during the day. There sh:adil not be too much patching of the timber. New timber sho Id be put up instead 01 trying to patch up the old timber, that was 1 the point he wanted to bring forward. Mr. Hall was very anxious to see the roads kept safe. The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death," and endorsed Mr. Matthewe' suggestion that new timber should be put in wherever the old timber was crushed by the roof.</p>
        <p>YOU FIND IT EVERYWHERE. CUTICURa now meets the traveller at every point in the civilised world. It seems like running across an old friend in a strange land to find displays of CUTICURA and Cuilmite Soio in the shop windows of Melbourne and Sydney. Yoko- hama, and Tokio, Hong Kong and Shanghai, Calcutta and tiombay, Constantinople and Cairo, Kim berley and Johannesburg, Berlin and Vienna, Paris and London. Civilization secros to march with CUTICURA in the front rauka.</p>
        <p>WARFINGTON.</p>
        <p>PERSONAL.</p>
        <p>The R3v. E. T. Ree-d-,--14;;..--lieed, and Miss Monk I have arranged to sail from Montreal on June 18th in the as. Dominion,of the Dominion Line,and hope to reach Liverpool on or about June 28th. Mr. Reed expects to be in Warrington in time for Walking Day. The Dominion is a fine new steamer of 6,000 tons, and this will be her second trip from Canada to Liverpool. The ex-Chief Constable, Mr. Samuel Hunt, has been lying seriously ill at his residence in Salisbury- Street for some five or six weeks. We are pleased Ilto state that an improvement has now set in, and it is hoped he will shortly be restored to good health. Mrs. Hunt has also been ill for a month ilpast, and in her case also the medical attendant's report is of a re-assuring character.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>RETURN OP THE MILITIA TO WARRINGTON. – – – –</head>
        <p>RETURN OP THE MILITIA TO WARRINGTON. - - - -</p>
        <p>The members of the 3rd South Lancashire Regi- ment of Militia, returned to Warrington from Altcar on Saturday morning, where they had been for the past four weeks undergoing their annual training. The weather was at times very trying, but the men returned in good health. A start was made shortly after eight o'clock, and the battalion under Colonel Spratt, arrived at the Barracks at ten a.m. __</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>MEETING OF SPIRITUALISTS.</head>
        <p>MEETING OF SPIRITUALISTS.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon, in the absence of Miss Smith, who through indisposition had been unable to arrive in time for the meeting, Mr. J. Pemberton delivered an address on " What is mau ? " In the evening Miss Smith occupied the platform and spoke on the question " What has Spiritualism done for humanity ?" after which she gave clairvoyance. On Monday evening,Sliss Smith devoted the whole evening to clairvoyance and psychometry. PREMIER CYCLES, special terrna.—C/Admoro, Agent Warrington. P.S.A. AT THR Y.M.C.A. A most successful meeting was held on Sunday. The hall.in spite of the fine weather,vras full. Mr. Gregson, the vice-president, was in the chair. The singing was very hearty, and Mr. Annan rendered Liddle's " Abide with me "in good style, Mr. Frank Hickman supplying the musical accompaniment. Mr. Henry Taylor, polies court missionary, offered prayer. The address was delivered by the Rev. J. P. Matthews, his subject being " Man ! How to measure him." His address was greatly appre- ciated. 1.0.G.T. TICISPHRANCE MISSION LODGE. The weekly session of the above was held in the Academy-street Temperance Hall on Wednesday. Bro. Roberts, C.T., presided over a good attendance. Bro. J. Sheath gave a report of the Convention meeting which was held in Warrington on Satur- day. Bros.O'Brien, Roberts, and Mortimore spoke On the !von which was adopted. The lodge was closed in due form by Bro. Roberts, C.T. EXCELSIOR and NEW ROW CYCLE 3.— aiRELND Ironmonger, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>1.0.G.T. No. I-ComvnsTrom S.W. LANCS.</head>
        <p>1.0.G.T. No. I-ComvnsTrom S.W. LANCS.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above Was held in the Academy-street Mission Room, Warrington, on Saturday. Bro. Roberts, C.C. C., presided over a good attendance of representatives. The roll of officers was called, after which the various com- mittees were formed. A communication was read from Bro. J. H. Punshon, St. Helens, resigning his position as convention secretary. The resignation was accepted and Bro. Lodge,Ashton,was appointed to the post. It was decided to hold the July session at Ashton. The meeting then adjourned for tea. On the resumption of busiaess, Bro. Williams gave a report of the Guide Committee, and moved that the convention publish a guide similar to that pub- lished by the District Lodge. After a long discus- sion it was decided to refer the matter back to the committee. A vote of thanks to the Warrington members terminated the proceedings,</p>
        <p>EXCITING-SCENE AT PRESCOT.—A LION TAMER ATTACKED. On Saturday evening an event happened which caused considerable consternation among the public attending the Prescot annual fair.On the fairground was located Chadwick's (No. 2)menagerie,and about nine o'clock, the lion tamer, Alphonse, entered the cage of a lioness for the purpose of carrying out the customary performance. The lioness at once sprang upon the tamer, and floored him at a hound. The tamer thwarted the animal's efforts for a time, and the attendants in charge used hot bars and pikels, but the lioness inflicted severe injuries upon Alphonse,making a daep gash in the thigh.fle man- aged to get clear of the cage,and medical aid was at once sent for. Dr. Cossens put stitches in the lacera- tion,which was of a most serious nature, and the injured man is doing as well as can be expected. The same animal, on the 27th December last, wheti at Antwerp, killed its then tamer, Captain Virgain. As can well be imagined, the incident caused great consternation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>DEATH AND FUNRICAL OF MRS. WALLINGTON.</head>
        <p>DEATH AND FUNRICAL OF MRS. WALLINGTON.</p>
        <p>The death occurred on Friday evening last, of Mrs. Wallington, of Hill Crest, Lower Walton, relict of the late Mr. George Wallington, formerly a member of the Warrington Town Council. The de- ceased lady was 83 years of age. She had been con- nected with Warrington for a great number of years, and at the time of her death she was the old- est member associated with Wycliffa Congregation Church.—The funeral took place at 11111 Ciiffe on Tuesday afternoon, and it was attended by over 40 relatives, including eons and daughters, grand- children, and great grand-children. She was borne to the grave by six of her grand.sons. Mr. Alfred Corney conducted the funeral service. At the close the hymn, ''o God our help in ages past" was sung.</p>
        <p>NW RAPID CYCLES.—DARLAND, 51, Bridge-etreet, Warrington. A SITCCESsVIIL JUMBLE SALE.</p>
        <p>In Wycliffe Hall on Saturday afternoon and even- ing a jumble sale in connection with the forthcom- ing Wycliffe bazaar was conducted. There was a large attendance, more especially in this afternoon, when the bulk of the articles offered for sale were disposed of. The articles had been contributed by members of the congregation, and they consisted chiefly of wearing apparel and household requisites. A refreshment stall was also provided, and this had been generously contributed to. The arrangements were admirably carried out by a committee of ladies and gentlemen associated with the church and school. At night, the Wycliffe Orchestral Band. under the conductorship of Mr. William Brown,dis- coursed music at short intervals, end a c )ncert given in the upper schoolroom was well-attended. The proceeds realised £3O, which was considered very satisfact, ry. CYCLING and TOURING ROAR MAPS.--Joint S GRKEN, Stationer, &amp;a., 31, Bridge-street. Warrington, TENNIS.—Rtmcom v. WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>This match was played at Runcorn on Saturday, and resulted in a victory for the visitors by one event and six games, viz., six events, 14 setts, 147 games, to five events, 14 setts' 141 games. Detail : —Ladies' Doubles : Miss Mainwaring and Miss Birtles (W) lost to Miss Wiswall and Miss Warner, 1-21 to 2-24. Miss R Mainwaring and Miss C Gill (W) lost to Misses Timmins, 0-3 to 2-12. Men's Doubles ; Messrs Hannah and C Bond (W) lost to Captain G F Ashton and A Timmins, 0-11 to 2-15. Messrs .1 C Robinson and T J Jackson (W) beat Messrs J E Meredith and Andrrson,2 12 to 0-3. Mixed Doubles: Mr Elannah and Miss Mainwaring (W) lost to Captain Ashton and Miss Wiswall, 2-13 to 0-6. Mr C Bond and Miss 11 Mainwaring (W) . beat Mr A Timmins and Miss P Timmins, 2-15 to 1-14. Mr J C Robinson and Miss Gill (W) beat Mr Ander- son and Miss F Timmins, 2 1.7 to 13. Mr H Gaudy and Miss D Birtles W) lost to Mr J E Meredith and Miss Warner, 2-14 to 8. Men's Singles : Mr C Bond (W) v. Mr A Timmins. a draw, 11 games to 12 in favour of Runcorn. Mr FJJackson (W) beat Mr Anderson, 2-12 to 3. Mr JO Robinson (W) beat Mr J E Meredith, 2-16 to 1-9. Mr H Gandy- (W) beat Dr McLennan, 2-15 to 1-9.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES,</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES,</p>
        <p>CHURCH OF THE SACRED HEART.</p>
        <p>In connection with this church the annual school ser- mons were preached on Sunday morning and evening, by the Rev. Father Eardweli, S.J., of Liverpool. Righ Mass was celebrated in the morning by the Rev. Father Ryan. The musical portion of the service consisted of Father Turner's Mass " Mary Magdalene." Mrs. F. Liston sang" Ave Maria "as an offertory piece in an admirabls manner. In the evening, during the Benedic- tion, Rossi's " Tantum Ergo "was rendered by Panics. J. Gibbins (bass) and G. Newton (tenor). Miss Liston sang Zingarelli's " E3CA Pani4." M. J. Gi btions was he conductor and MiA3 M. Balliaa presided at the ran.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The attendance at the moetiag of this society on Sun- day was moderately good. Mr. Daviour presided, the subject of his brief address being " Idol worship." Mr. Bent, ex-president of the Y.M.C.A. Society was the speaker and delivered a very practical address, hi 3 sub- ject being "To-morrow." lie warned hie hearers a the evils of procrastination. The aolsist was Mr.G. Gough, who sang very effectively "Dawning Light." Mr. Efealoy [ and Mr. W. Beowick, Ina., took part in the oervicc,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-06-17_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>LATEST NEWS.</head>
        <p>LATEST NEWS.</p>
        <p>GREAT FIRE IN LONDON,</p>
        <p>Shortly after one o'clock yesterday morning a great fire broke out in Heddon-street, Regent-street, I,ndoei in the neighbaurhood or Piccadilly Circus. A. large block of five-storeyed warehouses, Nos. 17, 19, and 21, were involved, and the fire burnt with the utmost fury, leav• lug in an incredibly short time little but the bare Wall standing. The building was in the occupation of several tenants, Messrs. Reverie, bookbinders, utilising We floors ; Messrs. Kutten, woollen merchant, another; and the top floor, where the dames were first noticed, was used by a firm of telegraph instrument makers- Above the roof, which early gave way, a heavy iron sup- port for some 200 telephone wires was erected, and dur- ing the progress of the fire the stanchions and loose wires hung perilously over the street. Many distill- guished medical men and others resident in the mansionS in Saville Row, spent a troubled night, the fire hose being unceremoniously dragged through their halls that the firemen might cape with the outbreak from the rear. INSURRECTION IN YEMEN SPREADING. The Constantinople correspondent of the 't Times states that a telegrarn received from Saner reports that the blockade of that town has been raised, but the 10' surrection in Yemen is spreading, led by one Hatuirli who calls himself the true Khalif. The old stoty of 187 Ci is being repeated. Reinforcements of 7.000 men are to be despatched. The Sultan insists that Abdullah Pacbs shall take the comman.l in Yemen. CHRISTLIN VILLAGES DESTROYED Br</p>
        <p>ALBANIANS. M. Bakitch, Minister of Montfmegro in Constantin9ple has made earnest representations to the Porte regardinig the situation on the Turco-Montenegrin frontier. Six Christian vililages in the district of &amp;wane onths Turkish side of the border were, the Minister says, st' tacked and burned by Albanians.</p>
        <p>THE WAR. NEWS FROM MADRID. MADRID THURSDAY. In the Chamber yesterday in reply to a question, the Minister of War stated that as Santiago cle Cuba was not in communication with Guantanatno,it was not sur- prising that the Government could , not say whether it was true that the Americans had landed. If thoy had there was no reason for concealing the fact, and as soon as the Government received news they would publish it. General Aytnar remarked that the reports relating be the Americans landing at Guantanamo were very favourable to the Spaniards. The Chamber adopted a proposal sub- mitted by republican members to the effect that the Government should bring forward a Bill on obligatory military service before the end of the session. The Minister of Foreien Affairs has instructed Lieut. Carranza and Senor Du 113 SC t 3 leave Canada, and there' by put an end to the representations occasioned by their stay in that country. Marshal Blanco has) been advised to exchange the Merrimac prisoners. RUNNING THE PORTO RICO BLOCKADE. CORUNNA, THURSDAY. The Spanish vessel Miguel Pinelles arrived here to-daY from Porto Eico,having successfully run the blockade of the American vessels. Her captain reports that his vessel was iu the port of San Juan at the time of its bombard- ment by the United States warships. Sne was struck by a shot which made a hole in her side. The hole, however, was above the water line and the damage wag easily repaired. DYNAMITE IN WARFARE. An Exchange Company's telegram from New York states that the Vesuvius took part in the naval engage' ment off Santiago on Monday, and for the first time ie naval warfare dynamite shells impelled by pneumatic power were ueed. Tile three shells thrown are said to have taken terrible effect.</p>
        <p>THE UNITED STATES WAR LOAN. NEW YORK, TEEERsDAY. The subscriptions for the war loan of two hundred million dollars continue to pour in, and it is estimated that the entire subscriptions will amount to a Ulla" dollars.</p>
        <p>ATLANTIC LINER IN COLLISION. NEW YORK, THURSDAY. The North German Lloyd liner Ems has arrived lealv leg, with her bows badly damaged, after a collision off Nantucket during ato last night. She collided with $ schooner which sank,and five of her crew were drowned The bows of the Ems were pierced in several places. SERVIAN HOUSES BURNED BY TFL TURKS.</p>
        <p>CETTINGE, THURSDAY. Intelligence has rea^hed here that up to yesterday th: Turks bad burned 450 Snvian houses in the district O' Borani. No fresh incident of importance occurred yer terday. THE WHEAT CORNER.</p>
        <p>CHICAGO, Tutx It is understood that Mr. Armour and his supporte" are the underwriters for Mr. Leiter's wheat loans. The consideration is a share in the profits which ma', bly be made. It is now estimated that over 12.000,000 bushels of wheat have been transferred. The taking et this load off the market is thought to vivo such a chance of recovery as may possibly turn Mr. Loiter' loss" into a small profit. M. E. 0 iijon will liqui late Mr. -Leiter's European contracts. Mr. Geddes controls the British trade. Mr. Joseph Leiter is so pleased with what has been done that be bones to make a similar arrange' ment for the remnant of his contracts in " futures." .A DESTRUCTIVE FIRE AT PORTSMOUTiI.</p>
        <p>Damage estimated at several thousand pounds via, done by fire on Wednesday at Messrs. Sadler and C0."14 ehmical work+, Stamshaw, Portsmouth.</p>
      </div>
